-- The object comments of the schema, carried into every database this image
-- migrates, in neutral wording. The baseline is a scrubbed pg_dump and drops
-- every COMMENT ON, so a database started from it had no descriptions at all,
-- while a database migrated file by file held the earlier wording of each:
-- the two shapes disagreed on 740 descriptions that tests and readers rely on.
--
-- Each statement is a plain description of what its object is and how it is
-- read and written: no ticket numbers, no file paths or code names of the
-- programs that use the schema, no environment variable names. The set of
-- objects is every comment on an object of ours (extension members,
-- partitions and built-in types left out) that exists in a database migrated
-- to 0302. On a database started from the baseline every statement adds a
-- description; on one migrated file by file it replaces the earlier wording
-- with this one. Nothing else changes.
--
-- COMMENT ON takes a lock on each of the ~200 relations it names; the bound
-- below makes the file give up rather than queue behind a long transaction
-- (migrate retries a 55P03).
--
-- rollback: irreversible in intent (the comments are documentation; removing
--   them restores the baseline's gap, and the earlier wording is not kept).
--   To forget the ledger row:
--   delete from schema_migrations where version = 303;

set local lock_timeout = '5s';
COMMENT ON TABLE control.budgets IS 'One row per budget key. licence_*, window_s, burst_window_s, hold_s: the provider''s terms, written only by publish_terms(). ceiling, burst_max, spacing_ms, reserve_pct: the values in force, written only by set_budget(); a CHECK holds each against its licence column (never more, never faster).';
COMMENT ON TABLE control.budget_state IS 'The hot row per budget key and THE lock: every admission takes it FOR UPDATE until commit, so every process is serialised on the key. Carries the spacing, the person stamp and the hold. Written only by admit, release, hold and want.';
COMMENT ON TABLE control.callers IS 'Who may ask. process: which entry point may name the caller. person: a person waits for the answer — it stamps the key''s person_wanted_at, and a caller that is not a person yields to it. Published from the workers'' declaration of provider terms; never deleted.';
COMMENT ON TABLE control.changes IS 'The database''s own record of every change to control.callers, budgets and shares, whoever made it: the actor (viafrei.actor, else current_user) and the reason (viafrei.reason, required). Append-only. ops_audit keeps the console ACTION; this keeps the VALUE.';
COMMENT ON TABLE control.feed_state IS 'One row per ledger key: the newest attempt, success, change and budget refusal, maintained by the feed_runs and ingest_runs triggers. refused_since: the first budget-held run after the last run that asked the provider; NULL = not paused by the budget.';
COMMENT ON TABLE control.providers IS 'Limiter id (the worker''s provider limiter) → budget key, with the in-process pacer''s spacing and concurrency. Several providers may share one key (the three Mobilithek limiters → mobilithek_account). Published.';
COMMENT ON TABLE control.refusals IS 'Every attempt the gate refused, with the budget shape it hit and about how long until there is room. budget_key is the provider id itself when the provider has no budget (no-budget). Kept 14 days.';
COMMENT ON TABLE control.shares IS 'Each caller''s share of a budget key: a floor no other caller may spend, and a cap. A caller with no row is refused (no-share). Seeded once from the declaration; the database owns the value after that. The deferred constraint trigger shares_shape holds at commit: max(cap) <= ceiling, sum(floor) <= ceiling, and on a key of two or more callers with a ceiling above 0 (0285) every share switched on (cap > 0) a floor of at least 1 (no-floor) and every cap at most the ceiling less the other callers'' floors (cap-takes-floors). A cap of 0 is a door switched off.';
COMMENT ON TABLE control.usage IS 'The gate''s count: admitted requests per key, caller and bucket (bucket_width seconds). The count in force is admitted - not_sent over the buckets overlapping the window. Kept 48 h; rollup_refresh deletes older buckets.';
COMMENT ON TABLE control.usage_hourly IS 'The admission rollup: per UTC hour, budget key and caller, the admitted, not-sent and refused counts (refused_unconfigured = no-budget + no-share), with the ceiling, cap and floor in force when it was computed so a chart of use against share stays honest after a change. Upserted for the last 2 hours by rollup_refresh(); kept 90 days.';
COMMENT ON TABLE ingestor.run_errors IS 'Per-row rejects and per-run failures, at most 50 per run, each sample cut to 2 KB. Pruned after 14 days.';
COMMENT ON TABLE ingestor.sources IS 'One row per DEPLOYED source config (status enabled or disabled; a draft is never registered). No licence, no row: licence_id and attribution are NOT NULL, so an unlicensed source cannot register. registered_by is the image tag and git sha that carried the config.';
COMMENT ON TABLE ingestor.units IS 'One row per run unit (units/*.yaml, or the implicit one-source cron unit). Written at each pod start; last_deployed_at moves when config_sha256 or the image moves.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.ags_regions IS 'The AGS (Amtlicher Gemeindeschlüssel) region map: region name or point in, an AGS PREFIX out, so a tool can select the rows of any dataset filed under that key — DELFI DHID stop ids (de:06435:… = 06435), DWD warnings per Gemeindeschlüssel, StaDa. Two levels only: Land (2 digits) and Kreis (5 digits); a Gemeinde question is rolled up to its Kreis and the answer says so, because DELFI codes its stops at Kreis level and an 8-digit filter would silently drop most of a town. The 16 Land rows are seeded by migration 0025 from the Statistisches Bundesamt key list (amtliches Werk, § 5 UrhG); the Kreis rows are refreshed from gazetteer_places on every gazetteer load. Derived, rebuilt whole, never soft-deleted.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.autobahn_event_texts IS 'One row per distinct (road, title, subtitle) of `autobahn_events`, with the sighting pair and the point of the NEWEST event that named it — the gazetteer''s junction source, folded once instead of re-derived from the archive on every load. It exists because that fold cost 11 389 805 rows to produce 10 131 answers and was cancelled at the 120 000 ms statement cap on every deploy, and because the cost of the old statement grew with the tick table while this one grows with how many different things the provider says. Maintained by the tick insert in the SAME STATEMENT that stores the ticks, so it cannot fall behind the archive; re-derivable at any time by `autobahn_event_texts_backfill()`, which is also what the read path names when it refuses a stale rollup. A row says "this text was published", never "this road exists" — that is `autobahn_roads`.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.autobahn_jam_hours IS 'Hourly jam rollup of autobahn_events: one row per road and UTC hour the road was swept, the jammed (title, subtitle) pairs of that hour. Absent hour = not observed. Kept 57 days by the retention job''s age family. Refreshed by autobahn_jam_rollup_refresh() from the periodic rollup job.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.autobahn_roads IS 'The motorway numbers we know EXIST, one row each, from TWO independent witnesses: the road list the Autobahn GmbH API publishes (written once per COMPLETE sweep, and stamped in `last_listed_at`) and our own `autobahn_events` archive (a road that produced an event exists, whatever today''s list says — written by every sweep, gate-free, and by migration 0042 for the history). Two witnesses because one was not enough: the published list is volatile day to day — A25 appeared in 19 of 236 snapshots on 2026-09-25 — so a quiet, unlisted motorway stayed absent from this register and was answered "not a road I know". It exists to separate what `autobahn_events` cannot tell apart, because that table only gets a row when something is HAPPENING on a road: a motorway that is CLEAR (a row here, nothing there — "nichts gemeldet", the good answer) from a road number nobody has ever published or ticked (no row here) from ingestion that has stopped (`ingest_runs`, plus a stale `max(last_listed_at)` here). It is also what makes the empty answer cheap: ~120 rows on one heap page instead of a day of the tick archive scanned with `select distinct road`. Never a source of event data, and never joined to a partition.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.bfrk_stops IS 'Step-free stop surveys and inventories, one summary row per stop or station, attribution-only licences (DL-DE BY 2.0, CC BY 4.0): the NVBW''s BFRK survey of Baden-Württemberg (bfrk_spnv, bfrk_oepnv), the Landeshauptstadt Dresden''s platform layer (dresden_bf_haltestellen), the Stadtwerke Münster''s step-free bus stops (muenster_bf_haltestellen) and the VBB''s station entrances and lifts (vbb_haltestellenzugaenge, CC BY 4.0). Platforms, boarding classes, lifts, ramps, stairs, escalators, entrances, or a publisher''s stop-level statement. Never photos, OSM ids or per-element records; nothing share-alike. validated = the publisher''s data status where it states one, NULL where it states none. Closed, never deleted.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.bw_detours IS 'Signed motorway detours of Baden-Württemberg (Bedarfsumleitungen), Verkehrsministerium BW via MobiData BW, DL-DE Zero 2.0. One row per sheet row per published version (the date in the file name); a newer version closes the older rows, nothing is deleted. Context for a motorway closure, never a route recommendation.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.charging_points IS 'One row per AFIR charging point (DATEX II `refillPoint` → `aegiElectricChargingPoint`) per source feed. Master data only: whether it is free RIGHT NOW comes from the dynamic deltaPull feeds and their own tick table, deliberately not here — a status column on a daily snapshot would be wrong within a minute of being written.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.charging_point_status IS 'Append-only availability history of AFIR charging points, day-partitioned on observed_at. MISS-IS-LOSS: afir_tesla_dyn is a deltaPull feed, so a gap in feed_runs means the state in that gap is UNKNOWN, never "unchanged". CHANGE-ONLY: a delta carries only changes, so every row is a change; the loader suppresses nothing except the same EVSE whose newest row carries the same last_updated AND the same status, operation_status and opening_status — the clock alone is not the test, because a status that moves under an unchanged lastUpdated is a change this feed offers exactly once.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.charging_sites IS 'AFIR charging locations from the static Mobilithek feeds, one row per (source_feed, provider site id). The two feeds are NEVER merged: Eco-Movement aggregates operators that also publish directly, and a merged row would mix two licences and two attribution strings (the source register''s aggregation rule). Rows are never deleted; a site that stops appearing gets closed_at.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.charging_status_runs IS 'One row per (observed_at, feed, cycle, delta-or-not) of the PUBLISHABLE charging_point_status ticks: the newest stated instant and the tick count, written by the charging_status_runs_note trigger. The charging liveness read takes its moved/received/restated facts from here instead of the ticks. Kept 35 days.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.city_traffic_pulls IS 'The newest pull of each city traffic-state feed: the freshness clock of every answer. A reading is fresh while measured_at is younger than the read rule''s limit.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.city_traffic_segments IS 'The street segments of the city traffic-state feeds: every segment a feed ever listed, its geometry once, never deleted. A segment with no current reading is simply not in city_traffic_now_live.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.city_traffic_state IS 'The present state of every city traffic-state segment: the status the newest pull gave it, or NULL when that pull did not list it. Every change is in city_traffic_ticks.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.city_traffic_ticks IS 'Append-only, day-partitioned, CHANGE-ONLY history of the city traffic-state feeds: a row only when a segment''s status moved, NULL when it left the listing. Nothing is lost because every change is kept; city_traffic_state carries the present.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.connect_transit_delays IS 'VBN Connect GTFS-RT trip updates (CC BY 4.0, register mobilithek_connect_gtfs_rt, Mobilithek offer 761221412930105344): append-only, day-partitioned, change-only. Its own table so CC BY rows never sit in the CC BY-SA transit_delays; the CHECK admits this register key only. Trip and stop ids are Connect''s own and resolve against gtfs_feed_key 2 only.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.db_facilities IS 'One row per lift or escalator DB InfraGO AG reports through the FaSta API, refreshed by a nationwide sweep of GET /fasta/v2/facilities. A SNAPSHOT of the publisher''s present, not history: db_facility_ticks holds the changes. CC BY 4.0; source_feed is check-constrained to the register key db_fasta so every row can state its licence. Carries no station NAME on purpose — the payload publishes none, and the crosswalk that has one (db_station_numbers) is loaded separately and may be empty.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.db_facility_ticks IS 'Append-only, day-partitioned, CHANGE-ONLY history of FaSta facility states. A sweep that finds nothing changed writes nothing: 3 752 facilities x 288 sweeps a day would be 1.08 M rows to record roughly 300 real changes, and the register''s delfi_rt_tu row sets the change-only precedent. Nothing is lost because every CHANGE is kept for ever; the snapshot db_facilities carries the present.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.db_station_boards IS 'DB Timetables departure boards of the DB category 1-2 stations, one row per station, rewritten every 10 minutes by the engine job db_station_boards (host ingest-db-fasta). CC BY 4.0, register key db_timetables. Read by the station page and describe_station; a row older than 20 minutes or degraded speaks for nothing.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.db_station_numbers IS 'EVA number ↔ StaDa station number, written from one StaDa /stations record per station. It exists because Deutsche Bahn keys its own APIs two different ways for the same building: Timetables takes the EVA number (8002549 = Hamburg Hbf) and StaDa/FaSta take the station number (1866 = the same station). Both are digits and a wrong-family id returns 404, so without an authoritative crosswalk a facility answer is a confident statement about the wrong station. Loaded by the gazetteer load alongside the gazetteer''s db_stada rows; empty until that pass has run, and an empty row set is reported as "cannot look this station up" and never as "no lift reported here".';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.db_stations IS 'The station index: one row per railway station with a page. source_id db_stada: a DB StaDa station (CC BY 4.0), keyed by the Bahnhofsnummer, written by the gazetteer load from the saved /stations file. source_id delfi_static: a rail-served zHV station level StaDa does not list, keyed by its DHID, derived from the DELFI timetable after every GTFS roll (name, point and Gemeinde from the zHV). updated_at moves only when content_hash changes; each writer closes only its own rows.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.delfi_service_alerts IS 'DELFI service alerts (CC BY-SA 4.0; GTFS-RT Service Alerts offer 747118037921857536 and SIRI SX offer 836244707009785856): the alerts in force, its own share-alike table (licence boundary delfi_alerts). closed_at is written when an alert is absent from a FULL GTFS-RT dataset, never on a 204. GTFS-RT is the primary: where both feeds carry the same message (same dedupe_key, or same message_ref) the SIRI SX row is not shown.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.delfi_siri_et_calls IS 'DELFI-Realtime SIRI ET Bahndaten (CC BY-SA, offer 832259263033151488): change-only call history, its own share-alike table (licence boundary delfi_siri_et), never joined to another licence. A call is written when it first is first seen and again only when its delay moved by the registry epsilon, its platform changed, or it was cancelled or recorded — so the newest row per (service_day, journey_ref, stop_ref, visit) IS the current state, and absence of a newer row means unchanged.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.delfi_siri_et_calls_latest IS 'The newest delfi_siri_et_calls tick with an aimed departure per (stop_ref, service_day, journey_ref, visit, source_feed), written by the SIRI ET loader with the ticks; rows older than eight hours removed per feed, those of any feed after 24 hours. CC BY-SA, boundary delfi_siri_et. A projection, not history: the ticks stay in delfi_siri_et_calls.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.delfi_siri_et_stop_pairs IS 'The EVA <-> DHID bridge for the DELFI SIRI ET calls: a SIRI ET stop reference paired with the DELFI GTFS stop where a matched trip calls at the same aimed minute. Adapted Material of delfi_siri_et (CC BY-SA, unversioned) and, one way, of delfi_static (CC BY 4.0): inside the delfi_siri_et share-alike boundary, and part of that boundary''s share-alike export. Current pairs only; a pair not re-confirmed for 30 days is deleted. Nothing outside the boundary is keyed on it.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.deployments IS 'One row per deploy run: which release, which commit, when, how long, and what the database head was afterwards. Read by the status consumer to answer "what is deployed" and, together with worker_boots, "did every process actually come back with it". Append-only in practice: the deploy inserts, nothing updates, nothing deletes. Deliberately holds NO deployer identity and NO host address — the page that renders it is not allowed to leak a person.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.energy_page_key_heads IS 'The generation of energy_page_keys the sitemap readers take, per kind. Written by the engine job energy_page_keys; flipped in the transaction that writes the generation.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.energy_page_keys IS 'One row per fuel station page and per charging site page, in page-key order, per generation. Derived from fuel_stations_live and charging_sites_live by the engine job energy_page_keys; read by the digest''s sitemap parts by ordinal range.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.energy_page_slug_history IS 'A slug a fuel station or charging site page carried before: the row it belonged to, so the old address answers 301 to the current slug. Written by the engine job energy_page_keys.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.energy_page_slugs IS 'The canonical slug of every fuel station and charging site page: the description alone, or with the shortest distinguishing key where descriptions collide. Written by the engine job energy_page_keys; read by the digest (lookup, links) and the MCP server (links).';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.error_events IS 'Cleaned warn/error lines of our services, one row per line hash and hour, kept 30 days. Written by the host''s log collector; read by the operator console through viafrei_ops only; excluded from backups.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.feed_catalog IS 'One row per feed or provider we ingest: who publishes it, under which licence, with which attribution string, at which cadence, and whether it is switched on. Written at WORKER BOOT from the feed registry — the Mobilithek worker publishes its own feeds, the Autobahn and Tankerkoenig workers their single provider row — so a reader with database access can attribute a feed_runs or ingest_runs row without reading the workers'' code. The authority for every licence and attribution string is the source register; this table is a mechanically written copy of it. Upsert by feed_key, never a delete: a feed that leaves the registry gets closed_at set, because ledger rows still point at its key.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.feed_run_extents IS 'Per feed, the charging liveness read''s 48-hour extent (max items_seen of its ok/unchanged/degraded runs) as of its newest such run''s finish, with the feed''s version then and the start of the run holding the maximum. Read only while the version is current, computed_at is not after the read''s instant and that run is still inside the read''s 48 hours.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.feed_runs IS 'One row per Mobilithek poll cycle per feed, including the cycles that deliberately did nothing. NOT related to `feeds`, which versions static GTFS datasets.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.feed_versions IS 'A data version per run-ledger key, incremented by every run that finishes ok or degraded. The digest keys its caches on the versions of the feeds a read draws from.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.fuel_city_seed IS 'One row per city of the fixed fuel seed list that has been asked. The list itself is fixed in the worker''s code and never a table, so nothing outside it can be seeded. A city is asked again only when it holds fewer than the per-city target of stations seen in the last 30 days within 5 km AND its last successful ask is older than 30 days AND its last attempt is older than a day. source_run_id is the tankerkoenig:discover ingest_runs row of the ask.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.fuel_sample_draws IS 'One row per draw of the stratified national fuel sample. It exists so that a published national figure can name the exact stations behind it: `rng_seed` + `design_version` + the frame reproduce the draw bit for bit. `capacity` is DERIVED from the poller''s cadence by the poller''s capacity rule and never written by hand — the seed script, this table and the scheduler read one arithmetic. It holds no price and no coordinate.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.fuel_sample_strata IS 'The design of one draw, per stratum: what we hold, what the allocator asked for, what was achieved, and how the achieved split into the fixed core and the rotating remainder. Two readers depend on it and neither can be answered by counting watch rows: the sample check fails naming a stratum whose achieved is below its target (a gate that judges the SAMPLE against its DESIGN rather than counting stations per Land), and the margin of error is Σ W_h² · s_h²/n_h · (1 − n_h/N_h) with W_h and N_h taken from `held`. An empty stratum is a row here with held = 0, never an absent row — an absence reads as a clean country.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.gazetteer_places IS 'The nationwide gazetteer: one row per known place, station, stop, motorway or junction, from files and from our own autobahn_events — never from a hosted geocoder. Free text in, a point (or a road number) out, so no MCP tool has to demand lat/lon from a person. Rows are never deleted; one that stops being published gets closed_at.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.gbfs_stations IS 'Docking stations of admitted GBFS systems, CC0-1.0 as each system states it. Diff-and-close: a station a full station_information no longer lists gets closed_at, and every station of a system the licence gate refuses is closed on that cycle. Never deleted.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.gbfs_station_status IS 'Append-only, day-partitioned, CHANGE-ONLY history of GBFS station counts: a row only when a station''s available vehicles, electric vehicles, free docks or one of its three flags moved. Nothing is lost because every change is kept; the newest row per station is the present. Counts of vehicles at a fixed station — not positions of people — so history is kept.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.gbfs_systems IS 'GBFS systems we read, one row per system, upserted on every refresh: the licence each stated at the last read and the gate''s verdict. A refused system keeps its row — the refusal is the record — and loses its stations (closed) and vehicles (removed) on the same cycle.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.gbfs_vehicles IS 'Free-floating GBFS vehicles of the CURRENT refresh only. PRIVACY: no vehicle id is stored and no history is kept — each refresh of a system replaces its rows, because a series of anonymous positions can reconstruct a trip. No history of these positions may be kept without a separate licence and privacy decision. Vehicles docked at a station are not stored here; the station''s count carries them.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.growth_weekly IS 'The growth funnel: per ISO week (Berlin), Search Console impressions/clicks/position, people/own/crawler/automation page views, home and connect views, external MCP initializes, tool calls, errors and distinct clients, own tool calls. Counts only. Recomputed by growth_weekly_refresh(); two years kept.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.gtfs_service_dates IS 'GTFS calendar.txt + calendar_dates.txt expanded to one row per (service, date), restricted to the rolling 48-hour window the loader keeps. The window IS this table: min/max(service_date) is what the database holds. REPLACED WHOLESALE per gtfs_feed_key on every load: a service the new release dropped must leave no date behind.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.gtfs_stale_trips IS 'Trips a GTFS load no longer carries: removed from trips by the load that found them stale (retired_run_id, the ingest_runs.id of that load), their stop_times still to be deleted. Drained in bounded batches by the GTFS loader (inside the load as far as its ceiling reaches, then a catch-up prune on every roll tick). A trip id present in trips again is skipped and dequeued: it belongs to the load that wrote it. Empty when the prune has caught up; the deploy check reports its size.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.hh_cameras IS 'Hamburg traffic-camera LOCATIONS from the published internet selection, DL-DE BY 2.0 (BVM): one row per camera, diffed daily and closed from absence, never deleted. Metadata only — no image is ever fetched or stored, and there is no binary or URL column; answers link to the Verkehrsportal and carry the publisher''s caveat that images are not reliably available around the clock.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.hh_roadworks IS 'Hamburg city roadworks from Bauweiser, DL-DE BY 2.0 (BVM): one row per site the WFS de.hh.up:baustelle lists, diffed hourly and closed from absence, never deleted. Only `released` rows are ever answered. Free text is stored REDACTED (no e-mail address, no telephone number) and only allow-listed fields are stored.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.hh_roadwork_versions IS 'Append-only history of every distinct content of a Bauweiser site: a row when the content hash moves, never updated, kept for ever. Not day-partitioned on purpose, so no partition archive ever drops it.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.hydrogen_stations IS 'Hydrogen filling stations: one row per published site with at least one refill point, diff-and-close, never deleted. No publisher comment and no photo is stored (separate works).';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.lez_zone_overlaps IS 'Derived: every ordered pair of open lez_zones zone rows of two different feeds whose interiors meet, with the area they share and the smaller one''s area (st_area of the 4326 geometry). Written by triggers on lez_zones (lez_zone_overlaps_refresh) in the writer''s transaction; the zone read applies its same-zone share threshold to the two numbers. Recomputable at any time, never history.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.lez_zones IS 'Low-emission-zone polygons from the cities'' own open documents, with the roads the zone does not apply to and the areas released inside it; one row per (feed, name), diffed per pull and closed from absence, never deleted. The plaque a zone requires is curated, not here.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.lez_zone_versions IS 'Append-only history of every distinct content of a lez_zones row: a row when the content hash moves, never updated, kept for ever.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.lorry_city IS 'Lorry networks, restrictions and zones a city publishes on its own geometry: Stadt Köln''s LKW Verbindungen and Strassenbeschränkungen, DL-DE Zero 2.0; Landeshauptstadt Dresden''s Lkw-Verbotszonen and Lkw-Vorrangroutennetz and Hamburg''s Großraum- und Schwertransport-Netz, DL-DE BY 2.0. One row per feature per weekly read, keyed by a hash of the publisher''s attributes and geometry; nothing deleted. A zone row is a drawing of its release date, never the ban in force; a restriction drawn over many sections shares its group_id; a heavy_transport section is a network to apply along, never a permit.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.lorry_hb IS 'VMZ Bremen (ASV Bremen, DL-DE BY 2.0 under the ASV Lizenzbedingungen 2015-11-23): the clearance heights and weight limits of the VMZ lorry pages, the publisher''s words kept verbatim in published_text and never parsed into value. Consumer answers only. One row per feature per weekly read; nothing deleted.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.lorry_ivm IS 'ivm Region Frankfurt RheinMain (DL-DE BY 2.0): the lorry priority network by category (lines) and the lorry-relevant sign locations (points), referenced on OpenStreetMap. One row per feature per release; nothing deleted.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.lorry_long_network IS 'The long-lorry (Lang-Lkw) positive network: BASt''s extract of the Bundesfernstraßen ASB sectors (CC BY 4.0, national; not legally binding, not for navigation — the publisher''s notice travels with every answer) and BVM Hamburg''s 2015 field-trial network (DL-DE BY 2.0). One row per feature per release, keyed by a hash of the publisher''s words; nothing deleted.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.lorry_sevas IS 'NRW.Mobidrom SEVAS (DL-DE Zero 2.0): lorry restriction segments and lorry priority-route segments maintained by the NRW municipalities, referenced on OpenStreetMap. One row per feature per weekly read; nothing deleted.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.los_lane_sites IS 'Autobahn GmbH LoS lanes in NRW, GeoNutzV: one row per lane predefinedLocation, diff-and-close, never deleted. site_id is ours, read from the lane id.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.los_lane_state IS 'Autobahn GmbH LoS per lane, GeoNutzV: the present state of every lane the newest pull reported; a lane absent from later pulls ages out by last_seen_at, never read as freeFlow.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.los_sites IS 'Autobahn GmbH LoS measuring sites in NRW, GeoNutzV: one row per predefinedLocation, diff-and-close, never deleted. road is ours, from the BASt TMC chain (CC BY 4.0), not the publisher''s.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.los_state IS 'Autobahn GmbH LoS, GeoNutzV: the present state of every site the newest pull reported. A site absent from later pulls keeps its row with an ageing last_seen_at, and the read path calls it no reading past the freshness window — never its last state, and never the publication''s freeFlow default.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.los_ticks IS 'Append-only, day-partitioned, CHANGE-ONLY history of the Autobahn GmbH LoS in NRW: a row only when a site''s status moved. Nothing is lost because every change is kept; los_state carries the present.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.mcp_usage IS 'MCP usage ledger: counts per hour/day, door, JSON-RPC method, tool and normalised client name. No IP, session id, arguments, text, place or user agent.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.mcp_usage_exports IS 'Export ledger of mcp_usage: one row per destination and UTC day a daily aggregate was sent. Counts only; never the API secret.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.motorway_summary IS 'One row per motorway: check_road_status {road} and check_autobahn_traffic computed once per Autobahn GmbH sweep, rewritten whole in one transaction. Derived, not history.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.mvv_bus_load IS 'MVV regional-bus occupancy profiles (opendata-oepnv.de, CC BY, no version stated; register id mvv_bus_load): the highest load class per day type, stop DHID, line, direction and scheduled minute, from the publisher''s passenger counts. Typical, never live. Closed, never deleted.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.oom_kills IS 'OOM kills read off the host kernel log by the host''s OOM collector: when, which of OUR containers (by name), the process, anon-rss and the container limit. Another tenant''s kill is kept only when host-wide, and then anonymously. Idempotent on line_sha256.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.opendata_roadworks IS 'Roadworks, permitted street events and traffic messages from open city and Land portals, one row per (feed, site), diffed per pull and closed from absence, never deleted. Free text stored REDACTED (no e-mail address, no telephone number), only allow-listed fields. Only answerable, traffic-relevant, open rows are ever read (view opendata_roadworks_live).';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.opendata_roadwork_versions IS 'Append-only history of every distinct content of an open-data road row: a row when the content hash moves, never updated, kept for ever.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.ops_passkeys IS 'The operator console''s WebAuthn credentials: at most ten, counter never decreases, written only through viafrei_ops. Public keys, not secrets.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.ops_rollup_feed IS 'Run rollups: one row per 5-minute bucket per ledger key that ran. Written by ops_rollup_refresh() every 5 min from feed_runs/ingest_runs; one LIST partition per UTC day, dropped after 90 days. The status pages read this, never the ledgers.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.osm_addresses IS 'The OSM address gazetteer, under ODbL 1.0 and in its OWN tables. Street-level input for the geo tools: free text in, a point out. Share-alike is a term of the DATABASE, so these rows are never joined to a table of another licence and no view unions them — a join would pull the ODbL obligation onto whatever it touched. A result an OSM row answered — this table or its sibling osm_pois — carries "OSM-Standortdaten: © OpenStreetMap-Mitwirkende, ODbL 1.0"; a result no OSM row answered carries neither that line nor ODbL in _meta.license.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.osm_boundary_de IS 'Germany''s national boundary from OpenStreetMap (ODbL 1.0, share-alike database): the sixteen Land relations (admin_level=4), assembled from their member ways and cut by st_subdivide into pieces of at most 256 vertices; their union is the territory. Read to confine the unpriced OSM fuel list and the OSM gap test to Germany, and by the digest to judge a visitor''s position (inside, near the border, far) and which motorway points stand within 2 km of the national boundary line (the border crossings) — each a statement over this table alone, the points passed as literals. Empty means no OSM fuel row counts as German and no position is judged by it (fail closed). Rebuilt from the staged extracts by the boundary install step; offered in the OpenStreetMap share-alike export; nothing joins it to a table of another licence.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.osm_pois IS 'Named points of interest from OpenStreetMap — offices, shops, amenities, clinics, hotels, leisure and industrial sites. ODbL 1.0, share-alike: this table and osm_addresses are the ODbL side of the boundary and nothing joins either to a table carrying another licence. A lexical check over the workers'' statements enforces it.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.osm_replication_state IS 'Where each Bundesland''s geocoding stands in OpenStreetMap''s replication stream: the sequence number the rows we hold were built from, the instant that sequence describes, and when this host applied it. One row per Geofabrik slug. It is NOT inside the ODbL share-alike boundary — a sequence number is a fact about Geofabrik''s publication, not content extracted from the OSM database — so it may not be named in one statement beside osm_addresses or osm_pois, and nothing here is an alteration of the OSM database that the § 4.6 offer has to carry.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.osm_speed_cameras IS 'OpenStreetMap fixed speed cameras (ODbL 1.0, a share-alike database of its own): one row per node tagged highway=speed_camera in the per-Land extracts. Read by find_speed_cameras and the place card in a statement of its own, only in a cell no official fixed or section-control row covers, with or without a point (a cell is the area nearest a BKG Gemeinde point, an approximation of the Gemeinde; the regional cut; never matched with an official row), as a block of its own, always with the § 23 Abs. 1c StVO notice. Loaded per Land by its install step; offered whole in its share-alike export; never named in a statement beside another table.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.osm_streets IS 'OpenStreetMap street geometry (ODbL 1.0, a share-alike database of its own): one row per way tagged highway=motorway/trunk/primary/secondary/tertiary/residential (cls 1-6), geom simplified to 3 m and geom_lo to 40 m (motorway to secondary only), EPSG:25832. Drawn by the digest as the streets under the marks of the place and motorway maps, credited on every map that draws one. Loaded per Land by its install step; offered whole in its share-alike export; never named in a statement beside another table.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.parking_facilities IS 'Every parking facility we know of, whatever published it: motorway rest areas, truck parking, city car parks, park-and-ride. The licence is a property of the ROW (attribution_id), not of the table, because parking reaches us under at least four different sets of terms and a single table licence would let the most restrictive source decide what every answer may say. Rows are never deleted: a facility the provider stops publishing gets closed_at, and a row WE retract gets deleted_at.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.parking_facilities_bysa IS 'Parking facilities published under CC BY-SA (NRW.Mobidrom, P+R NRW). A table of its own because CC BY-SA 4.0 § 4(b) makes a database holding a substantial part of the contents Adapted Material: no statement may name it beside a table of another licence, ODbL included. No soft delete; rows are never deleted.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.parking_facilities_odbl IS 'Parking facilities published under ODbL 1.0 (ENMO GmbH & Co.KG, Trucklounge Ecopark). A table of its own because ODbL share-alike attaches to the DATABASE: no statement may name it beside a table of another licence or beside the OpenStreetMap tables, and its complete contents are offered under ODbL by its share-alike export (§ 4.6). No soft delete: a retraction kept here would fork a database we offer back whole.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.parking_facilities_sid IS 'SID static sites the national ITP table does not carry. Gated like parking_occupancy_sid: a row leaves the server only after the person accepted ViaFrei''s terms of use, section 2; read only through parking_facilities_sid_live by the two gated readers. No contact, no raw body, no operator.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.parking_facility_refusals IS 'Per static parking feed, the sites its latest load refused to write (a swapped-back point not within 25 km of the record''s own address town). Read by the status loader so a reading for such a site is withheld and named rather than counted as an unknown id. Rewritten by every load of the feed.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.parking_forecast_profiles IS 'A publisher''s occupancy FORECAST as a profile: facility x day type x time band -> a traffic-light class, versioned by the file''s Stand. Never occupancy: no reader may present a row as a current reading or as a percentage — the source publishes neither.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.parking_occupancy IS 'Append-only occupancy history, one row per facility per pull, day-partitioned on observed_at. No foreign key to parking_facilities on purpose: occupancy is a minute feed and master data is a daily dump, so an id the dump has not caught up with is ORDINARY — the observation is still the fact a driver asked about, and it is stored, counted and visible rather than dropped.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.parking_occupancy_odbl IS 'Append-only occupancy history of the ODbL parking database (ENMO GmbH & Co.KG, Trucklounge Ecopark dynamisch). Part of the Derivative Database the § 4.6 offer covers in FULL, which is why it is deliberately NOT day-partitioned: the partition archive would drop old days out of the database the offer is made from. Joined only to parking_facilities_odbl.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.parking_occupancy_sid IS 'Append-only, day-partitioned truck-parking occupancy of the Toll Collect SID. Gated: a figure leaves the server only after the person accepted ViaFrei''s terms of use, section 2 (a click on the site, an accepted MCP elicitation); read only through parking_occupancy_sid_live by the two gated readers, never by an ungated one.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.parking_sites IS 'SUPERSEDED by parking_facilities. Its rows were copied there, with their first_seen_at/closed_at history, by the migration that created that table, and the loader writes to the new table from that release on. Kept, not dropped: dropping a populated table is irreversible, and the copy has not yet been observed in production. A later migration drops it once a release has run on parking_facilities. Nothing writes here.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.partition_archives IS 'The cold store''s ledger: one row per daily tick partition that has been dumped to the raw volume, and the only record of what that file is. Written BEFORE the partition is detached and dropped, with the row count read from the LIVE partition and the sha256 of the bytes actually written, because a .dump file is not evidence of itself. `dropped_at` is set in the same transaction as the DROP, so a row with a dump and no drop is a verified file whose partition is still queryable — the dry run''s world, and the state a crash between the two steps leaves for the next pass to finish. Nothing here deletes a file: the raw-archive pass acts on `<root>/<provider>/<YYYY-MM-DD>` day directories and `pg` is not a provider, so the two passes share a volume and nothing else.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.partition_settings IS 'One row: the policy ensure_daily_partitions() enforces (0065). min_daily_tables is the FLOOR of range-partitioned tables keyed on observed_at the function must find before it creates anything; it only rises, because a tick table is never dropped. The migration that adds a tick table raises it with one UPDATE. Deleting the row makes the function refuse.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.place_slugs IS 'The readable slug of each Gemeinde''s place page, by AGS. Assigned once by the slug builder and never recomputed: an UPDATE of slug and a DELETE are refused.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.place_website_checks IS 'The last liveness verdict of each Gemeinde website the place_websites load asked: kept between loads so a cut load resumes; an OK verdict younger than 25 days is carried forward, a dead one never; place_websites is replaced only when every URL has a fresh verdict.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.place_websites IS 'The official website of a Gemeinde by AGS: Wikidata P856 (CC0), checked on load (https, bounded, no markup characters, one current item per AGS, and one GET answering 2xx on its own domain), replaced whole by every monthly load.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.provider_key_claims IS 'Key-wide request spacing: one row per provider key, claimed atomically by an UPDATE right before every request made with that key, by whichever process makes it. The predicate last_request_at <= clock_timestamp() - spacing is the guarantee; the in-process limiter stays as well.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.road_context_features IS 'Static road context from open portals: road border crossings. One row per (feed, feature), diffed per pull and closed from absence, never deleted. Only allow-listed fields are stored. Only open, answerable rows are ever read (view road_context_live).';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.road_context_feature_versions IS 'Append-only history of every distinct content of a road-context feature: a row when the content hash moves, never updated, kept for ever.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.road_event_locations IS 'One row per BISSTRA stretch of a road event. `NonOrderedLocationGroupByList` events wrap up to hundreds of stretches (462 events / 15 374 stretches in the 2026-09-18 sample); seq 0 is the primary stretch, also kept in road_events so the single-stretch read needs no join.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.road_event_record_types IS 'Catalogue of DATEX II situationRecord types per feed, with first/last sighting and a running count. Written once per load cycle from the parse summary. Exists so that a record type nobody modelled is recorded rather than dropped, and so that the first pull of a never-seen feed reports its own contents.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.road_events IS 'DATEX II road events (roadworks, closures, police events) from the Mobilithek feeds, one row per situation, latest version. Natural key (source_id, publication_id); change detection compares `version`.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.road_event_ticks IS 'Append-only history of road events: one row per observed version change, day-partitioned on observed_at. Keyed naturally (source_id, publication_id) and NOT by road_events.id, so a rewritten parent cannot orphan history.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.road_event_twins IS 'The twin links: a road event (twin_id) that publishes the same works as another feed''s row (origin_id) under an id that does not match it. Written after a load of either feed; read by the road event reads. The rows themselves are never altered.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.road_weather_forecast IS 'The LATEST DWD SWSMOS run only: one row per road-weather station per valid hour (1 h steps to +167 h), replaced whole per run in one transaction. Every value is a MODEL FORECAST (MOS, and METRo for rc/ts) - never a measurement and never an amtliche Wetterwarnung. Raw runs live in the raw archive (14 days). CC BY 4.0 + GMA declaration.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.road_weather_stations IS 'DWD road-weather station catalogue (swsKatalog.csv, opendata.dwd.de), one row per station, upserted daily; closed_at when the catalogue stops listing a station. The position source of every road-weather answer (position_source). CC BY 4.0 + GMA declaration: "Quelle: Deutscher Wetterdienst. Datenquelle: BMVBS und Straßenbauverwaltungen der Länder".';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.runtime_config IS 'Tunables a worker or server re-reads. Bounds are the licence floors, enforced by the console before writing, by the reader when reading and by runtime_config_bounds here; a row outside them is ignored, never clamped. Precedence for a fuel key: this row > the process''s environment default > the code default.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.site_visits_daily IS 'Cookieless visitor count: per Berlin day, unique visitors (HMAC with a daily key that is never stored) and page views. No IP, hash, key, UA or per-visitor row.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.site_visits_hourly IS 'Cookieless visitor count: per Berlin day and hour ONLY the total visits and page views, never broken down by any dimension.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.speed_enforcement IS 'Speed-enforcement locations: fixed cameras, section control, announced mobile checks and published mobile sites, one row per published location, diff-and-close. Every reader shows the § 23 Abs. 1c StVO notice with them; never a live mobile position, never ordered by distance.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.station_accessibility IS 'DB OpenStation NeTEx: one row per StopPlace and per Quay a release lists — the AccessibilityAssessment (NeTEx LimitationStatusEnumeration words, verbatim), PlatformHeight (raw and folded to cm) and PlateCode. CC0 1.0. Diff-and-close: an element the newest release does not carry gets closed_at, and a row is never deleted.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.station_equipment IS 'DB OpenStation NeTEx inventory: one row per lift, escalator or travelator a release lists, keyed by its DIID. CC0 1.0. Diff-and-close: an id the newest release does not carry gets closed_at, and a row is never deleted. equipment_no is the FaSta equipment number, so a db_facilities row and a row here describe the same lift by a published id.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.station_equipment_state IS 'The present state of each facility, upserted per pull. OpenStation SIRI FM: CC0 1.0, a snapshot of every monitored facility. KVB: DL-DE Zero 2.0, inferred from a FRESH fault file — listed = inactive, not listed = active, and the escalators KVB says it cannot report a fault for = unknown; a failed or stale fault file writes nothing. In both, a row past the feed''s freshness window is read as unknown — never as its last state.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.station_equipment_ticks IS 'Append-only, day-partitioned, CHANGE-ONLY history of OpenStation facility states: a row only when the verbatim status or its description moved. Nothing is lost because every change is kept; station_equipment_state carries the present.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.station_metro_osm IS 'OpenStreetMap-derived (ODbL 1.0, its own boundary station_metro_osm): per zHV tram level, the nearest underground OSM station node (the railway family of the OSM named-place table) within 300 m. Replaced whole by each station-index derivation; a level listed here is a station page. Never named beside a table of another licence; offered in its share-alike export.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.station_slugs IS 'The readable slug of each station page, assigned once and never changed or freed (trigger). A renamed station gets a new row; the old slug keeps pointing at the station and is answered with a 301 to the newest.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.stop_access_odbl IS 'ODbL 1.0 — the TU Chemnitz (OPENER next) VDV-462 export of step-free stop attributes, derived from OpenStreetMap (register key tuc_vdv462). Its own share-alike database: never joined to another licence''s table, offered whole by its share-alike export (ODbL § 4.6). One summary row per stop-level DHID; never OSM node ids, path geometry or organisations. Closed, never deleted.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.street_bays IS 'On-street parking bays with an occupancy sensor, one row per sensor the publisher reported in its inventory window; diff-and-close, never deleted. Not parking_facilities: a disabled-badge bay is reserved, an EV bay belongs to its charge point, a loading zone is not parking. Dortmund: DL-DE Zero 2.0.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.street_bay_state IS 'The present state of every bay a pull reported: the state of the publisher''s NEWEST message, which an older message never overwrites. A bay whose newest message is older than the read rule''s window is no reading — never its last state.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.street_bay_ticks IS 'Append-only, day-partitioned, CHANGE-ONLY history of the on-street bay sensors: a row only when a bay''s state moved. Nothing is lost because every change we saw is kept; street_bay_state carries the present.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.tick_storage_days IS 'Bytes each daily-partitioned tick table held per UTC day, the largest size the tick cleaner measured before dropping the day. Kept 90 days by the same pass.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.tmc_locations IS 'BASt Location Code List (TMC, CC BY 4.0): point locations of table D/01, so Alert-C codes resolve to a road and a point. Reference data, rebuilt by the location-code load.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.traffic_count_latest IS 'The newest count of every site and measure: moved only by a newer interval or a correction of the same one. Every count is in traffic_count_ticks.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.traffic_count_profiles IS 'Counted-traffic profiles: per site, month, kind of day and local hour, the median hourly count over the month''s days of that kind and the days behind it. A typical value from an archive, never a present count.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.traffic_count_sites IS 'The counting sites of the traffic-count feeds: one row per site (one location, one direction), its point and the datastream of each measure. Diffed daily, closed from absence, never deleted.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.traffic_count_ticks IS 'Append-only, day-partitioned history of the traffic-count feeds: one row per (site, measure, interval, count) the first time a pull carries it; a corrected count is a second row for its interval. Never updated, never deleted.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.transit_delays IS 'Public-transport delay ticks from DELFI GTFS-RT Trip Updates (Mobilithek offer 858688352316981248). LICENCE: CC BY-SA 4.0 — register id delfi_rt_tu in the source register. Share-alike is viral: anything published from these rows carries the same licence, and they may not be blended into a CC BY table without re-licensing that table too. Rule 2 of the register puts BY-SA derived tables under a delfi_ prefix; this table predates the rule and keeps its name for now (renaming a partitioned table in production is its own reviewed change), so source = ''delfi_rt_tu'' is the marker that travels with the row. Append-only, day-partitioned on observed_at. Written change-only: a row exists because the delay or the schedule relationship for that (trip, stop, sequence) MOVED, so the absence of a row means "unchanged since the previous one", never "not observed". No MCP tool reads this table yet — the register says aggregates only until the share-alike opinion lands.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.translation_cache IS 'Machine translations of traffic-message sentences: key = sha256 of the normalised, masked German sentence; text = the masked translation. Insert-once (on conflict do nothing); last_used_at moved by the digest at most daily; rows unused 90 days are deleted by the retention job''s age family. Written by viafrei_translate only.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.validator_horizon IS 'At most one row: no feed_runs validator from before not_before (or before the feed''s first load after it) is sent again — written by the state copy step.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.vbb_transit_delays IS 'VBB GTFS Realtime trip updates (CC BY 4.0, register vbb_rt, https://production.gtfsrt.vbb.de): append-only, day-partitioned, change-only delay history for Berlin and Brandenburg. Its own table so CC BY rows never sit in the CC BY-SA transit_delays and the reverse; the CHECK admits the vbb_rt key only. Trip ids are VBB''s own and match nothing we hold; stop ids are DHIDs. Read only as region-wide aggregates (check_transit_disruption).';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.vbn_siri_et_calls IS 'VBN SIRI ET "Niedersachsen und Bremen" (CC BY 4.0, register vbn_siri_et, Mobilithek offer 782935022861258752): append-only, day-partitioned, change-only call history. Its own table so CC BY rows never sit in the CC BY-SA delfi_siri_et_calls and the reverse; the CHECK admits the vbn_siri_et key only. Read only as region-wide aggregates through the vbn_transit_delays views (check_transit_disruption, the place card), and only when DELFI is silent or stale.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.vrn_transit_delays IS 'VRN/RNN GTFS Realtime trip updates (CC BY 4.0, register vrn_rt, https://www.vrn.de/service/entwickler/gtfs-realtime/): append-only, day-partitioned, change-only delay history for the VRN and RNN Kreise. Its own table so CC BY rows never sit in the CC BY-SA transit_delays and the reverse; the CHECK admits the vrn_rt key only. Trip ids are VRN''s own and match nothing we hold; stop ids are DHIDs. Read only as region-wide aggregates (check_transit_disruption, the place card).';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.watches IS 'One open request to be told about a change. SESSION-SCOPED: session_id is the MCP session id and there is deliberately no identity column, no address and no channel — nothing leaves the MCP session, which is what keeps this table free of consent and spam questions. At most ten open rows per session (trigger watches_limit_per_session) and at most 24 hours of life (constraint watches_window_24h), both enforced here rather than in the transport.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.watch_events IS 'What the notifier found, one row per watch per rate-limit minute. Append-only except for the deliberate FOLD: a second change inside the minute rewrites this row (revision, updated_at, the union of source_ids) instead of inserting a second one, which is how "one notification per watch per minute" is implemented in the engine rather than in the transport. NOT a tick table — it is our own rendering of changes the tick archive already holds, it is recomputable, and it is purged by AGE after seven days by the retention job.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.weather_warnings IS 'Amtliche Wetterwarnungen of the Deutscher Wetterdienst, one row per warning per warn cell per version. CC BY 4.0 with the Quellenvermerk prescribed by § 7 DWD-Gesetz. The official text is stored verbatim and hashed: an ALTERED amtliche Warnung may not carry the Quellenvermerk at all, so the read path reproduces it byte for byte under "Quelle: Deutscher Wetterdienst" or summarises it under "Datenbasis: Deutscher Wetterdienst, zusammengefasst" - never both.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.weather_warnings_en IS 'The DWD''s own English text of its amtliche Wetterwarnungen (CAP, COMMUNEUNION_DWD_STAT, EN), one row per alert per text version. Text only: the German row in weather_warnings is the authority for level, window and cell, joined by base_identifier. CC BY 4.0, "Quelle: Deutscher Wetterdienst" when reproduced unaltered.';
COMMENT ON TABLE public.worker_boots IS 'One row per process start: which worker, which code, on which container, with which effective configuration. Written by the same contained boot hook that publishes feed_catalog, so a status page can answer "what is this thing running with" without reading anyone''s environment. Never updated; each worker deletes its OWN rows older than 30 days as it boots, so the table is bounded without a second process. config is an ALLOW-LIST report: unclassified variables are absent, credentials appear as set/unset, lists as counts, connection strings as host:port/dbname — never a user, a password, a token, a 19-digit Subscription-ID or an IP literal.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION control.admit(pg_catalog.text,pg_catalog.text,pg_catalog.text) IS 'THE admission: lock the key''s budget_state row, judge hold, spacing, yield (to a person, or to a person caller owed its floor, 0285), ceiling, share, reserved, burst in that order, and record the answer (a usage bucket, or a refusals row). A caller below its floor never yields to an owed mark; only an ask the counts would admit stamps a person''s want. The client treats any error as "no request" (fail closed).';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION control.fit_shares(pg_catalog.text) IS 'Move one key''s shares inside the bound of 0285: on a key of two or more callers with a ceiling above 0 each floor at least 1 and each cap at most the ceiling less the other callers'' floors; with one caller or a ceiling of 0, cap and floor at most the ceiling. A share with a cap of 0 is switched off and stays 0/0. A key already inside is not touched. Callable by the schema owner only.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION control.publish_terms(pg_catalog.jsonb) IS 'Publish the workers'' declaration of provider terms: run by every worker boot. Callable by the schema owner only.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION control.rollup_refresh(timestamp with time zone) IS 'The admission rollup: usage_hourly upserted from usage and refusals since p_from (default the last 2 hours), then usage older than 48 h, refusals older than 14 days and usage_hourly older than 90 days deleted. Called by ops_rollup_refresh() every 5 minutes.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.autobahn_event_raw(pg_catalog.text,timestamp with time zone) IS 'The keyed form of autobahn_raw_resolve, for a human and for the tests that prove "recoverable" is a claim rather than a sentence: name a tick by (ext_id, observed_at) and get its payload. Not for a read path — it costs a lookup of the row the read path already holds. It prunes to the tick''s own partition but is NOT index-assisted on `ext_id` for a row that stores no payload, so it is a diagnostic and never a loop.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.autobahn_event_texts_backfill(timestamp with time zone) IS 'Re-derive `autobahn_event_texts` from `autobahn_events` — the seed migration 0047 runs once, and the repair the read path names when it refuses a stale rollup. Idempotent, monotone AND deterministic: stamps widen with least/greatest, and the point moves for a strictly newer event or, among events sharing the newest instant, for the one the SAME total order the worker''s fold uses prefers — a located event over an unlocated one, then the smallest geom::text (migration 0050). Not ext_id: this rollup does not record which event its point came from, so an ext_id rule would be inexpressible in the worker''s upsert, which compares an incoming row against a stored one. So it may be re-run at any time, in any order, while the worker is writing, and it and the worker agree about which coordinate a tied triple gets. It does hold row locks for its duration, so a sweep upserting the same triples waits and can hit the worker pool''s statement timeout: the cost of repairing while ingestion runs is at most ONE lost cycle per road x kind, which the next sweep restates. `since` bounds the pass to one window; NULL is the whole archive. It cannot REMOVE a row whose ticks retention has dropped, and that is deliberate — a junction we learned in August is still a junction.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.autobahn_jam_rollup_refresh(timestamp with time zone,timestamp with time zone) IS 'Fold the autobahn_events ticks of the whole hours [p_from, p_to) touches into autobahn_jam_hours. Default: the previous and current hour, back to the newest hour held after an outage (at most a day). At most 2 days per call. Returns the rows inserted or changed; a re-run changes none.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.autobahn_raw_resolve(pg_catalog.jsonb,pg_catalog.bytea,pg_catalog.text,timestamp with time zone) IS 'The payload of one autobahn_events tick, whether or not that row stores it: `raw` when present, else the newest earlier row of the same ext_id in the same UTC day whose `raw` is stored and whose `raw_hash` is identical, else NULL. EVERY reader of `autobahn_events.raw` goes through this — the mcp consumer''s autobahn and road-status queries and the ingest worker''s road-now read do — because a bare `raw` is NULL on about 97 % of the rows this release writes. It never guesses: the hash equality is inside the search, so a wrong answer is impossible and a missing one is visible.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.autobahn_validity_overlaps(pg_catalog.jsonb,timestamp with time zone,timestamp with time zone) IS 'Whether an Autobahn GmbH item''s stated validity windows (its description, under "Die Baustelle ist zu folgenden Zeiträumen gültig:", minus the days under "gilt nicht an folgenden Tagen") overlap [from_at, to_at]: TRUE / FALSE, or NULL when the description states no readable window (0103). Berlin wall-clock times; a window ending at or before its start runs past midnight.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.charging_house_key(pg_catalog.text,pg_catalog.text) IS 'The first run of digits of the house number, or of the street line when the house number has none (Eco-Movement folds it into the street). NULL when neither has a digit: an unknown house number never contradicts a known one. The site-twin rule of find_charging_station (0072).';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.charging_operator_tokens(pg_catalog.text) IS 'The comparable words of a charge-point operator name: lower-cased, split on anything that is not a letter or digit, words under three letters and legal forms / generic words dropped. Two operators "match" when the arrays overlap. The site-twin rule of find_charging_station (0072); the stop list is part of the rule, so it lives here and nowhere else.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.charging_register_free_of_charge(pg_catalog.jsonb) IS 'The register''s "Bezahlsysteme" name "Kostenlos"; NULL exactly when charging_register_payment_card is (one cell).';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.charging_register_opening_hours(pg_catalog.jsonb) IS 'The Ladesäulenregister''s "Öffnungszeiten" (extras.device.openingHours), typed: always ("247") or restricted ("Eingeschränkt"); NULL for "Keine Angabe", absent or an unknown spelling — never read as either.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.charging_register_parking(pg_catalog.jsonb) IS 'The register''s "Informationen zum Parkraum" (extras.device.parking), typed: public ("Keine Beschränkung") or customers (a "Kunden" restriction); NULL otherwise.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.charging_register_payment_card(pg_catalog.jsonb) IS 'The register''s "Bezahlsysteme" (extras.device.payment) name a credit or debit card; NULL when no payment cell is published.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.charging_street_key(pg_catalog.text) IS 'A street name reduced to its letters, with ß folded to ss and Straße/Strasse/Str. folded to str — digits are dropped because Eco-Movement writes the house number into the street line ("Albstraße 15") and the Ladesäulenregister does not. Empty string for no street. The site-twin rule of find_charging_station (0072).';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.ensure_daily_partitions(integer) IS 'Daily partitions for every range-partitioned table in this schema whose single partition key is observed_at, from current_date - 3 through current_date + days_ahead. The table list is read from pg_partitioned_table (0033). It REFUSES fewer covered tables than partition_settings.min_daily_tables (0043; the floor was a constant of seven until 0065 moved it into that one-row table at eight; a tick table''s migration raises it with one UPDATE), a missing settings row, and any range-partitioned table the predicate does not reach; both assertions run before the first create. Since 0203 every partition in the window leaves with autosummarize = on on its BRIN indexes (tick_brin_autosummarize), set only where it is missing. Since 0269 a missing day is created LIKE its parent and ATTACHED (SHARE UPDATE EXCLUSIVE on the parent), never created as a partition (ACCESS EXCLUSIVE), so no read or write of a tick table waits for this run. The run ends in a notice naming the table count, the day range, how many partitions it made and how many BRIN indexes it set.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.fuel_price_outliers(numeric,numeric,numeric,interval) IS 'Ticks outside [lo, hi] or jumping more than `jump` (fraction) from the previous tick of the same station and grade, newest first, within `since`. Recorded, never dropped.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.fuel_station_land(public.geography) IS 'The Land of a point, as the fuel board names it: the nearest open BKG municipality''s left(admin_code, 2) (0069''s rule, one definition since 0141).';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.fuel_stations_relabel_land() IS 'Recompute fuel_stations.land and town_places where the nearest open BKG municipalities changed (after a gazetteer reload). Returns the rows changed (0141, 0217).';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.fuel_station_town_places(public.geography) IS 'The candidate towns of a fuel station: the five open BKG municipalities nearest the point, those within 20 km, nearest first, as [{ags, name}] (0217). Which one the station''s MTS-K town names is decided by the reader.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.fuel_warm_cadence(integer,double precision,double precision,double precision,double precision,integer,integer) IS 'The fuel warm cycle''s cadence: the median gap between consecutive price changes over the warm set, and the sample count. Two numbers only — no price, no station — so viafrei_mcp may run it while fuel_prices stays closed to it.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.growth_weekly_refresh(integer) IS 'Recomputes growth_weekly for the last N ISO weeks (Berlin, the current one included) from search_console_weekly, site_visits_daily/_routes and mcp_usage, then deletes weeks older than two years. Idempotent. Run by the periodic rollup job.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.lez_zone_overlaps_refresh(pg_catalog.text,pg_catalog.text) IS 'Recompute one lez_zones row''s pairs in lez_zone_overlaps: delete them, and insert both directions against every open zone row of another feed whose interior meets it. The one definition the triggers and the migration''s backfill use.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.mcp_usage_traffic_class(pg_catalog.text) IS 'The MCP usage traffic class of a ledger client name: own (our probes, rounds, soaks and any viafrei-* client), scanners (unknown, stateless) or real. Held equal to the status consumer''s own client lists by its tests.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.ops_audit_append_only() IS 'Refuses every UPDATE and DELETE on ops_audit (0104), except setting client_ip to NULL on a row older than 12 months with every other column unchanged.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.ops_audit_forget_ips() IS 'Sets ops_audit.client_ip to NULL on every row older than 12 months and returns the count; writes one ingest_runs row (provider ops_audit_retention) when the count is above 0, or as a daily heartbeat when there has been none for 24 h. Run by the periodic rollup job every 5 minutes.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.ops_rollup_refresh(timestamp with time zone,timestamp with time zone) IS 'Rolls feed_runs/ingest_runs into 5-minute buckets for [from, to) (at most 8 days per call), refreshes the Berlin days it touched in ops_rollup_daily and ops_rollup_usage (ops_rollup_usage_fold, 0251), records today''s held-fact estimates, drops bucket partitions older than 90 days, and refreshes the admission rollup (control.rollup_refresh, 0261/0263). Idempotent. Run by the periodic rollup job every 5 minutes.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.ops_rollup_usage_fold(pg_catalog.date,pg_catalog.date) IS 'Folds mcp_usage''s hourly tools/call counters into ops_rollup_usage for the Berlin days [from, to], per tool and traffic class; errors leave out a tool''s correct refusals (invalid_input, not_found, out_of_coverage). Idempotent. Called by ops_rollup_refresh() (migration 0251).';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.road_event_in_force(timestamp with time zone,timestamp with time zone,pg_catalog.jsonb,pg_catalog.tstzmultirange,timestamp with time zone) IS 'Is this road event in force at `at`? Outside the windows its validity states (road_events.valid_windows) it is not; otherwise the four-argument rule of 0024 decides (start, end, suspended). A NULL valid_windows changes nothing.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.road_event_in_force(timestamp with time zone,timestamp with time zone,pg_catalog.jsonb,timestamp with time zone) IS 'Is this road event in force at `at`? A stated start after `at` or a stated end before it is decisive; otherwise the event is in force unless every validityStatus in the payload says `suspended`. The clock is an ARGUMENT so that road_events_current can pass now() and the ingest worker''s road-now read can pass a test clock — one rule, two callers, no second copy to drift. Recurring sub-periods are NOT evaluated: see migration 0024 for the counts behind that choice.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.road_event_in_force_known(timestamp with time zone,timestamp with time zone,boolean,pg_catalog.jsonb,pg_catalog.tstzmultirange,timestamp with time zone) IS 'road_event_in_force(overall_start, overall_end, payload, valid_windows, at) with road_events.suspended read instead of the payload where it is set. The same answer for every row: the trigger keeps suspended = road_event_suspended(payload).';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.road_event_suspended(pg_catalog.jsonb) IS 'True when EVERY validityStatus in a DATEX II situation payload says `suspended` — a situation whose phases are all paused. A payload that states no status is not suspended: absence is not a claim. Used by road_event_in_force; see migration 0024 for the rule and for what it deliberately does not evaluate.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.road_ref_key(pg_catalog.text) IS 'A stored road number as one key: upper case, no space/dot/hyphen/underscore, leading zeros of the number dropped. The same rule as the workers'' road key.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.tick_brin_autosummarize(pg_catalog.regclass) IS 'Sets autosummarize = on on every BRIN index of one (leaf) relation that does not carry it, and returns how many it set (0203). Each ALTER runs in its own subtransaction under lock_timeout = 2s; an index another session holds is skipped with a NOTICE naming it (never an error), and the caller''s lock_timeout is restored. Called by ensure_daily_partitions for every partition in its window. A partitioned parent index cannot carry the option in PostgreSQL 16 (ALTER … SET is refused), so the partitions do.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.vf_ledger_mask(pg_catalog.text) IS 'The ledger mask in SQL: IPv4 then IPv6 literals to <ip>. Held equal to the workers'' mask by the ledger-mask fixture.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.vf_postcode(pg_catalog.text) IS 'A German postcode as five digits (one rule with the readers'' postcode display since 0184): edge whitespace (space, tab, LF, VT, FF, CR, NBSP) trimmed; five digits stand, four get their leading zero back, a text with exactly one run of exactly five digits is that run, anything else is null.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.watches_enforce_session_limit() IS 'Refuses the eleventh open watch of a session with a message naming the limit of ten. A trigger and not a constraint because a constraint cannot count rows; under a per-session advisory lock because an uncounted concurrent insert is how a limit becomes a suggestion.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.watch_events_notify() IS 'Wakes every LISTENer on channel viafrei_watch with {"v":1,"watch_id":N,"event_id":M} — ids only. A notification is NOT a queue: it is lost if nobody is listening, so the listener treats it as a hint and reads the table. Fires on INSERT only; the rate limit''s fold is deliberately silent.';
COMMENT ON FUNCTION public.weather_warning_english(bigint) IS 'The DWD''s own English text of one German warning (weather_warnings.id), or no row (0204). THE one definition of the match: same sent instant, same onset, same expiry (both open alike), the German cell among the English alert''s cells, and the same eventCode II; the newest sent wins a tie. The DWD gives ENG and DEU different UUIDs, so the identifier is not the key (0202 matched 0 of 54). Measured on prod 2026-10-23: exactly one English row for each of 11 194 open German area rows. Read by weather_warnings_area_live and by the MCP''s warning reads.';
COMMENT ON INDEX public.autobahn_events_raw_dedup IS 'The resolver''s walk-back: given an `ext_id` and a tick instant, the newest earlier row of that event IN THE SAME UTC DAY that stores a payload. Partial on `raw is not null and raw_hash is not null`, so it holds one entry per (event, stored payload) and nothing from before migration 0054 — it was empty on every existing partition the moment it was built, which is why a 73 GB table could grow it for free.';
COMMENT ON INDEX public.autobahn_events_recent IS 'The freshness-window index for the national road picture: the digest and the MCP road tools ask for the newest thirty minutes of ticks, and the 0001 BRIN cannot answer that, because the newest page ranges are the ones VACUUM has not summarised yet. A B-tree is maintained by the insert, so no render depends on when autovacuum last ran. The BRIN stays for wide historical range scans.';
COMMENT ON INDEX public.charging_points_evse_norm IS 'The separator-insensitive EVSE id: how find_charging_station finds the same physical point in a second feed to show it once.';
COMMENT ON INDEX public.charging_points_open_site IS 'The charging radius read''s points without the table: (source_feed, site_id) include the point columns the radius reads select from near points, partial on charging_points_live''s live, publishable rows (0207''s predicate). A column added to that read and not here turns the Index Only Scan back into a heap read.';
COMMENT ON INDEX public.charging_points_provider_point IS 'A status feed that names points by the publisher''s point id (a status feed whose registry row names point ids) is joined to its one master feed through this pair.';
COMMENT ON INDEX public.charging_sites_withheld_site IS 'Open sites a publisher marks withheld, by site id alone — the watch notifier''s charger anti-join. Almost empty by design.';
COMMENT ON INDEX public.delfi_siri_et_calls_latest_departed IS 'The loader''s prune: a row whose departure is eight hours past is removed through this index. aimed_dep never changes on an upsert, so unlike the observed_at indexes it replaced it does not stop an update from being HOT.';
COMMENT ON INDEX public.delfi_siri_et_calls_observed_brin IS 'The tick table''s BRIN on observed_at: the hourly EVA<->DHID bridge reads an eight-hour window of ticks, which without it was a Seq Scan of each whole day partition.';
COMMENT ON INDEX public.gazetteer_places_bkg_city_geom_gist IS 'The open BKG municipalities alone: the nearest-municipality kNN of fuel_station_board_live, the MCP fuel queries and the digest place lookups, without walking past every stop and GeoNames place.';
COMMENT ON INDEX public.gazetteer_places_geonames_name_en IS 'The exonym read: a GeoNames town by its English name, the few rows that have one (the station area card)';
COMMENT ON INDEX public.ingest_runs_provider_finished IS 'The newest finished run of a provider, with its status: the coverage read asks each ledger for its newest run of an accepted status with LIMIT 1, and this index answers it as an index-only walk. feed_runs_feed_finished (0207) is the same index for the other ledger.';
COMMENT ON INDEX public.osm_addresses_geom_gist IS 'Nearest-neighbour support for describe_location: `order by geom <-> point limit n`. Partial on closed_at is null for the same reason as gazetteer_places_geom_gist and osm_pois_geom — a withdrawn address is the wrong answer to "what is here", not a stale one.';
COMMENT ON INDEX public.osm_addresses_postcode IS 'A postcode alone for the place sheet: postcode = $1 on the live rows, the centre of the addresses that carry it.';
COMMENT ON INDEX public.osm_addresses_street_number_place IS 'The town as a prefix for find_address: street and house number by equality, the town as a byte range (~>=~ / ~<~), all three index conditions. text_pattern_ops because the default collation (en_US) cannot order a prefix with a space in it.';
COMMENT ON INDEX public.osm_pois_name_norm IS 'A point of interest by its exact folded name for the place sheet: name_norm = $1 on the live rows.';
COMMENT ON INDEX public.stops_name_trgm IS 'Fuzzy stop-name search: GIN over `lower(name)` with `gin_trgm_ops`. It REPLACED a plain btree on `lower(name)` that carried this same name from 0001 through 0051 and could serve neither `similarity()`/`%` nor a `like` prefix under the default collation, so the name promised a capability the object did not have — the defect being fixed here is as much the name as the plan. An expression and not a stored `name_norm` column (which is what `gazetteer_places` and `osm_pois` need, because their fold is `unaccent()` and that is not immutable): `lower()` is immutable and is already exactly what the reader computes. Single-column because `smallint` has no GIN operator class without `btree_gin`, so the `gtfs_feed_key` equality stays a filter.';
COMMENT ON INDEX public.stops_parent IS 'The children of a parent station: `gtfs_feed_key, parent_id`, partial on a non-null parent. It is the second arm of the OR both stop-resolution statements join on — `s.id = r.root or s.parent_id = r.root` — and the feed key leads so that arm is symmetric with the primary key''s and the pair can be a BitmapOr instead of a sequential scan of 550 396 rows. A parent station row carries no parent, so the partial predicate excludes only rows this index could never answer for.';
COMMENT ON INDEX public.transit_delays_kreis IS 'The Kreis index for check_transit_disruption: (first five digits of the stop DHID''s AGS, observed_at desc). A region asked at Kreis depth reads its own slice of the window instead of its whole Land''s, which for a 120-minute question about one NRW Kreis was past the 10 s pool timeout. The expression must stay identical to the Kreis expression the tool''s statement uses, or the index is never chosen.';
COMMENT ON INDEX public.transit_delays_region IS 'The region index for check_transit_disruption: (Land of the stop DHID, observed_at desc). The tool asks for one region over a window of at most 2 x 120 minutes, and before this index the region restricted no scan at all — the statement read the whole nationwide window (1.19 M rows for a 60-minute question) and was cancelled at the pool statement_timeout for every region on production. The key is the first two digits of the AGS because that makes the region an equality and the window a range on the second column; it is NULL for a stop id that is not a readable DHID, and that NULL key is read too, because unreadable_stop_ids is nationwide by design. The expression must stay identical to the Land expression the tool''s statement uses — an expression index that no longer matches is not an error, it is a silent full scan.';
COMMENT ON INDEX public.vbn_siri_et_calls_feed_ts IS 'VBN''s freshness probe: max(feed_ts) where observed_at >= $1 and feed_ts >= $1 walks this index backwards to its first row instead of reading every call of the window through the BRIN — one pull is one feed_ts over up to ~434 000 calls, 8.5 s on prod before.';
COMMENT ON MATERIALIZED VIEW public.gtfs_route_kreise IS 'Per timetable route: the distinct 5-digit AGS Kreis keys read off the DHIDs of every stop its trips call at (0083). The service-alert loader places an alert that names routes and no stop by these Kreise (placed_by = route) instead of by the route id''s network. Built from delfi_static (CC BY 4.0) only; refreshed after every GTFS load without blocking readers.';
COMMENT ON MATERIALIZED VIEW public.gtfs_untimed_trips IS 'Trips whose static timetable gives every stop ONE time (more than one stop, earliest = latest scheduled time): a real-time delay for them is their travel time, not a delay, so punctuality figures leave them out (0069). Refreshed after every GTFS load without blocking readers.';
COMMENT ON MATERIALIZED VIEW public.osm_street_tiles IS 'The open osm_streets ways of classes 2-6 (never 1) collected per class and 1 km cell of their box centre (EPSG:25832), each row a MultiLineString of the ways unchanged: the band-0 map read (frames up to 20 km) dumps the cells meeting its frame and keeps the ways whose own box meets it, the same set it read from osm_streets, from ~30x fewer pages. ODbL 1.0, inside the osm_streets boundary; refreshed without blocking readers by the streets loader after each Land.';
COMMENT ON MATERIALIZED VIEW public.osm_street_tiles_lo IS 'The open osm_streets ways of classes 2-4 (never 1) by their 40 m geometry (geom_lo), collected per class and 10 km cell of their box centre (EPSG:25832), each row a MultiLineString of the ways unchanged: the wide map bands (frames above 20 km) dump the cells meeting their frame and keep the ways whose own box meets it, the same set they read from osm_streets, from a few hundred pages instead of tens of thousands. ODbL 1.0, inside the osm_streets boundary; refreshed without blocking readers by the streets loader after each Land.';
COMMENT ON MATERIALIZED VIEW public.transit_stop_modes IS 'One row per stop family of the static timetable (the parent station, else the stop): name, point, the modes its routes run (rail, sbahn = extended type 109 only, subway, tram, bus, ferry), up to 200 line names and the member stop ids. Built from delfi_static (CC BY 4.0) only; refreshed after every GTFS load without blocking readers; its (stop, route) pairs are formed bytewise. Read by the digest consumer for the nearest-stations card.';
COMMENT ON SCHEMA control IS 'Controlling the workers: who may ask which provider how often. control.admit() is the one decision point; the workers'' HTTP client the one enforcement point. Licence facts are published from the workers'' declaration of provider terms; tunable values live only here.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN control.budgets.reserve_pct IS 'The share of the ceiling the allocator leaves unplanned (a retry, a slow cycle, a run-now). Planning only: admit() never reads it.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN control.budget_state.owed_by IS 'The caller the next free slot of a spaced key is owed to (0285): a caller below its floor refused for the spacing. For two spacings after owed_at every other caller at or above its floor yields. Cleared when that caller is admitted.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN ingestor.runs.status IS 'ok | unchanged (304) | partial (some rows rejected, or an incomplete read that closed nothing) | error | skipped (disabled or not due) | limited (the limit claim was not granted in the wait budget; no request was made).';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.ags_regions.ags IS 'The Amtlicher Gemeindeschlüssel, 2 digits for a Land and 5 for a Kreis or kreisfreie Stadt, leading zero INCLUDED — it is a key, not a number, and 06435 is not 6435. The hierarchy is the prefix: every AGS under Bayern starts 09, so a region filter is starts_with(code, ags) and never a recursive join.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.ags_regions.geom IS 'The region CENTROID, copied from the gazetteer''s point for this AGS — not a boundary. A lat/lon question is answered by the nearest centroid, and the answer names the region it landed on so a reader can see that it was placed rather than contained. NULL on a seeded Land row until a gazetteer load supplies one.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.ags_regions.name_en_norm IS 'name_en put through the SAME name fold as name_norm, and the column an English question is actually matched against. The resolver folds what the caller typed, so "North Rhine-Westphalia" arrives as "north rhine westphalia"; comparing that with lower(name_en) can never match a hyphenated name, which is how a quarter of the Länder came to answer "I do not know it" in English. NULL exactly when name_en is — ags_regions_name_en_paired enforces it, so an English name can never be stored unfolded and silently unreachable.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.ags_regions.name_norm IS 'The searchable fold of the name, written by the same name fold as gazetteer_places.name_norm: lower-case, diacritics to the bare letter (ü→u), ß→ss, runs of punctuation to one space, trimmed. NOT a generated column — unaccent() is STABLE, not IMMUTABLE, and an index over a fake-immutable wrapper corrupts when the dictionary changes.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.ags_regions.refreshed_at IS 'When the gazetteer load last confirmed this row. A row the gazetteer stops publishing keeps its last value: this is a derived map with no history of its own, and an AGS that vanished from one download is far more likely a truncated file than an abolished Kreis.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.ags_regions.source_id IS 'Provenance, and the ATTRIBUTION register key where one applies. destatis_gv = the 16 seeded Land rows: the Amtlicher Gemeindeschlüssel and the Länder''s names are an amtliches Werk (§ 5 Abs. 1 UrhG) with no copyright and no attribution obligation, so this is NOT a register key and the MCP layer drops it when it builds an attribution line. bkg_gvisys = a Kreis row refreshed from the gazetteer''s BKG gv-isys rows (dl-de/by-2-0), which IS a register key, so a result that used one names BKG.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.autobahn_events.raw_hash IS 'sha256 of the CANONICAL payload bytes of this tick — object keys sorted recursively, so two payloads that differ only in key order hash alike, which is also how jsonb stores them. NULL on every row written before migration 0054, where `raw` is always present. On a row written after it: `raw_hash` is always set, and `raw` is set only when the content differed from the payload this writer last stored for this `ext_id` in this UTC day. Read the payload of ANY row through autobahn_raw_resolve(), never through `raw` alone.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.autobahn_events.source_run_id IS 'The cycle that appended this tick: `feed_runs.id` for the Mobilithek feeds (transit_delays, road_event_ticks, charging_point_status), `ingest_runs.id` for the Autobahn and Tankerkönig sweeps (autobahn_events, fuel_prices). Ticks are append-only, so it is written once and never updated. NULL on partitions written before the provenance migration.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.autobahn_event_texts.first_seen_at IS 'The oldest `observed_at` of any event with this triple — for the seeded rows, of any event the archive still held when migration 0047 ran. Never read by the gazetteer; it costs one `min()` in a pass that is already running and answers "since when has the provider been saying this".';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.autobahn_event_texts.geom IS 'The point of the newest event that named this triple, and NULL when that event carried no readable coordinate. One real event''s point, never an average of several — which is what the statement this replaces did, and what the junction reader downstream still expects. When several events share the newest instant the choice among them is arbitrary, exactly as it was in the old `distinct on`, whose ORDER BY also stopped at `observed_at desc`.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.autobahn_event_texts.last_seen_at IS 'The newest `observed_at` of any event with this triple, and the column the gazetteer''s 30-day window filters on — `last_seen_at >= $1` selects exactly the triples the old `distinct on` over that window returned. It only ever advances (`greatest(…)`, in both writers), so a replayed or out-of-order sweep cannot drag it back. It is also the FRESHNESS signal: `max(last_seen_at)` here against `max(observed_at)` in the window says whether the rollup is still being maintained, and the read path refuses to answer when it is not.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.autobahn_event_texts.road IS 'The road the sweep asked about, exactly as `autobahn_events.road` holds it — not folded, not check-constrained, because the reader''s own ^A[0-9]{1,3}$ filter must keep seeing (and counting) exactly what it sees in the archive today.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.autobahn_event_texts.subtitle IS 'The event subtitle, or '''' where the archive holds NULL, for the reason `title` carries.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.autobahn_event_texts.title IS 'The event title, or '''' where the archive holds NULL. The empty string rather than NULL because the statement this table replaces grouped on `coalesce(title,'''')`: a nullable key column would make two rows out of one group. Nothing downstream can tell them apart — the junction reader finds no junction in null or ''''.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.autobahn_roads.first_seen_at IS 'The first time we saw this number at all — in a published list, or as the oldest `observed_at` of its own events for a row migration 0042 backfilled; never updated. Bookkeeping about us, not a fact about the road — but a rollback-and-reload does NOT reset it for free on every row: a listed road is refilled the same by the next sweep, while an event-only row (no witness but its own archive) is not refilled by any sweep and comes back only from a fresh event, a future listing, or re-running this migration''s backfill by hand.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.autobahn_roads.last_listed_at IS 'The most recent COMPLETE sweep that found this number in the provider''s published road list — NULL for a road we know only from our own event archive, which is the honest value and A25''s state on production the day this column was added. Only the list writer ever sets it, and only on a complete sweep, which is what keeps the two cross-road readings true: compared with `max(last_listed_at)` it says whether the provider still lists this road (which is why there is no `closed_at` — the close is derivable on one heap page), and that maximum compared with now() says whether our own sweep is still running, corroborating the `ingest_runs` verdict a tool must actually judge pipeline health by. A partial sweep must not write this column: it would advance the maximum and make every motorway it did not ask about look unlisted. Do not read a NULL, or a stamp behind the maximum, as "this road is gone": the published list is volatile (A25: 19 of 236 snapshots on 2026-09-25), and existence is what the ROW says.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.autobahn_roads.last_seen_at IS 'The most recent sighting by EITHER witness: a complete sweep that listed this number, or a sweep that stored an event for it (0042 backfilled it from that road''s newest `observed_at`). From a sweep''s own observedAt, never from a trigger, and it only advances (both writers use `greatest(…)`, so a replayed sweep cannot drag it back, which is what keeps `autobahn_roads_seen_order` from firing on a legitimate re-run). Read it as "are we still looking at all". It is NOT the column a cross-road comparison may use — an event witness is per-road, so a sweep pinned to A9 advances A9''s alone, on purpose: that is a true statement about A9 and about nothing else. Whether the PROVIDER still lists a road is `last_listed_at`.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.autobahn_roads.road IS 'The provider''s own motorway number, uppercase and trimmed (''A9''), exactly as the road-list parser normalises it — and the primary key, because "is A210 a motorway" IS the question this table answers. Check-constrained to ^A[0-9]{1,3}$: the provider ships ''A60 '' with a trailing space today, and an untrimmed duplicate would be a second A60 no query could ever ask for.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.bfrk_stops.entrances IS 'The station''s entrances from the street as the publisher lists them (the VBB''s `Zugang` elements); 0 where a publisher lists none.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.bfrk_stops.entrances_lift IS 'Of `entrances`, those whose own name says a lift serves them.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.bw_detours.route IS 'The Verlauf column verbatim — the ministry''s own words for the route, quoted to a person and never paraphrased into an instruction.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.closed_at IS 'When the point stopped appearing in this feed. NULL = still published. The PROVIDER''s fact, kept forever — our own retraction is deleted_at.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.confidentiality IS 'The AFIR (DATEX II v3) publication''s headerInformation.confidentiality; NULL when absent. Read paths return only ''noRestriction'' or NULL.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.connector_types IS 'Every connectorType.value of this point, as published (iec62196T2, iec62196T2COMBO, chademo, domesticF, teslaS, other, …). An array and not a child table: 98.9 % of points have exactly one and the maximum observed is three.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.created_at IS 'When WE first wrote this row. Never moves again — a row that is closed and reappears keeps it. Backfilled from first_seen_at by the provenance migration.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.current_type IS 'currentType.value as published: ''ac'', ''dc'', ''both''. Not derived from the power or the connector — a 22 kW AC post and a 22 kW DC post are different products, and the payload says which.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.deleted_at IS 'WE retracted this row (a duplicate folded, a parse judged wrong, a provider retraction, a manual correction) — it is hidden from every read and hard-deleted by the retention job once it is older than the retention window for retracted rows (at least 30 days). NOT closed_at, which says the PROVIDER stopped publishing the record and is kept forever; a row may carry both and neither implies the other.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.deleted_reason IS 'Why this row was retracted, in one short human sentence — "duplicate folded", "parse error, re-loaded", "provider retraction". Required by convention whenever deleted_at is set: a retraction with no reason is indistinguishable from an accident, and after the purge window nothing is left to ask.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.deleted_run_id IS 'The ingest_runs.id of the run that retracted the row, or NULL when a human did it by hand. Deliberately NOT a foreign key: an operator action has no run, and the ledger has its own (planned) retention, so a dangling id must stay readable rather than block the write.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.evse_id IS 'The EVSE id (ISO 15118 / DIN SPEC 91286, e.g. DE*EBW*E914082*2) — the ONLY id that joins this master data to the dynamic availability feeds and to the other publisher''s copy of the same socket. **The two feeds put it in different places and reading only one finds nothing in the other**: Eco-Movement has it in externalIdentifier[] where typeOfIdentifier.extendedValueG = ''evseId'' (66 247 of 66 808 points) and its idG is a provider-local string (''52000127*1'', a bare UUID) that differs from the EVSE id 60 089 times; EnBW carries no externalIdentifier on a point at all (0 of 11 472) and its idG IS the EVSE id (11 472 of 11 472 match the grammar). So: externalIdentifier first, then idG when idG matches the EVSE-id grammar, else NULL.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.extras IS 'Fields the publisher sends that no column of this table holds, kept as published rather than dropped. Written by the Bundesnetzagentur Ladesäulenregister loader (source_feed = ''bnetza_lsr''): the device''s status, kind, commissioning date, Adresszusatz, Kreis, Bundesland, location label, parking information, payment systems, opening hours, the registered operator name, and per point the plug text and Public Key. NULL on every AFIR row. The opening hours, payment systems and parking information are typed by the charging_register_* functions (0249), which charging_points_live exposes as register_* and the charging filter reads; no tool reads this column itself.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.first_seen_at IS 'The first cycle this row appeared in a payload. SEEN, not created: `created_at` is when we wrote it. Never updated.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.geom IS 'Copied from the station''s coordinates when it has them (EnBW: all 5 286) and from the site''s otherwise (Eco-Movement: 0 stations carry one, all 17 748 sites do). A charging point never carries its own coordinate in either feed. Denormalised on purpose: "points within N metres" is the read, and joining 78 280 points to their sites to answer it buys nothing.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.last_seen_at IS 'The most recent cycle this row appeared in a payload, and the diff-and-close presence marker. SEEN, not CHANGED: a publisher that re-sends an identical record moves this and leaves `updated_at` where it was.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.max_power_kw IS 'The maximum maxPowerAtSocket over this point''s connectors, converted from WATTS — both feeds publish watts (150000 for a 150 kW CCS post, 1 000 000 as the top value seen) and a column labelled kW holding 150000 is the kind of unit error nobody questions. Range observed: 0 W to 1 000 000 W.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.operator IS 'Copied from the parent site so that "every point of operator X" is one index scan on this table. Denormalisation with a stated reason: the source of truth is charging_sites.operator and the loader writes both in the same statement.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.point_key IS 'The row''s identity within its feed: the EVSE id when the payload carries one, otherwise `<site id>:<provider point id>`. Part of the primary key so the daily upsert is idempotent even where the publisher repeats a record.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.point_key_synthetic IS 'True when the payload carried no EVSE id and point_key is our own `<site id>:<provider point id>`. 561 of 66 808 Eco-Movement points (0.8 %), 0 of 11 472 EnBW. The surrogate is built from the provider''s own point id rather than the position in refillPoint[], so it survives the publisher reordering the array — an index-based key would close and re-open the same socket on every reshuffle.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.price_adhoc_zero IS 'The AFIR ad-hoc rate charges nothing: every energyPrice of it — per kWh, per minute, flat — is 0. False when any component is above 0, NULL when no ad-hoc rate is published or a component carries no number. Read as "free of charge" only while price_last_updated is fresh. Written by the AFIR parser.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.price_currency IS 'energyRate.applicableCurrency[0]. EUR everywhere observed; stored because a price without its currency is a number, and Eco-Movement is a Dutch publisher aggregating across borders.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.price_last_updated IS 'The energyRate''s own lastUpdated, which is NOT the publication time and is often years older: one sampled Eco-Movement rate was last updated 2022-12-08. A price with no freshness beside it would be presented as current when it is four years old.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.price_per_kwh IS 'The ad-hoc (`ratePolicy: adHoc`) `pricePerKWh` energyPrice, in price_currency per kWh. **NET, not gross**: taxIncluded was false on all 64 591 Eco-Movement and all 12 149 EnBW price entries of 2026-09-19, with taxRate 19 — so a consumer-facing number is this × 1.19 and price_tax_included is the column that says so per row rather than per assumption. Rounded to mills by numeric(6,3) (the house money type): EnBW publishes up to eight decimals, e.g. 0.66386555, which is a net price divided out of a gross tariff and not a precision anyone charges at.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.price_tax_included IS 'Whether price_per_kwh already contains VAT, per row and per the payload. Observed false everywhere so far; kept as a column because a publisher that switches to gross without telling anyone would otherwise silently inflate every price we show by 19 %.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.price_tax_rate IS 'The energyPrice''s taxRate (19 everywhere observed), i.e. the VAT to add when price_tax_included is false. Stored beside the price rather than assumed, because the reduced rate and a future change are both the publisher''s to declare.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.provider_point_id IS 'The publisher''s own point id (aegiElectricChargingPoint.idG) kept verbatim even when it is not the EVSE id: it is what the surrogate key is built from, and it is the only handle a provider''s support desk will recognise. Eco-Movement sends provider-local strings and bare UUIDs here; EnBW sends the EVSE id.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.provider_updated_at IS 'The publisher''s OWN timestamp for the payload this row was last seen in, never ours. NULL when the provider publishes none (Tankerkönig) — a substituted "now" would make a stale copy look fresh. A SIGHTING column like source_run_id: assigned on every sighting, changed or not. The publication lag is therefore `last_seen_at - provider_updated_at` (how long after the publisher published we had the payload) and NEVER `updated_at - provider_updated_at`, which is negative for every row that has not changed since the publisher''s last release.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.site_id IS 'The parent site, with source_feed the other half of the foreign key. A point whose site was skipped (implausible coordinate, no id) is skipped with it — the FK makes that structural rather than a convention.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.source_feed IS 'Part of the primary key, like charging_sites.source_feed, and for the same reason: one socket published by an aggregator and by its operator is two rows under two licences. It is ALSO the author in the sense of the provenance convention: the key of the feed in the feed registry. Never a subscription id.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.source_publisher IS 'Who PUBLISHED the record, as the payload names them: the DATEX II publicationCreator / situation publisher, the AFIR publicationCreator, or the register''s publisher where the payload names none (Tankerkönig: the source register decides the string). Attribution is carried per feed today; this is what lets one tool result name the actual publisher of the one record it is reading aloud.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.source_run_id IS 'The cycle that LAST SAW this row: `feed_runs.id` for a Mobilithek feed, `ingest_runs.id` for the Autobahn and Tankerkönig workers — which one is a property of this table. A snapshot upsert writes it on every sighting, changed or not, so it answers "which cycle had this row in its payload", NOT "which cycle last changed it" — that is `updated_at`, beside it. No foreign key and no index by design. NULL on rows written before the provenance migration.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.source_version IS 'The publication time of the release this row came from, as on charging_sites.source_version. NOT versionG.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.station_id IS 'energyInfrastructureStation.idG — the physical station between the site and the point. Kept as a column rather than a table because no read asks about a station on its own, and it is what makes the surrogate point key unambiguous. Eco-Movement publishes exactly one station per site (17 748 of 17 748); EnBW publishes 1 to 26 (5 286 over 2 421 sites).';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_point_status.confidentiality IS 'The AFIR status publication''s headerInformation.confidentiality; NULL when absent. Read paths return only ''noRestriction'' or NULL.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_point_status.evse_id IS 'reference.idG of the charging point, verbatim and unnormalised. Tesla sends EVSE ids (DE*TSL*E0TO95C); VW sends UUIDs. Normalising them would invent a scheme neither publisher uses, and the join to master data is by the id the master data itself carries: charging_points.evse_id (0009), which is the ONLY column that crosses the two feed families — charging_points is keyed (source_feed, point_key), and a point_key is the EVSE id only where the publisher sent one, so joining on the key would match nothing.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_point_status.exchange_protocol IS 'exchangeContext.codedExchangeProtocol of THIS pull: deltaPull or snapshotPull. Read per payload and never inferred from the feed key — afir_vw_dyn is catalogued as dynamic and answers snapshotPull (2026-09-19), and whether a gap is lost data depends on this value.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_point_status.feed_ts IS 'aegiEnergyInfrastructureStatusPublication.publicationTime, verbatim. The VW publication of 2026-09-19 carried 2026-08-20T07:37:50Z here while calling itself online, which is precisely why this is stored rather than assumed to equal observed_at.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_point_status.known_point IS 'Did charging_points (0009) hold this evse_id when the row was written? TRUE/FALSE = checked; NULL = there was nothing to check against (the master-data table does not exist on this database, or the loader could not consult it). Deliberately NOT a foreign key: master data is a daily dump and availability is a 60 s feed, so an id the dump has not caught up with is ORDINARY. An unknown id is stored, counted and visible here; it is never dropped, because the status of a point we cannot describe yet is still the fact a driver asked about.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_point_status.last_updated IS 'The charging point''s own lastUpdated, with its offset honoured (a constructed fixture pins +02:00). NULL means the publisher sent none or sent something that is not a timestamp: the status is still the publisher''s truth and is kept, but such a row cannot take part in the duplicate test, so a publisher with a broken clock produces one row per pull.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_point_status.observed_at IS 'When we pulled the payload, not when the publisher changed anything — that is last_updated. The partition key, and the only column a reader may use to line a row up against feed_runs.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_point_status.raw IS 'The aegiElectricChargingPointStatus record whole — unknown fields included — plus its station and site records with their child arrays removed (those children are the sibling rows of this same tick, and keeping them would store an n-point delta n² times). The envelope is not duplicated here: its load-bearing facts are feed_ts and exchange_protocol, and the whole document is in the raw archive under data/raw/.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_point_status.source_feed IS 'The registry feed key that wrote the row. Attribution travels with the row: the licence and the attribution string are looked up from it, and it is also how the same physical point reached us through two publishers if that ever happens.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_point_status.source_run_id IS 'The cycle that appended this tick: `feed_runs.id` for the Mobilithek feeds (transit_delays, road_event_ticks, charging_point_status), `ingest_runs.id` for the Autobahn and Tankerkönig sweeps (autobahn_events, fuel_prices). Ticks are append-only, so it is written once and never updated. NULL on partitions written before the provenance migration.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_points.updated_at IS 'When this row last actually CHANGED — not when it was last seen. Maintained by the writers, in the same statement that decides a row moved; a payload that repeats a record unchanged moves last_seen_at and leaves this alone.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_sites.access_private IS 'The site''s name says it is not for the public (0185): "private" as a word, separated by a space, a dash, an underscore or a digit (not when it names an institution: "Private Hochschule", "Private Klinik"), the compounds Privatparkplatz, Privatgrundstück, Privatgelände and Privateinfahrt (not Privathotel, Privatbrauerei, Privatklinik), "nicht/kein öffentlich", "not public", "nur für Mitarbeiter/Bewohner/Anwohner/Mieter". Generated from the name, never written by a loader; charging_sites_live leaves these rows out.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_sites.city IS 'address.city, taken from the German entry of the localised text where there is one. Eco-Movement publishes lang=''en'' at the publication level but German city names inside it.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_sites.closed_at IS 'When the site stopped appearing in this feed. NULL = still published. Scoped per source_feed by the loader: one feed must never withdraw another feed''s sites. The PROVIDER''s fact, kept forever — our own retraction is deleted_at.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_sites.confidentiality IS 'The AFIR (DATEX II v3) publication''s headerInformation.confidentiality; NULL when absent or for the Autobahn GmbH writer. Read paths return only ''noRestriction'' or NULL.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_sites.country_code IS 'address.countryCode. ''DE'' on every one of the 17 748 Eco-Movement and 2 421 EnBW sites of 2026-09-19 — kept because Eco-Movement is a Dutch aggregator whose German extract is a filter that could change, and a non-DE row is then a fact rather than a mystery.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_sites.created_at IS 'When WE first wrote this row. Never moves again — a row that is closed and reappears keeps it. Backfilled from first_seen_at by the provenance migration.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_sites.deleted_at IS 'WE retracted this row (a duplicate folded, a parse judged wrong, a provider retraction, a manual correction) — it is hidden from every read and hard-deleted by the retention job once it is older than the retention window for retracted rows (at least 30 days). NOT closed_at, which says the PROVIDER stopped publishing the record and is kept forever; a row may carry both and neither implies the other.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_sites.deleted_reason IS 'Why this row was retracted, in one short human sentence — "duplicate folded", "parse error, re-loaded", "provider retraction". Required by convention whenever deleted_at is set: a retraction with no reason is indistinguishable from an accident, and after the purge window nothing is left to ask.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_sites.deleted_run_id IS 'The ingest_runs.id of the run that retracted the row, or NULL when a human did it by hand. Deliberately NOT a foreign key: an operator action has no run, and the ledger has its own (planned) retention, so a dangling id must stay readable rather than block the write.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_sites.first_seen_at IS 'The first cycle this row appeared in a payload. SEEN, not created: `created_at` is when we wrote it. Never updated.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_sites.geom IS 'geography, not geometry, like parking_sites: distances here are asked in metres (ST_DWithin). From locationReference.locAreaLocation or .locPointLocation coordinatesForDisplay — BOTH must be tried, because the two feeds put it under different ones. NOT NULL: a charging site without a location cannot answer any question we ask, so a record without one is skipped at parse time and named in the log.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_sites.house_key IS 'charging_house_key(street, house_number), stored: the first run of digits of the house number, or of the street line. NULL when neither has a digit. Generated; never written by a loader.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_sites.house_number IS 'The addressLine[] entry of type ''houseNumber''. Often absent even where the street is present, which is why it is separate and nullable rather than concatenated into street.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_sites.last_seen_at IS 'The most recent cycle this row appeared in a payload, and the diff-and-close presence marker. SEEN, not CHANGED: a publisher that re-sends an identical record moves this and leaves `updated_at` where it was.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_sites.name IS 'NULL for the whole EnBW feed: 0 of 2 421 EnBW site records carry a `name` element at all (Eco-Movement carries one on all 17 748). The address columns are the only identifying text an EnBW site has, which is why they are columns and not payload.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_sites.operator IS 'The operator''s name from operator.afacAnOrganisation.name (''EnBW mobility+ AG & Co. KG'', ''ChargePoint''), as published and not normalised. Denormalised onto the point as well, because "every point of operator X" must not need a join to be indexable. 449 distinct values in Eco-Movement, 1 in EnBW.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_sites.operator_id IS 'The operator''s short code from externalIdentifier[] where typeOfIdentifier.extendedValueG = ''operatorId'' (''EBW'', ''WLI''). It is the middle field of an EVSE id, so it is the cheapest cross-check that a point''s id belongs to the operator the site claims.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_sites.operator_tokens IS 'charging_operator_tokens(operator), stored: the comparable words of the operator name, computed once at write because the site-twin rule compares it per pair of sites and the function costs ~16 µs a call. Generated; never written by a loader.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_sites.point_count IS 'How many charging points this publisher says the site has, where it publishes a number and no per-point detail. The Autobahn GmbH feed is that case: it carries no EVSE id, connector type, power or price, so writing charging_points rows from it would mean inventing ids that can never join to the AFIR master data (0009''s design rests on the EVSE id being the join column). NULL for the AFIR feeds, where the authoritative count is the number of charging_points rows.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_sites.postcode IS 'address.postcode, verbatim. Text and not an integer: German postcodes have a significant leading zero (01067 Dresden) that an integer column would eat.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_sites.provider_updated_at IS 'The publisher''s OWN timestamp for the payload this row was last seen in, never ours. NULL when the provider publishes none (Tankerkönig) — a substituted "now" would make a stale copy look fresh. A SIGHTING column like source_run_id: assigned on every sighting, changed or not. The publication lag is therefore `last_seen_at - provider_updated_at` (how long after the publisher published we had the payload) and NEVER `updated_at - provider_updated_at`, which is negative for every row that has not changed since the publisher''s last release.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_sites.road IS 'The road the site serves, as the SOURCE spells it (''A9''). Set by the Autobahn GmbH worker, which asks the API one road at a time and therefore knows the answer rather than inferring it; NULL for both AFIR feeds, whose publishers never say. Never derived from the geometry: a charging site 200 m from a motorway is not on it.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_sites.site_id IS 'energyInfrastructureSite.idG. NOT unique within one payload: 236 Eco-Movement ids appeared 2–5 times in the pull of 2026-09-19 (540 records in excess of 17 208 distinct ids), and (site_id, station_id) repeats the same 540 times. Repeated records are the same site published more than once and are folded into this row, FIRST record winning (the upsert guard is last_seen_at < excluded.last_seen_at, which is false for a second copy inside one cycle). Their POINTS are not folded: 2026-09-19 has a pair of Spirii-52166 records carrying the same nine sockets under two different EVSE-id spellings (DE*CKE*EXD038505*01*1 and DE*CKE*EXD03850501*1), both of them published ids a status message may quote, so both are kept.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_sites.source_feed IS 'Part of the primary key, not provenance decoration: the same physical site legitimately exists twice, once per publisher. Correlating the two is a query over charging_points.evse_id, never a row. It is ALSO the author in the sense of the provenance convention — the key of the feed in the feed registry, which the status dashboard groups by and the attribution line is read from. Never a subscription id.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_sites.source_publisher IS 'Who PUBLISHED the record, as the payload names them: the DATEX II publicationCreator / situation publisher, the AFIR publicationCreator, or the register''s publisher where the payload names none (Tankerkönig: the source register decides the string). Attribution is carried per feed today; this is what lets one tool result name the actual publisher of the one record it is reading aloud.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_sites.source_run_id IS 'The cycle that LAST SAW this row: `feed_runs.id` for a Mobilithek feed, `ingest_runs.id` for the Autobahn and Tankerkönig workers — which one is a property of this table. A snapshot upsert writes it on every sighting, changed or not, so it answers "which cycle had this row in its payload", NOT "which cycle last changed it" — that is `updated_at`, beside it. No foreign key and no index by design. NULL on rows written before the provenance migration.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_sites.source_version IS 'The publication''s publicationTime (Eco-Movement 2026-09-19T03:40:19Z, EnBW 2026-09-19T00:00:00.012+02:00), i.e. which nightly release this row came from. NOT energyInfrastructureSite.versionG: Eco-Movement sends the constant string "1" for every site in 99.5 MB, so versionG cannot detect a change.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_sites.street IS 'From the addressLine[] entry whose type.value is ''street''. Both feeds publish the address as an ORDERED LIST of typed lines rather than fields, so these four columns are a projection of it and the order attribute is what distinguishes street from house number.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_sites.street_key IS 'charging_street_key(street), stored: the street reduced to its letters, for the site-twin rule''s address arm. Empty string for no street. Generated; never written by a loader.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.charging_sites.updated_at IS 'When this row last actually CHANGED — not when it was last seen. Maintained by the writers, in the same statement that decides a row moved; a payload that repeats a record unchanged moves last_seen_at and leaves this alone.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.db_facilities.extras IS 'Every field of the payload we did not model, kept verbatim (unknown fields are kept, never dropped). A new FaSta field therefore arrives in the database on the first sweep after it is published, and schema drift is visible rather than silent.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.db_facilities.last_seen_at IS 'The last sweep that still reported this facility. It is the page''s freshness clock: max(last_seen_at) answers "when did we last hear from FaSta at all", which is how a reader tells "nothing is broken" apart from "the worker has stopped".';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.db_facilities.resume_at IS 'FaSta `operationalResumeDate`: when the operator expects the equipment back. Published on a small minority (18 of 3 752 on 2026-09-20), so NULL is the normal case and means "the operator said nothing", never "it is not coming back".';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.db_facilities.state IS 'active | inactive | unknown, folded from FaSta''s ACTIVE/INACTIVE/UNKNOWN. `unknown` is the PUBLISHER''s third value — DB has equipment here whose status it cannot currently report — and is deliberately not folded into either of the other two: a page that renders "we do not know" as "it works" has told a wheelchair user the opposite of the useful thing. An unrecognised string also lands here, never on `active`.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.db_facilities.state_since IS 'When WE first observed the current state, written by the same statement that moves `state` so the two cannot drift. It is our observation and not the publisher''s: FaSta sends no state timestamp, so this is the earliest we can honestly claim, and a facility broken before our first sweep reads as broken since that sweep.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.db_facility_ticks.previous_state IS 'What we believed immediately before this tick, or NULL the first time we ever saw this equipment. It is here so that "what changed today" is one indexed scan of one partition rather than a self-join across a growing archive.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.db_facility_ticks.state IS 'The state as of this tick, plus one value the snapshot cannot hold: `gone`, written when a facility we saw in the previous sweep is absent from this one. Without it a decommissioned lift would keep its last state in the snapshot for ever and the page would report a problem that no longer exists.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.db_station_numbers.category IS 'DB StaDa `category` (Bahnhofskategorie), 1 = the largest stations to 7, as the record published it. Ranks an ambiguous station name, largest first. NULL until the first StaDa load after migration 0136.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.db_station_numbers.eva_no IS 'The Timetables identifier, and the key: it is what `gazetteer_places.external_id` holds for a db_stada row, so a tool that has resolved a station name already has it. Only the station''s main EVA (StaDa `isMain`) is written — the per-Bahnhofsteil numbers are the same building and would be several rows claiming one station number.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.db_station_numbers.municipality_code IS 'DB StaDa `municipalityCode`: the station''s Gemeinde as an eight-digit AGS, the key of the BKG Gemeinde rows of gazetteer_places (whose population breaks a tie in category). NULL until the first StaDa load after migration 0136.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.db_station_numbers.part_eva_nos IS 'The station''s OTHER EVA numbers (StaDa evaNumbers with isMain false), in the order StaDa lists them — one per Bahnhofsteil, e.g. Berlin Hbf 8011160 carries {8089021,8098160}. DB Timetables files each part''s trains under its own number, so a departure board for the station asks eva_no and every number here. Empty for a single-part station and for a row written before migration 0059 until the next gazetteer load.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.db_station_numbers.source_id IS 'The register key carrying the licence of this pairing: db_stada, CC BY 4.0. Both numbers are DB''s, published in one record; a result built on this crosswalk owes the db_stada attribution line as well as the line of whatever answered the question itself.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.db_station_numbers.station_no IS 'The StaDa `number` (Bahnhofsnummer), which is the path parameter of FaSta''s /stations/{stationnumber}. Stored as text because it is an identifier: leading zeros, if a publisher ever emits them, are part of it, and nothing here does arithmetic on it.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.delfi_service_alerts.kreise IS '5-digit AGS Kreis keys: read off the DHID stop ids (placed_by = stop), or the Kreise the named routes stop in (placed_by = route, 0083); empty for a network-placed or unplaced alert.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.delfi_service_alerts.lands IS 'Where the alert is placed, as 2-digit Land codes: from the AGS inside each DHID stop id (placed_by = stop); else from the Kreise the named routes stop in, per the static timetable (gtfs_route_kreise, placed_by = route, 0083); else from the network a route id names (de:VBN → 03, 04; placed_by = network). A stop- or route-placed alert answers Land and Kreis questions; a network-placed one answers a Land question and never a Kreis question.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.delfi_siri_et_calls.source_feed IS 'The registry key of the Mobilithek feed whose pull wrote this row: delfi_siri_et (Bahndaten) or one of the regional DELFI-Realtime SIRI ET feeds. A reader trusts a row only while that feed is alive.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.delfi_siri_et_calls.stop_ref IS 'StopPointRef as published. Rail calls carry the 7-digit EVA number (58 591 of 89 526 calls on 2026-10-05), trams and some private railways a DHID (de:<AGS>:…); nothing is rewritten.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.delfi_siri_et_calls.vehicle_mode IS 'SIRI VehicleMode of the journey as published (lower-cased, from the SIRI value list), or null when the journey carried none.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.deployments.app_digest IS 'The app image this deploy ran, by registry digest (sha256:…). NULL for a deploy from before the column existed and for a compose deploy, which builds on the host.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.deployments.deploy_duration_s IS 'Wall-clock seconds from the start of the deploy to this insert. A deploy that suddenly takes four times as long is a signal before it is an outage (a slow migration, a full disk, a rebuild that stopped hitting the cache).';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.deployments.deployed_at IS 'When the deploy finished its migrate step, UTC, from the deploying machine''s clock (clock_timestamp). Rendered Europe/Berlin with the offset; the age of the newest row is the "running since" of the release.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.deployments.git_ref IS 'The tag name when HEAD was tagged (e.g. v0.0.5), else the branch name. Convenience for a human; never trusted as identity — see git_sha. Free text from the deploying machine, so the dashboard masks it like any other value it did not generate.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.deployments.git_sha IS 'The full commit sha that was built and shipped. THE identity of what is running: tags move, branches move, versions are typed by hand, a sha is a fact. Compared against worker_boots.git_sha per process, and a process whose sha differs is running the previous image.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.deployments.id IS 'Surrogate key. Identity rather than a natural key because two deploys of the same commit are two events, and "redeployed the same sha after a rollback" is exactly the sequence an operator needs to see.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.deployments.image_built_at IS 'When the container image this deploy started was built. A deploy that "changed nothing" because the image was a cached rebuild is otherwise invisible: same sha, same version, old bytes.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.deployments.migration_head IS 'max(schema_migrations.version) after the migrate step of this deploy. A release whose code expects a column the head does not have is the failure mode this column exists to make visible: the dashboard shows it beside the head the database reports NOW, and the two disagreeing means somebody applied or rolled back migrations outside a deploy.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.deployments.note IS 'Optional free text from the deploy, at most 200 characters: "rollback to previous image", "hotfix, no migration". Never a command line, never a credential — the dashboard masks 19-digit runs and routable IP literals in it before rendering, and that mask is the second line of defence, not the first.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.deployments.previous_git_sha IS 'The sha this deploy replaced, as the deploy script read it before rsync. The first half of "what changed when it broke" — the other half is the row before this one. NULL on the first deploy of a host.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.deployments.translator_digest IS 'The translator image this deploy ran, by registry digest. NULL for a deploy from before the column existed and for a compose deploy.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.deployments.version IS 'The root package.json version at deploy time (e.g. 0.0.5). What a human calls the release. Not derived from the tag: a deploy from an untagged commit still has a version, and the difference between the two is itself worth seeing.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_catalog.attribution IS 'The attribution string that must be shown with anything derived from this feed, verbatim from the source register. NULL means "no approved string exists yet" — for the two BAB offers whose terms are unknown the register calls its own draft "must not be emitted", and a NULL is how that stays true in the database as well.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_catalog.cadence_s IS 'The cycle this feed is actually polled at ON THIS HOST: the registry default unless the host''s configuration overrides it. The number a freshness alarm multiplies, so it is the effective one and not the default.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_catalog.closed_at IS 'Set when the feed is no longer in the writing worker''s registry, and NULLed again if it comes back. The row is never deleted: feed_runs rows still reference the key and an attribution that vanished would make the history unattributable. NULL = current.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_catalog.display_name IS 'The feed title as the publisher writes it (registry `title`), or a plain description of a provider API. Rendered as the feed name; not an id and not stable enough to key anything on.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_catalog.enabled IS 'Whether this feed is switched on for this host: the Mobilithek worker''s feed allow-list, or "this worker is running" for a provider row. A registry feed that is off still gets a row — a reader needs to know it exists and is silent by choice, which is a different colour on the dashboard from silent by fault.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_catalog.feed_key IS 'The registry key for a Mobilithek feed (feed_runs.feed_key — join it), or the provider id for a worker that has no registry (ingest_runs.provider: autobahn, tankerkoenig). One namespace, so one row describes whatever the ledgers call the source.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_catalog.format IS 'What the bytes are: xml (DATEX II), json (AFIR, provider APIs) or protobuf (GTFS-RT). Registry `format`; it names the archived raw file''s extension too.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_catalog.licence_id IS 'The register id — a row of the source register, and the same value that travels on data rows as `source` (e.g. delfi_rt_tu, mobilithek_roadworks_bast, autobahn). A feed whose licence has never been READ carries a *_pending id instead, and that is deliberate: pending is not a licence, and the gap has to stay visible.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_catalog.licence_label IS 'The licence''s short name, verbatim from the register: CC BY 4.0, CC BY-SA 4.0, GeoNutzV, Datenlizenz Deutschland – Zero – Version 2.0, … For a *_pending id it says so in words. Not the licence text: the terms live in the register, which is where they are read.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_catalog.listed_at IS 'When this database first listed the feed; the boot publish never rewrites it (updated_at moves on every boot). The feed-catalogue gate measures a feed that never ran from it.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_catalog.min_interval_s IS 'The politeness FLOOR for this feed: the smallest cycle we allow ourselves, from the registry row or the provider''s limiter. An override below it is refused at boot, never clamped. Lowering a floor is a provider-terms decision, not a tuning knob.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_catalog.offer_id IS 'Mobilithek publicationId — the PUBLIC catalogue id printed on mobilithek.info/offers/<id>: an optional minus sign (0143: the older direct offers are numbered negative) and 1 to 19 digits (0120). NULL for a provider with no Mobilithek offer. Never a Subscription-ID: that is read from the environment by the NAME in subscription_var and never enters the registry.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_catalog.offer_url IS 'Where a human confirms the source: the Mobilithek offer page where there is one, else the provider''s own documented endpoint or licence page. Safe to render as a link — it carries no credentials.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_catalog.profile IS 'Which loader reads this payload (registry `profile`: roadworks, parking, gtfs_rt_tu). NULL means the worker has no parser for it yet and refuses to enable it — or, for autobahn/tankerkoenig, that the worker is not registry-driven at all.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_catalog.provider IS 'Which worker fetches this, in the limiters'' vocabulary: mobilithek, autobahn, tankerkoenig. This is the RATE-LIMIT and process owner, not the publisher — the publisher is in display_name and the licence row. It is also what scopes a boot publish: a worker closes rows of its OWN provider only, so the Mobilithek worker cannot close the Autobahn row.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_catalog.published_by IS 'The catalogue list that last published this row, e.g. lorry-routes, hamburg. The boot publish closes only its own list''s rows that it no longer lists; NULL = not published since the column was added, closed by no one.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_catalog.registry_version IS 'The version of the @viafrei/ingest package that wrote this row. Which code''s idea of the catalogue you are looking at, so a stale row after a partial deploy is visible rather than merely wrong.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_catalog.subscription_var IS 'The NAME of the environment variable holding this feed''s Mobilithek Subscription-ID — never the 19-digit value, which is kept outside the database and is redacted out of every log line. NULL for a direct download and for a provider with no subscription. The check constraint allows upper-case identifiers only, so the secret cannot be written here by accident.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_catalog.updated_at IS 'Last boot that published this row, as clock_timestamp() — the moment the statement wrote it, not the transaction''s start, so two writes in one transaction are distinguishable. Moves on every boot even when nothing else changed: that is the signal that the worker started and agreed with the registry.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_runs.body_sha256 IS 'sha256 (lowercase hex) of the DECODED body, computed once in the client on the bytes it already held — never by re-reading `raw_path`. Set on `ok` only: a 304 carries no body, and the hash of the body we still hold is on the previous `ok` row. Two consecutive `ok` rows with the same hash mean the publisher answered 200 with an identical file, which is the one thing a 304 cannot tell us and a delta feed does routinely.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_runs.budget_hit IS 'Set when the admission gate refused this run''s request: the budget shape it hit, or unavailable when the gate could not be read. No request was made: the held rows stay and answer, the feed stays due, and nothing counts it as a check or a strike.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_runs.duration_ms IS 'How long the PROVIDER took to answer this cycle''s one conditional GET, as the client measured it — not the cycle''s wall time, which is `finished_at - started_at` and includes parsing and loading. NULL when no request was made at all (held, skipped, or a feed with no resolvable address).';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_runs.etag IS 'The ETag the access point returned. Load-bearing, not diagnostics: the next pull of this feed sends it back as If-None-Match, and the age of the OLDEST consecutive row carrying it is how the alarms tell "the publisher stopped" from "we stopped polling" — a long run of `unchanged` rows with one ETag is health, not silence.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_runs.exchange_protocol IS 'The codedExchangeProtocol the loaded payload stated (deltaPull, snapshotPull, ...), the label its rows carry in charging_point_status.exchange_protocol; NULL when the cycle loaded nothing or the profile has no such label.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_runs.id IS 'The cycle id, and the value every row this cycle writes carries in `source_run_id`. `generated by default as identity` rather than `always`: the ledger row is written once, AFTER the loaders, so the worker takes the next value from the sequence at the start of the cycle and inserts the row with it. Never set by hand outside that reservation — the ledger write falls back to `nextval` when no id was reserved.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_runs.items_changed IS 'The SUM of items_inserted + items_updated + items_closed, kept as a column because the volume alarm and `feed:health` read it and because rows written before schema 13 have no split. A reader that needs to know WHICH of the three moved must read the split; a reader that only needs "did anything happen" keeps working unchanged.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_runs.items_closed IS 'Rows this cycle closed (`closed_at`) because the snapshot stopped carrying them. Never a DELETE: absence is an end date, and the row keeps everything we knew about it. 0, not NULL, on a feed that has nothing to close (append-only ticks).';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_runs.items_inserted IS 'Of `items_seen`, the rows that were NEW to us. NULL on every path that loaded nothing. For an append-only tick feed (gtfs_rt_tu) every written row is an insert, so this equals `items_changed`.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_runs.items_seen IS 'How many items the payload carried, on the cycles that loaded one — only `ok` rows have it, because `unchanged` loaded nothing by design. The volume-drop alarm compares the newest `ok` run against the trailing median of the previous ones; counting `unchanged` cycles here would drag every median to zero.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_runs.items_updated IS 'Of `items_seen`, the rows we already held and that MOVED (a new DATEX II `version`, a changed parking attribute). A row the payload repeated unchanged is counted in neither `items_inserted` nor `items_updated` — that is the difference between `items_seen` and the split. What counts as MOVED is the loader''s change comparison, which is not always about content: for a feed whose `source_version` is the publication timestamp — both AFIR static feeds, where it is the DATEX II envelope''s publicationTime — the version differs on every release, so a new release makes EVERY retained row an update and `items_updated` approaches `items_seen`. Read it as "rows this release re-stated", not "rows whose content differs from yesterday''s".';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_runs.jumped IS 'True when this cycle asked WITHOUT validators because of the jump rule and was answered with a publication other than the one its validators named: the publications in between were skipped. On a change-only feed that is a gap (0010), which the charging judge reads.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_runs.last_modified IS 'The Last-Modified header verbatim. Load-bearing, not diagnostics: the next pull sends it back as If-Modified-Since.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_runs.next_due_at IS 'When this feed''s next DELIVERY is expected, written by the scheduler at the end of the run from the SAME cadence the cycle planner honours: `started_at` + the feed''s effective interval on ok/unchanged/degraded, and on error the later of that and the provider''s back-off hold. On `held` it is the moment the hold lifts. NULL means "no planned time": a `skipped` feed is already overdue and takes the next free request, and a `pending` feed has no ACTIVE subscription, so a timestamp there would promise data nobody can deliver. It is a PLAN, not a promise — a restart, a back-off or a longer queue moves it.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_runs.note IS 'One line of human commentary about this cycle, written from facts the worker already had: "identical body (same sha256 as the previous run) …", "first body recorded for this feed …", "closed N absent …". A SUMMARY, capped at 200 characters by the writer — NEVER a per-record log (logging each inserted record is too slow at this volume). Per-record detail stays at debug level in the logs.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_runs.raw_path IS 'Where the archived body was written. A `.xml.gz` file holds the gzip bytes as received, so a reader must not assume plain XML.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_runs.reader_epoch IS 'The generation of the reader that wrote this row. Load-bearing: the worker sends a stored etag/last_modified back only from a row of the current generation, so the first cycle after a reader starts storing something new (0064: the DATEX II marking) is a 200 that rewrites every row, never a 304 that loads nothing. NULL on every row written before 0064, and on any row the previous release writes.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_runs.status IS 'ok | unchanged (304, zero bytes) | pending (subscription not ACTIVE) | held (rate limiter held the cycle back) | skipped (due, but the cycle''s one request went to another feed) | degraded (partial) | error. A feed that is NOT due writes no row at all. Corrects the 0006 comment, which said skipped = "not due".';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_runs.wire_bytes IS 'Bytes as they crossed the network, i.e. compressed where the server compressed. `bytes` is the DECODED size, and the pair is the whole argument for asking for gzip: the BASt roadworks feed is 104 679 564 B decoded and 6 527 746 B on the wire (measured 2026-09-18). NULL when no body arrived (304, pending, held, skipped, or a request that never reached a server).';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feed_runs.worker_version IS 'Which code wrote this row: the ingest worker''s version plus, when the image was built from a checkout, `+<git sha>` (a build argument baked into the image). It is how a row can be attributed to a release when two versions of the worker have touched the same feed in one day.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feeds.active IS 'This is "the key holds a timetable", not "this is the live version": there is one stable feeds row per provider and nothing to cut over to, so the loader sets it on the first load that writes stop_times. The partial unique index feeds_one_active_per_provider still holds one active row per provider.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feeds.created_at IS 'When WE first wrote this row. Never moves again — a row that is closed and reappears keeps it. Backfilled from first_seen_at by the provenance migration.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feeds.extras IS 'The feed''s own metadata, and it has a convention rather than being empty: release_id (the provider''s release id, text), release_valid_from / release_valid_to (date, when the operator passes them), rows (per-table counts of the last load), window ([first, last] service date kept), loaded_run_id (the ingest_runs.id of that load, which is also what it stamped on its trips) and loaded_at (ISO instant, UTC). Written by the GTFS load; a key nothing in that list names is nobody''s.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feeds.feed_version IS 'The STABLE key of the feed, not the release: "delfi-stable". The provider''s own release id is extras->>''release_id''. It used to be the release date and was the leading column of every static-GTFS index, which cost 15.7 percent of that family''s disk and doubled the tables on every weekly swap. (provider, feed_version) stays unique so a second, deliberately-created key for one provider is still possible.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feeds.gtfs_feed_key IS 'The smallint every static-GTFS row of this feed carries instead of a text feed_version: stops, routes, trips, stop_times and gtfs_service_dates are keyed by it. Unique on its own and STABLE for the life of the feed — one row per provider, assigned here (delfi_static = 1) and by whichever migration first loads the next provider. Never re-used: a retired key''s rows are an operator''s business, because no loader touches a key it was not asked to load.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feeds.provider_updated_at IS 'The publisher''s OWN timestamp for the payload this row was last seen in, never ours. NULL when the provider publishes none (Tankerkönig) — a substituted "now" would make a stale copy look fresh. A SIGHTING column like source_run_id: assigned on every sighting, changed or not. The publication lag is therefore `last_seen_at - provider_updated_at` (how long after the publisher published we had the payload) and NEVER `updated_at - provider_updated_at`, which is negative for every row that has not changed since the publisher''s last release.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feeds.source_feed IS 'THE AUTHOR: the registry key of the feed this row came from (the feed registry''s key for a Mobilithek feed, the provider name for a worker that has no registry row). Never a subscription id — those are secrets and are kept outside the database. What the status dashboard''s "source" column and the per-row attribution line are read from.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feeds.source_publisher IS 'Who PUBLISHED the record, as the payload names them: the DATEX II publicationCreator / situation publisher, the AFIR publicationCreator, or the register''s publisher where the payload names none (Tankerkönig: the source register decides the string). Attribution is carried per feed today; this is what lets one tool result name the actual publisher of the one record it is reading aloud.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feeds.source_run_id IS 'The cycle that LAST SAW this row: `feed_runs.id` for a Mobilithek feed, `ingest_runs.id` for the Autobahn and Tankerkönig workers — which one is a property of this table. A snapshot upsert writes it on every sighting, changed or not, so it answers "which cycle had this row in its payload", NOT "which cycle last changed it" — that is `updated_at`, beside it. No foreign key and no index by design. NULL on rows written before the provenance migration.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.feeds.updated_at IS 'When this row last actually CHANGED — not when it was last seen. Maintained by the writers, in the same statement that decides a row moved; a payload that repeats a record unchanged moves last_seen_at and leaves this alone.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_price_latest.created_at IS 'When WE first wrote this row. Never moves again — a row that is closed and reappears keeps it. Backfilled from first_seen_at by the provenance migration.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_price_latest.deleted_at IS 'WE retracted this row (a duplicate folded, a parse judged wrong, a provider retraction, a manual correction) — it is hidden from every read and hard-deleted by the retention job once it is older than the retention window for retracted rows (at least 30 days). NOT closed_at, which says the PROVIDER stopped publishing the record and is kept forever; a row may carry both and neither implies the other.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_price_latest.deleted_reason IS 'Why this row was retracted, in one short human sentence — "duplicate folded", "parse error, re-loaded", "provider retraction". Required by convention whenever deleted_at is set: a retraction with no reason is indistinguishable from an accident, and after the purge window nothing is left to ask.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_price_latest.deleted_run_id IS 'The ingest_runs.id of the run that retracted the row, or NULL when a human did it by hand. Deliberately NOT a foreign key: an operator action has no run, and the ledger has its own (planned) retention, so a dangling id must stay readable rather than block the write.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_price_latest.provider_updated_at IS 'The publisher''s OWN timestamp for the payload this row was last seen in, never ours. NULL when the provider publishes none (Tankerkönig) — a substituted "now" would make a stale copy look fresh. A SIGHTING column like source_run_id: assigned on every sighting, changed or not. The publication lag is therefore `last_seen_at - provider_updated_at` (how long after the publisher published we had the payload) and NEVER `updated_at - provider_updated_at`, which is negative for every row that has not changed since the publisher''s last release.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_price_latest.source_feed IS 'THE AUTHOR: the registry key of the feed this row came from (the feed registry''s key for a Mobilithek feed, the provider name for a worker that has no registry row). Never a subscription id — those are secrets and are kept outside the database. What the status dashboard''s "source" column and the per-row attribution line are read from.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_price_latest.source_publisher IS 'Who PUBLISHED the record, as the payload names them: the DATEX II publicationCreator / situation publisher, the AFIR publicationCreator, or the register''s publisher where the payload names none (Tankerkönig: the source register decides the string). Attribution is carried per feed today; this is what lets one tool result name the actual publisher of the one record it is reading aloud.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_price_latest.source_run_id IS 'The cycle that LAST SAW this row: `feed_runs.id` for a Mobilithek feed, `ingest_runs.id` for the Autobahn and Tankerkönig workers — which one is a property of this table. A snapshot upsert writes it on every sighting, changed or not, so it answers "which cycle had this row in its payload", NOT "which cycle last changed it" — that is `updated_at`, beside it. No foreign key and no index by design. NULL on rows written before the provenance migration.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_price_latest.updated_at IS 'When this row last actually CHANGED — not when it was last seen. Maintained by the writers, in the same statement that decides a row moved; a payload that repeats a record unchanged moves last_seen_at and leaves this alone.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_prices.source_run_id IS 'The cycle that appended this tick: `feed_runs.id` for the Mobilithek feeds (transit_delays, road_event_ticks, charging_point_status), `ingest_runs.id` for the Autobahn and Tankerkönig sweeps (autobahn_events, fuel_prices). Ticks are append-only, so it is written once and never updated. NULL on partitions written before the provenance migration.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_sample_draws.rng_seed IS 'The string the rotation''s PRNG is keyed by. Not a secret and not random at write time: the draw defaults it to its UTC date, so "today''s sample" is a name anybody can re-derive. An unreproducible national index is not a measurement.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_sample_strata.core IS 'The part of this stratum that is held FIXED across draws, so "cheaper than last Tuesday" is a valid statement about the same stations. The rotating remainder is what makes "Germany today" unbiased; it is bounded by the stratum''s target and never accumulates.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_stations.created_at IS 'When WE first wrote this row. Never moves again — a row that is closed and reappears keeps it. Backfilled from first_seen_at by the provenance migration.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_stations.deleted_at IS 'WE retracted this row (a duplicate folded, a parse judged wrong, a provider retraction, a manual correction) — it is hidden from every read and hard-deleted by the retention job once it is older than the retention window for retracted rows (at least 30 days). NOT closed_at, which says the PROVIDER stopped publishing the record and is kept forever; a row may carry both and neither implies the other.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_stations.deleted_reason IS 'Why this row was retracted, in one short human sentence — "duplicate folded", "parse error, re-loaded", "provider retraction". Required by convention whenever deleted_at is set: a retraction with no reason is indistinguishable from an accident, and after the purge window nothing is left to ask.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_stations.deleted_run_id IS 'The ingest_runs.id of the run that retracted the row, or NULL when a human did it by hand. Deliberately NOT a foreign key: an operator action has no run, and the ledger has its own (planned) retention, so a dangling id must stay readable rather than block the write.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_stations.first_seen_at IS 'The first cycle this row appeared in a payload. SEEN, not created: `created_at` is when we wrote it. Never updated.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_stations.land IS 'The two-digit Land of the nearest open BKG municipality, set by trigger when the point is written (0141, fuel_station_land). A derivation from GN250 (dl-de/by-2-0), NULL when no municipality is held.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_stations.last_seen_at IS 'The most recent cycle this row appeared in a payload, and the diff-and-close presence marker. SEEN, not CHANGED: a publisher that re-sends an identical record moves this and leaves `updated_at` where it was.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_stations.overrides IS 'detail.php "overrides": the provider''s free-text opening exceptions, stored exactly as received.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_stations.provider_updated_at IS 'The publisher''s OWN timestamp for the payload this row was last seen in, never ours. NULL when the provider publishes none (Tankerkönig) — a substituted "now" would make a stale copy look fresh. A SIGHTING column like source_run_id: assigned on every sighting, changed or not. The publication lag is therefore `last_seen_at - provider_updated_at` (how long after the publisher published we had the payload) and NEVER `updated_at - provider_updated_at`, which is negative for every row that has not changed since the publisher''s last release.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_stations.source_feed IS 'THE AUTHOR: the registry key of the feed this row came from (the feed registry''s key for a Mobilithek feed, the provider name for a worker that has no registry row). Never a subscription id — those are secrets and are kept outside the database. What the status dashboard''s "source" column and the per-row attribution line are read from.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_stations.source_publisher IS 'Who PUBLISHED the record, as the payload names them: the DATEX II publicationCreator / situation publisher, the AFIR publicationCreator, or the register''s publisher where the payload names none (Tankerkönig: the source register decides the string). Attribution is carried per feed today; this is what lets one tool result name the actual publisher of the one record it is reading aloud.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_stations.source_run_id IS 'The cycle that LAST SAW this row: `feed_runs.id` for a Mobilithek feed, `ingest_runs.id` for the Autobahn and Tankerkönig workers — which one is a property of this table. A snapshot upsert writes it on every sighting, changed or not, so it answers "which cycle had this row in its payload", NOT "which cycle last changed it" — that is `updated_at`, beside it. No foreign key and no index by design. NULL on rows written before the provenance migration.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_stations.town_places IS 'The open BKG municipalities nearest the station (at most five, within 20 km, nearest first) as [{ags, name}], set by trigger when the point is written (0217, fuel_station_town_places). A derivation from GN250 (dl-de/by-2-0); no distance is stored. NULL for a station with no point.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_stations.updated_at IS 'When this row last actually CHANGED — not when it was last seen. Maintained by the writers, in the same statement that decides a row moved; a payload that repeats a record unchanged moves last_seen_at and leaves this alone.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_station_watch.asked_at IS 'When the last user question about this station was asked; NULL = no live question. The scheduler resets priority/tier/cadence to the defaults once it is older than the TTL.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_station_watch.city_ags IS 'The eight-digit AGS of the list city this row serves, NULL for a row that serves none. Set and cleared together with city_tier.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_station_watch.city_tier IS 'The fuel city watch: 1 = Berlin or a state capital; 2 = another Großstadt, ordered by population; NULL = not a city station. Written by the worker''s reconcile from the fixed list in the worker''s code; the due order passes a NULL (village) row over first, then 2, then 1.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_station_watch.detail_attempted_at IS 'When detail.php was last asked about this station, success or not: the refresh queue is ordered by it, nulls first.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_station_watch.detail_errors IS 'Consecutive detail.php strikes — the provider answered, about another station; not its outages. Reset by a success, two deactivate the row with the reason in last_error.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_station_watch.sample_role IS 'core | rotating | explorer | complete. `complete` is the one that is a product claim rather than bookkeeping: every station of that place is watched, so the local cheapest is PROVABLY correct and not estimated. `explorer` is the one slot per stratum spent re-measuring a station the candidacy model has written off, which is what lets a station that changes its pricing policy be found without any new request pattern.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.fuel_station_watch.sample_stratum IS 'The stratum this row was drawn for, or NULL for a row that is not part of a draw — every `reason = ''user''` row, and every row on a host that still carries the earlier sampling grid. Never a substitute for `fuel_sample_strata`: this column says which cell a station is in, that table says how big the cell was supposed to be.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.gazetteer_places.closed_at IS 'Set when a source no longer publishes this row. Never a DELETE: a withdrawn entry still explains an older answer, and a reload that deleted first would leave every tool without a gazetteer for the length of the load.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.gazetteer_places.external_id IS 'The source''s own identifier — GeoNames geonameid, BKG AGS/RS, StaDa EVA number, zHV DHID, the road number for an Autobahn row. With source_id it is the upsert key, which is what makes a reload idempotent: a second run of the same files touches last_seen_at and nothing else.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.gazetteer_places.geom IS 'geography(Point, 4326), NULL for a motorway (a 960 km line has no point; its answer is `road`). Geography and not geometry because distances here are asked in metres — ST_DWithin over geography is metres with no projection choice to get wrong (the same reason as parking_sites.geom, 0006).';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.gazetteer_places.kind IS 'city | district | admin | station | stop | motorway | junction | island. city = a populated place (GeoNames P.*, BKG Gemeinde); district = a Kreis/Landkreis; admin = a Bundesland or other higher administrative unit; station = a railway station (DB StaDa, EVA number); stop = a public-transport stop (DELFI zHV); motorway = an A-road as a whole, geom NULL and road set; junction = an Anschlussstelle/Kreuz/Dreieck named by an Autobahn event; island = an island or land-tied island (GeoNames T.ISL, T.ISLT; migration 0086), a point and no outline. Constrained, because a kind the resolver does not know is a row that can never be returned.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.gazetteer_places.name_norm IS 'The searchable fold of the name, written by the loader''s name fold: lower-case, diacritics folded to the bare letter (ü→u), ß→ss, every run of punctuation to one space, trimmed. NOT a generated column: unaccent() is STABLE, not IMMUTABLE, and an index over a fake-immutable wrapper corrupts when the dictionary changes. Administrative type words are stripped here and kept in `name` ("Fulda, Landkreis" → name_norm "fulda"), which is what lets "Kreis Fulda" and "Fulda" reach the same row.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.gazetteer_places.part_of IS 'AGS of the Gemeinde a settlement lies in when it is not that Gemeinde itself (GeoNames admin4); null otherwise';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.gazetteer_places.part_type IS '''stadtteil'' (GeoNames PPLX) or ''ortsteil'' (another settlement inside a Gemeinde of another name); null when part_of is null';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.gazetteer_places.rank IS 'The default-answer score, one integer on a population scale so that one ORDER BY compares every kind. A city uses its population (0 when unknown), so "Frankfurt" resolves to Frankfurt am Main before Frankfurt (Oder). A Kreis is population/4 and a Bundesland population/10, because a container is not what a bare name means. The kinds with no population use fixed constants: motorway 5 000 000; station 200 000; junction 20 000; stop 1 000; island -1 - after every row of the same score, so an island never pushes a town of its name out of a short candidate list (the resolver places islands from the settlements of their name). Ranking picks the DEFAULT, it does not hide the alternatives.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.gazetteer_places.source_id IS 'The register key that carries this row''s licence and attribution string — geonames (CC BY 4.0), bkg_gvisys (dl-de/by-2-0, changed data), db_stada (CC BY 4.0), delfi_zhv (DELFI zHV, open), autobahn (Autobahn GmbH open data). NOT NULL and constrained by gazetteer_places_source_registered: a tool builds its attribution from the rows it returned, so a row of unknown provenance is a row that may not be shown. Adding a source is a migration ON PURPOSE — it means a register row whose licence was read first. No ODbL (OpenStreetMap) row may enter this table; share-alike data lives in its own tables under its own decision.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.gbfs_systems.licence_basis IS 'Why an admitted system is admitted: stated = its own license_id; gbfs_3_default = a GBFS 3.x document stating neither license_id nor license_url, which the 3.0 specification reads as CC0 1.0. NULL when refused.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.gbfs_systems.license_id IS 'system_information.license_id as the publisher stated it at the last read, verbatim. NULL means the system stated no licence: refused, unless the document is GBFS 3.x and states no license_url either (licence_basis = gbfs_3_default).';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.gbfs_vehicles.seq IS 'The vehicle''s position in this refresh''s list after sorting by point, so two vehicles at one point are two rows. It is NOT an identity: the same number names an unrelated vehicle after the next refresh, by construction.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.gtfs_service_dates.gtfs_feed_key IS 'WHICH FEED this row belongs to: feeds.gtfs_feed_key, a smallint, and the leading column of this table''s primary key and of every index that used to lead on feed_version. It replaced a 19-character text key that was stored three times per row — once in the heap, once in each index — which was 15.7 percent of the family''s 6.07 GiB. There is ONE row per provider in feeds now (delfi_static = 1) and it is STABLE: a new weekly release upserts in place instead of being loaded beside the old one, and the release id itself lives in feeds.extras->>''release_id''.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.hh_roadworks.props IS 'The allow-listed publisher fields, each free-text value with e-mail addresses and telephone numbers replaced. Never the whole record: a field nobody has read may name a person.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.hh_roadworks.released IS 'istfreigegeben = true and nothing else. A site that is not released is stored and never answered.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.hh_roadworks.starts_on IS 'baubeginn as published (DD.MM.YYYY): a Berlin CALENDAR date, not an instant. A site whose starts_on is after the Berlin date today is PLANNED, labelled with its period.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.hydrogen_stations.closed_at IS 'When the PUBLISHER stopped publishing the site (or its last refill point). NULL = still published. Rows are never deleted.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.hydrogen_stations.opening IS 'Recurring opening periods as published, [{"day": "monday", "from": "08:00", "to": "20:00"}, …]; NULL when the site publishes none, which means UNKNOWN and never "always open".';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.hydrogen_stations.provider_updated_at IS 'The site''s own `lastUpdated`: when the publisher last changed the record. Said with the answer, because some are years old.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.hydrogen_stations.refill IS 'The refill points as published, one object per point: {"solution": "gaseousHydrogen700barCar", "unit": "kg", "pricing": ["pricePerDeliveryUnit"]}. Never empty (a site without one is not stored).';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.ingest_runs.body_sha256 IS 'sha256 (lowercase hex) of the one body that defines this run, where the worker has one: the Autobahn road LIST (the document that decides which roads the sweep walks) and the Tankerkönig response of a cycle that made exactly one request. NULL when the run made many requests and no single body describes it — a run-level hash over concatenated payloads would look like evidence and be none.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.ingest_runs.budget_hit IS 'Set when the admission gate refused this run''s request: see feed_runs.budget_hit.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.ingest_runs.duration_ms IS 'Wall time of the run as the WORKER measured it. `started_at`/`finished_at` are the database''s clock in two separate statements; this is one monotonic measurement in the process, which is the number to compare across cycles.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.ingest_runs.id IS 'The run id, and the value a run may stamp on the rows it wrote. `generated by default as identity` rather than `always` (the same change the provenance migration made to feed_runs.id): a run whose ledger row is written at the END but which must stamp rows while it works reserves the id from the sequence first and inserts the row with it. Never set by hand outside such a reservation — a reserved value that is never used is a gap in the sequence, which identity columns have always had and which nothing reads as a count.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.ingest_runs.next_due_at IS 'When this worker plans to sweep again: the sweep''s START + the loop interval that the loop itself is using — the loop schedules from the start of a cycle (it waits `interval - elapsed`), so anchoring this on `finished_at` would make every row late by the whole sweep duration, which for the Autobahn sweep is tens of seconds against a 300 s cadence. The interval is handed to the writer as a value rather than re-read from the environment — a writer that consulted the environment could print a cadence the running loop is not obeying. NULL when the run was not part of a loop (a one-shot discovery) or when the cycle threw before a plan existed. A PLAN, not a promise: a back-off after repeated failures pushes the real next sweep later.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.ingest_runs.note IS 'Human commentary about this run, ≤ 200 characters: what was unusual, or the per-table counts of a retention purge. NEVER per-record — a ledger row is a run, not a log.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.ingest_runs.skipped_targets IS 'Targets (road x kind, station, feed) this sweep did not ask because they are in per-target backoff after consecutive failures. NULL for a worker that has no targets; 0 when every target was asked.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.ingest_runs.worker_version IS 'Which code wrote this row, exactly as `feed_runs.worker_version`: the ingest worker''s version plus `+<git sha>` when the image was built from a checkout. Here for parity — an operator reading the status dashboard must not have to learn that the three workers answer a different set of questions.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.lez_zones.geom_m IS 'geom in ETRS89 / UTM 32N (EPSG:25832, metres), generated: the reach and distance tests of the zone read run here, so no geography is cast per request. At most ~0.2 % scale error inside Germany.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.lez_zones.place_name IS 'The city a zone named only by its register key lies in: the most populous BKG Gemeinde whose point lies inside it, else the nearest within 20 km; null when the publisher names the zone itself. Written by the loader.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.lez_zones.role IS 'zone = the zone''s area; exempt_road = a road through it the zone does not apply to (the publisher''s list); transfer = an area inside it released for vehicles without the plaque (the publisher''s "Transferfläche").';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.los_sites.road IS 'Assigned by the loader, never published: the motorway whose TMC chain lies within 300 m of the site (road_rule says which branch decided). NULL when none or two are equally near.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.mcp_usage.refusals IS 'Requests the protocol refused with an HTTP 4xx before any tool ran. Zero on rows written before the column existed.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.mcp_usage.server_errors IS 'Requests answered with an HTTP 5xx. Zero on rows written before the column existed.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.mcp_usage.tool_error_internal IS 'Tool error results with _meta.errorCode internal; a subset of tool_errors.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.mcp_usage.tool_error_invalid_input IS 'Tool error results with _meta.errorCode invalid_input; a subset of tool_errors.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.mcp_usage.tool_error_limit_reached IS 'Tool error results with _meta.errorCode limit_reached; a subset of tool_errors.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.mcp_usage.tool_error_not_found IS 'Tool error results with _meta.errorCode not_found; a subset of tool_errors.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.mcp_usage.tool_error_out_of_coverage IS 'Tool error results with _meta.errorCode out_of_coverage; a subset of tool_errors.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.mcp_usage.tool_errors IS 'Delivered answers that were errors: isError results and JSON-RPC errors. Zero on rows written before the column existed.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.mcp_usage.tool_error_timeout IS 'Tool error results with _meta.errorCode timeout; a subset of tool_errors.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.mcp_usage.tool_error_unavailable IS 'Tool error results with _meta.errorCode unavailable; a subset of tool_errors.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.motorway_summary.page_listing IS 'The motorway page''s listing rows at the summary''s tick: check_road_status {road}''s listing, at most 50 per class.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.motorway_summary.page_warnings IS 'The motorway page''s warning rows at the summary''s tick: check_autobahn_traffic''s first 50 warnings of the road.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.opendata_roadworks.affects_traffic IS 'False when the publisher says the row restricts no carriageway: a no-stopping zone (Haltverbot), a footpath closure, "keine Verkehrsbehinderung". Stored and never answered by a road tool.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.opendata_roadworks.answerable IS 'False for a row we may store and may not show (props.not_answered_reason says why), e.g. another publisher''s row inside an aggregate the register row does not cover.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.opendata_roadworks.closure_ends_at IS 'Exclusive end of the closure the row''s own words date ("Vollsperrung vom 19.10.2026 bis 26.10.2026"), read at load. Null when the words do not date it: readers then judge `closure` alone. A closure whose window has ended at the request''s clock is not a closure.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.opendata_roadworks.closure_window_rule IS 'The version of the closure-window parser that wrote closure_starts_at/closure_ends_at; a row with an older one is re-written by the next pull even when its content did not change.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.opendata_roadworks.ends_at IS 'Exclusive end. A day-precision publisher''s last day D is stored as the Berlin start of D+1, so a work has ENDED exactly when ends_at <= the request''s clock, for every feed alike.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.osm_addresses.closed_at IS 'Set when an object was absent from an extract covering its Land. Rows close, never delete. The loader''s default close scope is ''covered'', which since 0038 means this row''s `land` and not the set of place names the file happened to carry — see this migration''s header for what the earlier approximation cost.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.osm_addresses.extract_date IS 'The date of the OSM extract this row was last seen in, passed by the operator to the load and never guessed from our clock. A tool result says how old the geocoding is from this column alone, because a join to a run ledger is exactly what this table may not do.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.osm_addresses.house_number_norm IS 'The fold of addr:housenumber: lower-case, spaces removed ("12 a" → "12a"). A range ("12-14") is stored and folded as published — asking for 13 does not reach it, which is honest rather than clever.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.osm_addresses.land IS 'The Geofabrik slug of the Bundesland extract this row is counted under, as the operator named it at load. Recorded, never derived from the row: an address knows its town and its postcode, and no postcode prefix maps cleanly onto a Bundesland. It exists so the close sweep can say "everything of THIS Land that this file did not carry" — the only withdrawal a per-Land extract can honestly make. Where two extracts both carry an object (brandenburg contains Berlin, niedersachsen contains Bremen) the MORE SPECIFIC one owns it, whatever order they were loaded in: the upsert replaces a recorded Land only when the recorded Land contains the incoming one, and any other overlap leaves the row alone and is counted in the run''s ingest_runs note (0044). NULL means the row predates 0038 and is outside every scoped close until its Land is loaded again.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.osm_addresses.osm_type IS 'node | way | relation — with osm_id, OSM''s own identity and this table''s upsert key. A house-number node and the building way that repeats the same address are two rows, because they are two objects.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.osm_addresses.place_norm IS 'The fold of addr:city (or addr:town/addr:village/addr:suburb, in that order), by the same name fold the place gazetteer uses. NULL only when the object carried no place at all, and then the postcode is what tells two Hauptstraßen apart — the locatable check refuses a row with neither.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.osm_addresses.source_id IS 'Always ''osm'', enforced by osm_addresses_source_is_osm. The column exists to make the licence a property of the ROW, as everywhere else in this schema, and the constraint exists so that a loader with a bug cannot put a CC BY row under an ODbL attribution or the reverse. Its mirror image is gazetteer_places_no_odbl below.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.osm_addresses.street_norm IS 'The searchable fold of addr:street, written by the address loader''s street fold and applied identically to the query: lower-case, ß→ss, diacritics folded, punctuation to one space, and the German street suffixes expanded ("Hauptstr." → "hauptstrasse"). NOT a generated column — unaccent() is STABLE, not IMMUTABLE (0016).';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.osm_pois.access IS 'OpenStreetMap''s access=* tag as published (trimmed), null when the object carries none. A car park whose every value is one of the not-public values (private, no, customers, permit, …) is left out of every parking answer. Null on rows loaded before 0198 until their Land is reloaded.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.osm_pois.category IS 'The OSM tag KEY that identified this object — the family. Constrained by osm_pois_category_known to the twelve families the loader may admit (0037''s eight, plus historic and natural since 0070, the latter for peak/volcano/glacier only, highway since 0084, for motorway_junction nodes only, and railway since 0254, for underground station nodes only, its value the kind: subway or light_rail_underground); adding another is a migration on purpose, so that a loader change cannot multiply this table by the size of OSM''s infrastructure tagging without anyone deciding to.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.osm_pois.closed_at IS 'Set when an object was absent from an extract covering its Land. Rows close, never delete — the same rule as osm_addresses and every tick table here. The loader''s default close scope is ''covered'', which since 0038 is scoped by the row''s `land`, so importing one Bundesland does not withdraw the other fifteen.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.osm_pois.extract_date IS 'The date of the EXTRACT the row came from (osmium fileinfo''s osmosis_replication_timestamp), never the date of the import. Sixteen Länder come from sixteen files with sixteen dates, and this column is the only thing that can answer how stale a given Land is.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.osm_pois.land IS 'As osm_addresses.land: the Geofabrik slug of the extract this row is counted under, recorded from the operator''s choice at load so that importing one Bundesland cannot withdraw the other fifteen, and owned by the MORE SPECIFIC extract where two overlap (0044) so that the value does not depend on the order the sixteen files were loaded in.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.osm_pois.source_id IS 'Always ''osm'', enforced by osm_pois_source_is_osm. The column exists to make the licence a property of the ROW, and the constraint exists so that a loader with a bug cannot file an ODbL row under a CC BY attribution. Its mirror image is gazetteer_places_no_odbl (0026), which refuses ''osm'' in the place gazetteer.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.osm_replication_state.applied_at IS 'When THIS host applied that sequence. Beside sequence_timestamp it separates a refresh that is running and finding nothing (applied recently, sequence moving) from one that is stuck (applied recently, sequence frozen) and from one nobody runs (applied months ago).';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.osm_replication_state.extract_date IS 'The extract_date the rows of this Land carry, copied here so the staleness verdict can read one table instead of aggregating over nineteen million address rows. NULL on a row written before the loader passed it. It is a COPY for cost, so the authority stays osm_addresses.extract_date: a disagreement between the two means a load wrote rows without recording its state, which is the state the refresh falls back to a full load over.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.osm_replication_state.sequence IS 'Geofabrik''s replication sequence number for this region, as published in its state.txt or read out of the extract''s own pbf header (osmosis_replication_sequence_number). The refresh applies every diff from this number to the published head and then writes the head here; a number that does not move across refreshes is a stuck refresh, which is the failure this table exists to make visible.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.osm_replication_state.sequence_timestamp IS 'The instant that sequence describes, from the publisher''s own state.txt (osmosis_replication_timestamp), never our clock. This is what "how old is this Land''s geocoding" means and what the deploy staleness verdict reads: our clock would answer "when did we last run something", which is a different question and the one a stuck refresh answers correctly.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.osm_replication_state.source IS 'baseline = written by a full load of a staged extract, from the sequence in that extract''s own pbf header. diff = written by a refresh that applied replication diffs. A Land that has only ever been baselined has never been refreshed, and the verdict says so in different words from a Land whose diffs are simply up to date.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.osm_speed_cameras.tags IS 'Every tag of the node but highway, maxspeed, direction and name, as mapped (never dropped).';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_facilities.attribution_id IS 'The key of the row in ATTRIBUTION that states this row''s licence, its attribution line and its restrictions. Mandatory, checked non-empty, and checked not to be a *_pending label. A tool builds _meta.attribution from the rows it actually returned, so a row with the wrong key here does not fail — it MISATTRIBUTES, which is why the value comes from the feed registry and not from the loader''s own opinion.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_facilities_bysa.attribution_id IS 'The ATTRIBUTION register key. Check-constrained to the CC BY-SA parking keys, so a row of any other licence cannot be written here by mistake.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_facilities_bysa.confidentiality IS 'The DATEX II publication''s headerInformation/confidentiality; NULL when absent. Read paths and the export return only ''noRestriction'' or NULL.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_facilities.closed_at IS 'When the PROVIDER stopped publishing the facility. NULL = still published. Not deleted_at, which says we retracted the row ourselves.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_facilities.confidentiality IS 'The DATEX II publication''s headerInformation/confidentiality; NULL when absent or for a non-DATEX writer (Bahnpark, NRW.Mobidrom). Read paths return only ''noRestriction'' or NULL.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_facilities.deleted_at IS 'WE retracted this row (a duplicate folded, a parse judged wrong, a provider retraction, a manual correction) - it is hidden from every read and hard-deleted by the retention job once it is older than the retention window for retracted rows (at least 30 days). NOT closed_at, which says the PROVIDER stopped publishing the facility and is kept forever; a row may carry both and neither implies the other.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_facilities.deleted_reason IS 'Why this row was retracted, in one short human sentence - "duplicate folded", "parse error, re-loaded". Required by convention whenever deleted_at is set: a retraction with no reason is indistinguishable from an accident, and after the purge window nothing is left to ask.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_facilities.deleted_run_id IS 'The feed_runs.id of the run that retracted the row, or NULL when a human did it by hand. Deliberately NOT a foreign key: an operator action has no run, and the ledger has its own retention, so a dangling id must stay readable rather than block the write.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_facilities.external_id IS 'The provider''s own id, verbatim. Not normalised and not parsed: the static truck feed uses DE-<Land>-<number>, DATEX II publishers use anything at all, and a scheme we invented would match nothing on the next pull.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_facilities.facilities IS 'Amenities the facility''s own publisher states as present (0189): the closed amenity vocabulary of the parking loaders, filled at ingest from the record itself, never guessed and never from another source.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_facilities.facilities_absent IS 'Amenities the publisher states as NOT present (DATEX II notAvailable; a parkingSecurity list that is only "none"). The only ground on which an answer says "kein WC".';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_facilities.facilities_extra IS 'Publisher facility terms outside the vocabulary, verbatim with their origin (equipment:refuseBin, icon:<icon>|<label>) — kept, never dropped, never rendered.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_facilities.facilities_source IS 'The register key (source_feed) of the record that stated the amenities; NULL exactly when it stated none.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_facilities_odbl.attribution_id IS 'The ATTRIBUTION register key. Check-constrained to the ODbL parking keys, so a row of any other licence cannot be written here by mistake.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_facilities_odbl.closed_at IS 'When the PUBLISHER stopped publishing the facility. NULL = still published. Rows are never deleted.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_facilities_odbl.confidentiality IS 'The DATEX II publication''s headerInformation/confidentiality; NULL when absent. Read paths and the ODbL export return only ''noRestriction'' or NULL.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_facilities.opening IS 'Opening hours exactly as the source sends them. No schema is imposed yet on purpose: five sources spell them five ways, and a normalisation written before the second source has been read is a normalisation that loses the third.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_facilities.road IS 'The road the facility serves, as the source spells it (''A8''). NULL where the source says nothing — most city car parks — and never inferred from the geometry: a car park 200 m from a motorway is not on it.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_facilities.source_feed IS 'The feed registry''s key of the feed that wrote the row. It is half of the natural key rather than a provenance column: a provider''s external_id is unique inside that provider and nowhere else. The attribution string and the licence are looked up from the feed''s register row, never from a constant in a tool.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_facilities.source_version IS 'The publisher''s own release identifier for the document this row came from - for the static truck feed, parkingTable/@version, whose file name carries a date. It is how "the same site, a new release" is told from "the same site, the same release", which is the only change signal a feed with no tick table has.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_facilities.station_name IS 'The station''s name as the source spells it ("Köln Hbf"). The join that works TODAY: gazetteer_places.name for kind = ''station''. Kept beside station_ref because the two fail differently — a name matches a human''s question and drifts with spelling, a number is stable and is private to its publisher.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_facilities.station_name_norm IS 'The searchable fold of station_name, written by the loader''s name fold — the SAME function that writes gazetteer_places.name_norm, because the only purpose of this column is to be equal to that one. Lower-case, diacritics folded, punctuation collapsed. NOT a generated column: unaccent() is STABLE, not IMMUTABLE, and an index over a fake-immutable wrapper corrupts when the dictionary changes (0016).';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_facilities.station_ref IS 'The PUBLISHER''s identifier for the station this facility serves, as text and unnormalised — DB Bahnpark''s station number, another source''s EVA or DS100. NULL means the facility serves no station (a motorway rest area, a city car park), which is a fact and not a missing value. It is not a foreign key: it is one publisher''s numbering, and the gazetteer is keyed by another.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_forecast_profiles.class IS 'green = "mehr als 75 % der Parkplätze sind frei verfügbar", yellow = "mehr als 10 % sind verfügbar", red = "weniger als 10 % sind verfügbar" (the dataset''s own definition). A class, not a number.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_forecast_profiles.deleted_at IS 'WE retracted this row (a join judged wrong, a parse judged wrong) - it is hidden from every read and hard-deleted by the retention job once it is older than the retention window for retracted rows (at least 30 days). NOT closed_at/superseded_at, which say the PROVIDER stopped publishing this Stand (a newer one replaced it) and are kept forever; a row may carry both and neither implies the other.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_forecast_profiles.deleted_reason IS 'Why this row was retracted, in one short human sentence. Required by convention whenever deleted_at is set.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_forecast_profiles.deleted_run_id IS 'The feed_runs.id of the run that retracted the row, or NULL when a human did it by hand. Deliberately NOT a foreign key: an operator action has no run.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_forecast_profiles.superseded_at IS 'When a newer Stand of the same forecast replaced this row. The row is kept: the history of a forecast is part of the record. NOT deleted_at: superseded is the PUBLISHER''s act, kept forever.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_occupancy.confidentiality IS 'The DATEX II status publication''s headerInformation/confidentiality; NULL when absent or for a non-DATEX writer. Read paths return only ''noRestriction'' or NULL.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_occupancy.implausible_fields IS 'The published values that cannot be right (0220): occupancy_pct, spaces_free, spaces_total, feed_ts — each one NULL in its column, its published value in raw. NULL when none is. A negative figure, a percentage beyond the column, more free spaces than the site has (both counts), a stamp beyond the reader''s clock-skew tolerance in the future (the reading is then dated by observed_at, our receipt). The record is kept; only a malformed one is refused.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_occupancy.observed_at IS 'When we pulled the payload, not when the publisher changed anything — that is feed_ts. The partition key, and the only column a reader may use to line a row up against feed_runs.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_occupancy.occupancy_pct IS 'The percentage AS PUBLISHED, never computed from spaces_free and spaces_total. Some publishers send only a percentage and some send counts that disagree with their own percentage; computing it here would erase which of the two the publisher actually stood behind. Above 100 is stored as published — an overfull site (0220); never negative.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_occupancy_odbl.attribution_id IS 'The ATTRIBUTION register key of the occupancy dataset. Check-constrained to the ODbL parking occupancy keys, so a row of any other licence cannot be written here by mistake.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_occupancy_odbl.confidentiality IS 'The DATEX II status publication''s headerInformation/confidentiality; NULL when absent. Read paths and the ODbL export return only ''noRestriction'' or NULL.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_occupancy_odbl.observed_at IS 'When we pulled the payload. The publisher''s own stamp is feed_ts; find_parking judges a reading''s age by the publisher''s clock and refuses to call a value that has not moved "free now".';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_occupancy_sid.facility_id IS 'parking_facilities.id of the truckpark_static site, or parking_facilities_sid.id for a site only the SID static offer carries; both take their id from parking_facilities_id_seq, so the id names one site.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_occupancy.spaces_total IS 'The total AT THIS INSTANT as the publisher stated it, which is not parking_facilities.capacity_total: a lane closed for works lowers the former and leaves the latter alone, and the difference is the interesting part.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_sites.closed_at IS 'When the site stopped appearing in the download. NULL = still published. The PROVIDER''s fact, kept forever — our own retraction of a row is deleted_at.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_sites.created_at IS 'When WE first wrote this row. Never moves again — a row that is closed and reappears keeps it. Backfilled from first_seen_at by the provenance migration.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_sites.deleted_at IS 'WE retracted this row (a duplicate folded, a parse judged wrong, a provider retraction, a manual correction) — it is hidden from every read and hard-deleted by the retention job once it is older than the retention window for retracted rows (at least 30 days). NOT closed_at, which says the PROVIDER stopped publishing the record and is kept forever; a row may carry both and neither implies the other.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_sites.deleted_reason IS 'Why this row was retracted, in one short human sentence — "duplicate folded", "parse error, re-loaded", "provider retraction". Required by convention whenever deleted_at is set: a retraction with no reason is indistinguishable from an accident, and after the purge window nothing is left to ask.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_sites.deleted_run_id IS 'The ingest_runs.id of the run that retracted the row, or NULL when a human did it by hand. Deliberately NOT a foreign key: an operator action has no run, and the ledger has its own (planned) retention, so a dangling id must stay readable rather than block the write.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_sites.first_seen_at IS 'The first cycle this row appeared in a payload. SEEN, not created: `created_at` is when we wrote it. Never updated.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_sites.geom IS 'geography, not geometry, unlike road_events.geom: distances here are asked in metres (ST_DWithin) and this feed always carries a point.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_sites.last_seen_at IS 'The most recent cycle this row appeared in a payload, and the diff-and-close presence marker. SEEN, not CHANGED: a publisher that re-sends an identical record moves this and leaves `updated_at` where it was.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_sites.provider_updated_at IS 'The publisher''s OWN timestamp for the payload this row was last seen in, never ours. NULL when the provider publishes none (Tankerkönig) — a substituted "now" would make a stale copy look fresh. A SIGHTING column like source_run_id: assigned on every sighting, changed or not. The publication lag is therefore `last_seen_at - provider_updated_at` (how long after the publisher published we had the payload) and NEVER `updated_at - provider_updated_at`, which is negative for every row that has not changed since the publisher''s last release.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_sites.source_feed IS 'THE AUTHOR: the registry key of the feed this row came from (the feed registry''s key for a Mobilithek feed, the provider name for a worker that has no registry row). Never a subscription id — those are secrets and are kept outside the database. What the status dashboard''s "source" column and the per-row attribution line are read from.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_sites.source_publisher IS 'Who PUBLISHED the record, as the payload names them: the DATEX II publicationCreator / situation publisher, the AFIR publicationCreator, or the register''s publisher where the payload names none (Tankerkönig: the source register decides the string). Attribution is carried per feed today; this is what lets one tool result name the actual publisher of the one record it is reading aloud.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_sites.source_run_id IS 'The cycle that LAST SAW this row: `feed_runs.id` for a Mobilithek feed, `ingest_runs.id` for the Autobahn and Tankerkönig workers — which one is a property of this table. A snapshot upsert writes it on every sighting, changed or not, so it answers "which cycle had this row in its payload", NOT "which cycle last changed it" — that is `updated_at`, beside it. No foreign key and no index by design. NULL on rows written before the provenance migration.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.parking_sites.updated_at IS 'When this row last actually CHANGED — not when it was last seen. Maintained by the writers, in the same statement that decides a row moved; a payload that repeats a record unchanged moves last_seen_at and leaves this alone.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.partition_archives.dropped_at IS 'When the partition was detached and dropped, in the same transaction as the drop. NULL means the file exists and the partition does too.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.partition_archives.restored_at IS 'The last successful restore. Never cleared: that a day has been read back is the one thing a restore drill leaves behind worth keeping.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.partition_archives.rows IS 'The LIVE partition''s count, taken before the dump and compared against what a pg_restore into a scratch schema yields. 0 is legal — a day the workers never wrote.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.partition_archives.sha256 IS 'Lower-case hex sha256 of the dump''s bytes, CHECK-constrained to 64 hex characters so a truncated hash cannot be stored and then compare equal to itself.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.partition_archives.table_name IS 'The partitioned PARENT (`autobahn_events`), never the partition. The partition name is derivable from (table_name, day); the pair is what a human asks about.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.provider_key_claims.person_wanted_at IS 'The last time a PERSON''s request tried to claim this key, granted or refused: the MCP server and hand-run CLIs stamp it before they claim. The price worker''s background claim succeeds only while it is older than two key spacings, so background work never takes the key a person is waiting for.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_context_features.answerable IS 'False for a row we may store and may not show (props.not_answered_reason says why).';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_context_features.variant IS 'The kind''s sub-kind out of a closed list: for a border crossing the class of the German road it carries (motorway, federal, state, district), ferry, or other; for a traffic signal acoustic (the publisher lists an acoustic aid for blind pedestrians) or plain (it lists none); for a winter-service section the publisher''s priority (priority_1 cleared first, priority_2, priority_3) or unranked; for an entrance to a pedestrian zone the sign the publisher lists at it (pedestrian_zone 242, no_motor_vehicles 260, no_entry 267, no_cycling 254) or sign_not_listed; for a detour route the traffic it is signed for (motor_vehicles, lorries, cars, cycles, all_traffic) or other; for a loading zone the use the publisher names (load_deliver, load_deliver_work with trades, alternating) or other; for a place for coaches parking or stop_only (setting down and picking up only); for a numbered detour signed for motorway traffic signed (signs 460/455, which of the two is not published); for a street''s on-street bays under one rule set the first rule the city''s register names (ticket, disc, unmanaged, disabled, ev_charging, loading, sharing, disabled_personal, restricted_stopping, no_stopping, unresolved, other); for a signal installation where it stands: junction (a provisional one included), pedestrian_crossing, or other.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_event_locations.created_at IS 'When we wrote this stretch. NOT the same "never moves again" as elsewhere: the stretches of an event are deleted and rewritten wholesale whenever the parent event''s version moves, so this is when the CURRENT set of stretches was written, and it moves with the parent''s updated_at.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_event_locations.deleted_at IS 'WE retracted this row (a duplicate folded, a parse judged wrong, a provider retraction, a manual correction) — it is hidden from every read and hard-deleted by the retention job once it is older than the retention window for retracted rows (at least 30 days). NOT closed_at, which says the PROVIDER stopped publishing the record and is kept forever; a row may carry both and neither implies the other.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_event_locations.deleted_reason IS 'Why this row was retracted, in one short human sentence — "duplicate folded", "parse error, re-loaded", "provider retraction". Required by convention whenever deleted_at is set: a retraction with no reason is indistinguishable from an accident, and after the purge window nothing is left to ask.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_event_locations.deleted_run_id IS 'The ingest_runs.id of the run that retracted the row, or NULL when a human did it by hand. Deliberately NOT a foreign key: an operator action has no run, and the ledger has its own (planned) retention, so a dangling id must stay readable rather than block the write.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_event_locations.provider_updated_at IS 'The publisher''s OWN timestamp for the payload this row was last seen in, never ours. NULL when the provider publishes none (Tankerkönig) — a substituted "now" would make a stale copy look fresh. A SIGHTING column like source_run_id: assigned on every sighting, changed or not. The publication lag is therefore `last_seen_at - provider_updated_at` (how long after the publisher published we had the payload) and NEVER `updated_at - provider_updated_at`, which is negative for every row that has not changed since the publisher''s last release.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_event_locations.source_feed IS 'THE AUTHOR: the registry key of the feed this row came from (the feed registry''s key for a Mobilithek feed, the provider name for a worker that has no registry row). Never a subscription id — those are secrets and are kept outside the database. What the status dashboard''s "source" column and the per-row attribution line are read from.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_event_locations.source_publisher IS 'Who PUBLISHED the record, as the payload names them: the DATEX II publicationCreator / situation publisher, the AFIR publicationCreator, or the register''s publisher where the payload names none (Tankerkönig: the source register decides the string). Attribution is carried per feed today; this is what lets one tool result name the actual publisher of the one record it is reading aloud.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_event_locations.source_run_id IS 'The cycle that LAST SAW this row: `feed_runs.id` for a Mobilithek feed, `ingest_runs.id` for the Autobahn and Tankerkönig workers — which one is a property of this table. A snapshot upsert writes it on every sighting, changed or not, so it answers "which cycle had this row in its payload", NOT "which cycle last changed it" — that is `updated_at`, beside it. No foreign key and no index by design. NULL on rows written before the provenance migration.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_event_locations.updated_at IS 'When this row last actually CHANGED — not when it was last seen. Maintained by the writers, in the same statement that decides a row moved; a payload that repeats a record unchanged moves last_seen_at and leaves this alone.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_event_record_types.known IS 'Whether the type was in the parser''s KNOWN_*_RECORD_TYPES when it was FIRST seen. False is the normal value for a feed whose payload has never been dissected; it is not re-evaluated later, so the first sighting stays readable.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_event_record_types.seen_count IS 'Records of this type summed over all cycles — a rate, not a population. The same situation counted every quarter hour is counted every quarter hour; compare types with each other, never with a row count.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_events.closed_at IS 'When the PROVIDER stopped publishing this event — a business fact, kept forever. Not a retraction: that is deleted_at, which we set and the retention job eventually purges.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_events.closure_ends_at IS 'End (EXCLUSIVE) of that dated closure; null when not dated or open-ended ("bis auf Weiteres"). Rewritten on every pull.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_events.closure_starts_at IS 'Start (inclusive) of the closure the message''s own comment text dates ("Vollsperrung vom 19.10. bis 26.10."); null when not dated or open from the start. Rewritten on every pull.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_events.confidentiality IS 'The publisher''s DATEX II marking for this situation: the first restrictive value among the publication header, headerInformation/confidentiality and every situationRecord/confidentialityOverride; else ''noRestriction'' when stated; NULL when absent. Stored always; every read path that answers a person returns only ''noRestriction'' or NULL.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_events.created_at IS 'When WE first wrote this row. Never moves again — a row that is closed and reappears keeps it. Backfilled from first_seen_at by the provenance migration.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_events.deleted_at IS 'WE retracted this row (a duplicate folded, a parse judged wrong, a provider retraction, a manual correction) — it is hidden from every read and hard-deleted by the retention job once it is older than the retention window for retracted rows (at least 30 days). NOT closed_at, which says the PROVIDER stopped publishing the record and is kept forever; a row may carry both and neither implies the other.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_events.deleted_reason IS 'Why this row was retracted, in one short human sentence — "duplicate folded", "parse error, re-loaded", "provider retraction". Required by convention whenever deleted_at is set: a retraction with no reason is indistinguishable from an accident, and after the purge window nothing is left to ask.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_events.deleted_run_id IS 'The ingest_runs.id of the run that retracted the row, or NULL when a human did it by hand. Deliberately NOT a foreign key: an operator action has no run, and the ledger has its own (planned) retention, so a dangling id must stay readable rather than block the write.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_events.feed_key IS 'The feed whose pull last wrote this row. Four feeds carry road events and they overlap; overlapping publishers are not reconciled, so for now the last writer wins and this column says who that was.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_events.first_seen_at IS 'The first cycle this row appeared in a payload. SEEN, not created: `created_at` is when we wrote it. Never updated.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_events.geom IS 'NULL is the NORMAL case, not missing data: the BASt roadworks feed carries no coordinates at all — not one <latitude> in 104 MB. Location is BISSTRA linear referencing (road_number, referent_from/to, distance_from/to_m). Resolving those to coordinates needs the BISSTRA network, which we do not hold; a fabricated point would be worse than NULL.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_events.last_seen_at IS 'The most recent cycle this row appeared in a payload, and the diff-and-close presence marker. SEEN, not CHANGED: a publisher that re-sends an identical record moves this and leaves `updated_at` where it was.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_events.overall_end IS 'The outer window''s end. 2099-12-31 is the publishers'' open-ended sentinel and is stored AS RECEIVED, never rewritten to NULL; it is interpreted at query time.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_events.overall_start IS 'The outer window''s start. The recurring validity (day-of-week, hour-of-day periods) lives in `payload`; the outer window is NOT the answer to "is it in force now".';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_events.payload IS 'The full DATEX II record. DATEX II carries far more than we model and history cannot be re-fetched.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_events.provider_updated_at IS 'The publisher''s OWN timestamp for the payload this row was last seen in, never ours. NULL when the provider publishes none (Tankerkönig) — a substituted "now" would make a stale copy look fresh. A SIGHTING column like source_run_id: assigned on every sighting, changed or not. The publication lag is therefore `last_seen_at - provider_updated_at` (how long after the publisher published we had the payload) and NEVER `updated_at - provider_updated_at`, which is negative for every row that has not changed since the publisher''s last release.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_events.publisher IS 'The DATEX II publication creator, e.g. DE-MIA, DE-NWBIS-VAS, DE-BAST-VAS, DE_BY — one feed carried all four in the sample. Usually equal to source_id, but a publisher may relay another source''s situation, which is why both exist.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_events.source_feed IS 'THE AUTHOR: the registry key of the feed this row came from (the feed registry''s key for a Mobilithek feed, the provider name for a worker that has no registry row). Never a subscription id — those are secrets and are kept outside the database. What the status dashboard''s "source" column and the per-row attribution line are read from.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_events.source_publisher IS 'Who PUBLISHED the record, as the payload names them: the DATEX II publicationCreator / situation publisher, the AFIR publicationCreator, or the register''s publisher where the payload names none (Tankerkönig: the source register decides the string). Attribution is carried per feed today; this is what lets one tool result name the actual publisher of the one record it is reading aloud.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_events.source_run_id IS 'The cycle that LAST SAW this row: `feed_runs.id` for a Mobilithek feed, `ingest_runs.id` for the Autobahn and Tankerkönig workers — which one is a property of this table. A snapshot upsert writes it on every sighting, changed or not, so it answers "which cycle had this row in its payload", NOT "which cycle last changed it" — that is `updated_at`, beside it. No foreign key and no index by design. NULL on rows written before the provenance migration.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_events.suspended IS 'road_event_suspended(payload), kept by the road_events_suspended trigger on every write of the payload. NULL only on a row no write has reached since 0280 (closed rows): road_event_in_force_known reads the payload for those.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_events.updated_at IS 'When this row last actually CHANGED — not when it was last seen. Maintained by the writers, in the same statement that decides a row moved; a payload that repeats a record unchanged moves last_seen_at and leaves this alone.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_events.valid_windows IS 'The windows the situation''s DATEX II validity states: every record''s overall span intersected with its validPeriods, recurrences expanded in Europe/Berlin civil time. NULL when no validPeriod is stated or one is stated that the rule does not read: the overall window then governs. Rewritten on every pull.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_event_ticks.source_run_id IS 'The cycle that appended this tick: `feed_runs.id` for the Mobilithek feeds (transit_delays, road_event_ticks, charging_point_status), `ingest_runs.id` for the Autobahn and Tankerkönig sweeps (autobahn_events, fuel_prices). Ticks are append-only, so it is written once and never updated. NULL on partitions written before the provenance migration.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_weather_forecast.rc IS 'Straßenzustand forecast by the METRo road-weather model: 1 Trocken, 2 Feucht, 3 Schnee, 4 Reif, 5 Überfrierende Nässe, 6 Glatteis. A FORECAST class: it is never shown as a measured road state and never as an official warning.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_weather_forecast.run_at IS 'The run time from line 2 of the SWSMOS file (UTC). The forecast''s age is measured from this, never from the file''s Last-Modified.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.road_weather_stations.flag_raw IS 'The catalogue column headed "Flughafen", verbatim. It carries A/L/K/B/F/P/62 and no published description says whether it is a road class or an airport flag, so no answer reads it.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.routes.deleted_at IS 'WE retracted this row (a duplicate folded, a parse judged wrong, a provider retraction, a manual correction) — it is hidden from every read and hard-deleted by the retention job once it is older than the retention window for retracted rows (at least 30 days). NOT closed_at, which says the PROVIDER stopped publishing the record and is kept forever; a row may carry both and neither implies the other.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.routes.deleted_reason IS 'Why this row was retracted, in one short human sentence — "duplicate folded", "parse error, re-loaded", "provider retraction". Required by convention whenever deleted_at is set: a retraction with no reason is indistinguishable from an accident, and after the purge window nothing is left to ask.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.routes.deleted_run_id IS 'The ingest_runs.id of the run that retracted the row, or NULL when a human did it by hand. Deliberately NOT a foreign key: an operator action has no run, and the ledger has its own (planned) retention, so a dangling id must stay readable rather than block the write.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.routes.gtfs_feed_key IS 'WHICH FEED this row belongs to: feeds.gtfs_feed_key, a smallint, and the leading column of this table''s primary key and of every index that used to lead on feed_version. It replaced a 19-character text key that was stored three times per row — once in the heap, once in each index — which was 15.7 percent of the family''s 6.07 GiB. There is ONE row per provider in feeds now (delfi_static = 1) and it is STABLE: a new weekly release upserts in place instead of being loaded beside the old one, and the release id itself lives in feeds.extras->>''release_id''.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.speed_enforcement.ags IS 'The 8-digit Gemeinde key: where the publisher gives no point, the row is shown for this Gemeinde only.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.speed_enforcement.data_as_of IS 'The publication''s own date for the row ("Stand"), when it states one; provider_updated_at is the same stamp as an instant when the publication gives one.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.speed_enforcement.extras IS 'Every published field the shape does not name, as published (never dropped).';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.station_accessibility.platform_height_cm IS 'PlatformHeight in centimetres: the publisher writes metres with a decimal point (0.76) and, 363 times in the 2026-10-18 release, bare centimetres (55). Below 3 is metres; 0 and above 200 cm are NULL.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.station_equipment.eva_no IS 'The EVA number from the StopPlace keyList (Key EVA), verbatim. NULL when the StopPlace publishes none: the row is still stored, and simply cannot be reached by an EVA question.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.station_equipment_state.state IS 'active | inactive | unknown, folded from SIRI Status: available -> active; notAvailable -> inactive; everything else (unknown, partiallyAvailable, an unseen word) -> unknown. `unknown` is the publisher''s own third value ("not monitored", "monitoring disrupted") and is never rounded up to working.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.station_equipment.stop_place_id IS 'The stop the equipment belongs to, as a DHID. OpenStation: the enclosing NeTEx StopPlace @id (dhid:…), never NULL. KVB: the zHV DHID whose number is the KVB stop area''s VRSNummer (de:05315:11110), NULL when no zHV row matched — the unit is then stored and reported as unmapped.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.station_metro_osm.decisive IS 'True when the level qualifies as a station page ONLY through this match (no rail or S-Bahn family, no U-Bahn mode, no U-named line, no lift or escalator in the inventory): its page and every list naming it carry the OpenStreetMap notice and the ODbL offer. Computed by the derivation from the timetable and the inventory in a statement of its own and written as a bound value; true for a row nobody judged.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.stops.deleted_at IS 'WE retracted this row (a duplicate folded, a parse judged wrong, a provider retraction, a manual correction) — it is hidden from every read and hard-deleted by the retention job once it is older than the retention window for retracted rows (at least 30 days). NOT closed_at, which says the PROVIDER stopped publishing the record and is kept forever; a row may carry both and neither implies the other.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.stops.deleted_reason IS 'Why this row was retracted, in one short human sentence — "duplicate folded", "parse error, re-loaded", "provider retraction". Required by convention whenever deleted_at is set: a retraction with no reason is indistinguishable from an accident, and after the purge window nothing is left to ask.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.stops.deleted_run_id IS 'The ingest_runs.id of the run that retracted the row, or NULL when a human did it by hand. Deliberately NOT a foreign key: an operator action has no run, and the ledger has its own (planned) retention, so a dangling id must stay readable rather than block the write.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.stops.gtfs_feed_key IS 'WHICH FEED this row belongs to: feeds.gtfs_feed_key, a smallint, and the leading column of this table''s primary key and of every index that used to lead on feed_version. It replaced a 19-character text key that was stored three times per row — once in the heap, once in each index — which was 15.7 percent of the family''s 6.07 GiB. There is ONE row per provider in feeds now (delfi_static = 1) and it is STABLE: a new weekly release upserts in place instead of being loaded beside the old one, and the release id itself lives in feeds.extras->>''release_id''.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.stop_times.deleted_at IS 'WE retracted this row (a duplicate folded, a parse judged wrong, a provider retraction, a manual correction) — it is hidden from every read and hard-deleted by the retention job once it is older than the retention window for retracted rows (at least 30 days). NOT closed_at, which says the PROVIDER stopped publishing the record and is kept forever; a row may carry both and neither implies the other.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.stop_times.deleted_reason IS 'Why this row was retracted, in one short human sentence — "duplicate folded", "parse error, re-loaded", "provider retraction". Required by convention whenever deleted_at is set: a retraction with no reason is indistinguishable from an accident, and after the purge window nothing is left to ask.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.stop_times.deleted_run_id IS 'The ingest_runs.id of the run that retracted the row, or NULL when a human did it by hand. Deliberately NOT a foreign key: an operator action has no run, and the ledger has its own (planned) retention, so a dangling id must stay readable rather than block the write.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.stop_times.gtfs_feed_key IS 'WHICH FEED this row belongs to: feeds.gtfs_feed_key, a smallint, and the leading column of this table''s primary key and of every index that used to lead on feed_version. It replaced a 19-character text key that was stored three times per row — once in the heap, once in each index — which was 15.7 percent of the family''s 6.07 GiB. There is ONE row per provider in feeds now (delfi_static = 1) and it is STABLE: a new weekly release upserts in place instead of being loaded beside the old one, and the release id itself lives in feeds.extras->>''release_id''.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.street_bays.kind IS 'OURS, from the publisher''s word in publisher_kind by one table in the street-bay parser; a word nobody maps is ''unspecified'' and degrades the pull. Never inferred from another column (a "Parksensor" on a NoxBlock lamp stays unspecified).';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.street_bays.last_message_at IS 'The publisher''s newest message of this bay when the inventory was read: the inventory''s own freshness, not the state''s (street_bay_state.message_at is that).';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.street_bays.reserved_when IS 'The publisher''s words for when the reservation holds ("durchgehend", "Mo - Fr 8 - 18 Uhr"), verbatim; NULL when none is stated. Quoted by readers, never parsed.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.street_bays.spaces IS 'How many reserved spaces this row stands for, as the publisher counts them. NULL = one bay with one sensor (0146); a sensor-less publisher states it on every row. Readers count bays as coalesce(spaces, 1).';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.transit_delays.delay_s IS 'Seconds late, negative = early. Departure delay where the feed gives one, otherwise arrival delay. NULL is a real state and not a missing value: 11% of a pull carries a predicted TIME and no delay, which cannot be turned into a delay without the static schedule (see trip_id). Values beyond +/-6 h are dropped at parse time with a warning and never stored.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.transit_delays.feed_ts IS 'The FeedHeader.timestamp of the pull this row came from. The only clock this feed gives us: DELFI leaves TripUpdate.timestamp unset on every entity, so per-trip freshness is not available. A loader that cannot read a usable header timestamp writes no rows at all rather than rows stamped 1970.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.transit_delays.observed_at IS 'When WE pulled the feed (UTC). The partition key, and the identity of the tick.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.transit_delays.raw IS 'The parts of the stop-time update that delay_s does not carry, and ONLY where there are any: a non-SCHEDULED relationship, a predicted time with no delay, or a trip-level cancellation (11.8% of rows). A raw jsonb on every row costs 310 B instead of 180 B per row measured, which at this feed''s volume is the difference between fitting the disk budget and not.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.transit_delays.schedule_relationship IS 'The GTFS-RT enum NAME, never the number: 1 means SKIPPED on a stop-time update and ADDED on a trip, so a number here could not be read. Stop-time level: SCHEDULED, SKIPPED, NO_DATA, UNSCHEDULED. Trip level (stop_id IS NULL): CANCELED, DELETED. A value the spec grew after our bindings arrives as UNKNOWN_<n>.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.transit_delays.source IS 'The provider/licence id from the feed registry, and the attribution key the MCP layer emits. delfi_rt_tu = DELFI GTFS-RT, CC BY-SA 4.0. Attribution travels with the row.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.transit_delays.source_run_id IS 'The cycle that appended this tick: `feed_runs.id` for the Mobilithek feeds (transit_delays, road_event_ticks, charging_point_status), `ingest_runs.id` for the Autobahn and Tankerkönig sweeps (autobahn_events, fuel_prices). Ticks are append-only, so it is written once and never updated. NULL on partitions written before the provenance migration.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.transit_delays.stop_id IS 'GTFS stop_id (DHID/IFOPT form, e.g. de:06435:13520:1:1). NULL on a TRIP-level row — a cancellation, which this feed publishes with no stop-time updates at all.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.transit_delays.stop_sequence IS 'StopTimeUpdate.stop_sequence. NULL on a trip-level row, for the same reason as stop_id.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.transit_delays.trip_id IS 'GTFS TripDescriptor.trip_id, verbatim. NOT resolvable to a route, a stop name or a timetable yet: the static DELFI GTFS (528 MB weekly, opendata-oepnv.de) is not ingested, so these ids are opaque keys.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.trips.deleted_at IS 'WE retracted this row (a duplicate folded, a parse judged wrong, a provider retraction, a manual correction) — it is hidden from every read and hard-deleted by the retention job once it is older than the retention window for retracted rows (at least 30 days). NOT closed_at, which says the PROVIDER stopped publishing the record and is kept forever; a row may carry both and neither implies the other.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.trips.deleted_reason IS 'Why this row was retracted, in one short human sentence — "duplicate folded", "parse error, re-loaded", "provider retraction". Required by convention whenever deleted_at is set: a retraction with no reason is indistinguishable from an accident, and after the purge window nothing is left to ask.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.trips.deleted_run_id IS 'The ingest_runs.id of the run that retracted the row, or NULL when a human did it by hand. Deliberately NOT a foreign key: an operator action has no run, and the ledger has its own (planned) retention, so a dangling id must stay readable rather than block the write.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.trips.gtfs_feed_key IS 'WHICH FEED this row belongs to: feeds.gtfs_feed_key, a smallint, and the leading column of this table''s primary key and of every index that used to lead on feed_version. It replaced a 19-character text key that was stored three times per row — once in the heap, once in each index — which was 15.7 percent of the family''s 6.07 GiB. There is ONE row per provider in feeds now (delfi_static = 1) and it is STABLE: a new weekly release upserts in place instead of being loaded beside the old one, and the release id itself lives in feeds.extras->>''release_id''.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.trips.loaded_run_id IS 'The ingest_runs.id of the load that last WROTE this trip — the generational marker the prune reads. Every trip the current release carries is stamped on every load (unconditionally, which is why a trip row is rewritten once per load), so a trip whose stamp is not the current run''s is exactly a trip the release has DROPPED, and the prune deletes it and its stop_times. This is the column that keeps a stable feed_version honest: without it a dropped trip would stay on a departure board for as long as its service dates sat inside the 48-hour window. Deliberately NOT on stop_times: stamping 18 M rows per load was measured at a doubling of the heap in one daily roll.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.trips.stop_times_fp IS 'Hash of this trip''s own ordered `(stop_sequence, stop_id, arrival, departure)` tuples, written by the GTFS loader as `stop_times.txt` streams. The loader deletes a trip''s `stop_times` before inserting them when this value differs from the one it just computed — which is the only thing that removes a `stop_sequence` the release WITHDREW from a trip it KEPT. 0051''s `loaded_run_id` stamp covers the trip the release dropped entirely; this covers the trip it shortened, and without it the upsert overwrites what still exists and deletes nothing, leaving a departure at a stop the trip no longer serves. NULL means never fingerprinted and is read as "differs", so the first load after 0053 rewrites each trip''s rows once. It is a CHANGE DETECTOR and not a checksum: a collision costs a missed deletion on one trip, never a wrong row, which is why 4 bytes is enough and why nothing here depends on it being cryptographic.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.trips.trip_short_name IS 'GTFS `trips.trip_short_name` as the feed published it, trimmed: on DELFI the train number of a rail trip, zero-padded ("000540"), and an operator''s internal trip number on a bus. The departure board shows it for long-distance route types only. NULL where the feed carries none, and on rows written before migration 0137 until the next roll that loads a release.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.vbn_siri_et_calls.source_feed IS 'The registry key of the Mobilithek feed whose pull wrote this row: vbn_siri_et.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.vbn_siri_et_calls.vehicle_mode IS 'SIRI VehicleMode of the journey as published (lower-cased, from the SIRI value list), or null.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.watches.condition IS 'The threshold as a jsonb object, e.g. {"delay_min": 10}. NULL means the kind''s default. The per-kind shape is defined and validated by the watch notifier.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.watches.expires_at IS 'When the watch stops producing events. At most 24 hours after created_at, enforced by watches_window_24h — a constraint and not a convention, because both ends are columns of this row and neither needs now().';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.watches.key IS 'What is watched, in the vocabulary of kind — a road number, a DHID stop id, an AGS prefix, a place name, a DWD warncell, a charging site id. The only caller-supplied string in this schema, bounded to 200 characters.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.watches.kind IS 'road | station | region | place | weather | charger. Text with a check and not an enum: a seventh kind is then a one-line migration instead of an ALTER TYPE on a live database.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.watches.session_id IS 'The MCP session id, and NO OTHER FACT ABOUT THE USER. No name, no e-mail, no IP, no user agent. It dies with the session; the row is purged by the retention job once the watch is past its window.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.watches.stopped_at IS 'Set by stop_watch. A stopped watch produces no further events; the row is kept as the evidence that it existed until the retention window passes.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.watch_events.as_of IS 'The instant the change is ABOUT, from the data (observed_at / valid_from / last_seen_at), never our clock. Stored UTC, rendered Europe/Berlin.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.watch_events.revision IS 'How many changes this summary stands for. > 1 means the rate limit folded later changes into the pending row instead of notifying again.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.watch_events.source_ids IS 'Attribution register keys of the rows behind the summary, so the resource that serves it names what actually answered. A single event never mixes CC BY-SA with CC BY: each kind reads one relation.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.watch_events.summary IS 'Generated by our renderer from our rows. No user text reaches this column — the only caller-supplied string in the engine is watches.key.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.weather_warnings.area_sha256 IS 'sha256 of the cell''s GeoJSON geometry exactly as received. The loader''s change key reads this and never the geography column: ST_AsGeoJSON re-renders, so two different documents would compare equal and a warn cell redrawn with identical wording would keep its old shape for ever.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.weather_warnings.closed_at IS 'When this version stopped being the published one - see closed_reason. NOT deleted_at, which says WE retracted the row.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.weather_warnings.closed_reason IS '''withdrawn'' - the warning disappeared from the publication (cancelled, or expired out of it); ''superseded'' - a newer version of the same warning replaced it and keeps its own row. Nothing is ever deleted: a warning that was in force is a fact about the past.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.weather_warnings.deleted_at IS 'WE retracted this row (a duplicate folded, a parse judged wrong) - it is hidden from every read and hard-deleted by the retention job once it is older than the retention window for retracted rows (at least 30 days). NOT closed_at, which says the PROVIDER stopped publishing this version - withdrawn or superseded - and is kept forever; a row may carry both and neither implies the other.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.weather_warnings.deleted_reason IS 'Why this row was retracted, in one short human sentence. Required by convention whenever deleted_at is set: a retraction with no reason is indistinguishable from an accident.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.weather_warnings.deleted_run_id IS 'The feed_runs.id of the run that retracted the row, or NULL when a human did it by hand. Deliberately NOT a foreign key: an operator action has no run.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.weather_warnings.description IS 'THE OFFICIAL TEXT, exactly as published: never edited, never shortened, never translated. A translation is an alteration, and an altered amtliche Warnung carries no Quellenvermerk. Hashed into official_sha256 at ingest so the read path can prove the bytes are unchanged before it reproduces them.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.weather_warnings_en.base_identifier IS 'The CAP identifier without its language suffix (.ENG/.DEU/.MUL) — the same rule the read paths apply to weather_warnings.identifier. That the two sets share it is the DWD''s naming as documented; the first real pull is to confirm it.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.weather_warnings.level IS 'The OFFICIAL warning level 0..4 as published (or derived from the publication''s own severity word by the documented CAP mapping when no separate level is sent - level_source says which). Never rescaled, never softened: the number is a safety statement. The DWD''s own names are 1 Wetterwarnung, 2 Markante Wetterwarnung, 3 Unwetterwarnung, 4 Warnung vor extremem Unwetter; 0 is the Vorabinformation Unwetter and is not a Warnstufe at all.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.weather_warnings.level_source IS '''published'' - the payload carried the level; ''derived_from_severity'' - it carried only a CAP severity word and the documented mapping produced the number. Carried to the tool result, because a mapping has to be stated where it is read: a derived level shown as a published one is our claim wearing the DWD''s authority. Nullable only because a column added to a populated table has to be; every row this loader writes fills it, and a NULL is read as ''published''.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.weather_warnings.official_sha256 IS 'sha256 of description as it arrived. The verbatim gate: the mcp consumer re-hashes the column before emitting the text under the Quellenvermerk, and falls back to its own summary - with the changed-data line instead of the source note - when the two disagree.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.weather_warnings.warncell_id IS 'The publisher''s own warn-cell id (Gemeinde/Kreis), verbatim. Not normalised and not translated into an AGS of our own: the cell''s polygon travels with the warning and answers "is this place inside it" without a mapping table.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.worker_boots.booted_at IS 'When this process started (clock_timestamp: the statement''s moment, not the transaction''s). Ordering column for "the newest boot of each worker"; also the retention key.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.worker_boots.config IS 'The redacted effective configuration as a FLAT jsonb object (key -> string/number/boolean/null). Produced by the worker''s configuration report from an allow-list: value (technical knobs), presence (set/unset for credentials, plus an _EXISTS key for a certificate path), count (a _COUNT key for a list whose membership is sensitive), db (a connection string reduced to host:port/dbname). Every produced string is additionally scrubbed of 19-digit runs and IP literals, and the check constraint refuses the former outright.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.worker_boots.git_sha IS 'The commit the image was built from, when the image says so. NULL until the deploy pipeline sets it — an honest NULL, not a guess.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.worker_boots.hostname IS 'The container id / host name the process saw. Which of two containers wrote a row, when one is a leftover.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.worker_boots.node_version IS 'process.version of the runtime, because "it only happens on the new Node" is a real bug report.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.worker_boots.worker IS 'The process: ingest-mobilithek, ingest-autobahn, ingest-tankerkoenig, mcp, retention, status. Matches the compose service name minus the project prefix, which is what an operator types into docker logs.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN public.worker_boots.worker_version IS 'The version of the package that booted (@viafrei/ingest, @viafrei/mcp, …). With git_sha it answers "which code wrote the rows I am looking at" after a partial deploy.';
COMMENT ON CONSTRAINT gazetteer_places_no_odbl ON public.gazetteer_places IS 'The ODbL boundary, stated twice on purpose: no OpenStreetMap row may enter the CC BY / dl-de / open-data place gazetteer. Share-alike is a term of the database, so one ODbL row in this table would put every place answer under ODbL. OSM addresses live in osm_addresses and are read by a second statement, never by a join.';
COMMENT ON VIEW control.data_status IS 'The read model of the admission gate: per ledger key, the time of the data we serve (held_since), whether its refresh is paused and why (owner, hold, budget), about when it resumes, and whether it is stale. Readers go through the workers'' data-status read.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.bfrk_stops_live IS 'Step-free stop survey summaries, open rows only, for the stations card: never the run id, never the publisher''s note (the card speaks seven languages, the note one).';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.bw_detours_live IS 'The open rows of bw_detours (the newest published version) — what a tool may read.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.charging_feed_runs_live IS 'The feed ledger columns the charging liveness read takes, for every feed (the register picks the status feeds at read time): feed, start, finish, status, items read, the two HTTP validators, the run id, whether the cycle jumped and the payload protocol; never the error text, archive path, HTTP status or byte count.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.charging_points_live IS 'charging_points with retracted rows removed. Not filtered by its site''s deleted_at: the foreign key does not cascade and the geo read reaches points directly, so a point is its own entity.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.charging_point_status_live IS 'Charging point status ticks for the place cards: the EVSE id, both publisher clocks and our pull, the feed, the three status words, the protocol, the DATEX II marking and the cycle that wrote the row; never the raw record.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.charging_sites_live IS 'charging_sites with retracted rows and sites whose name says they are private (access_private) removed.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.city_traffic_now_live IS 'The city traffic state as the newest pull of each feed listed it. The reader judges freshness by measured_at; Hamburg lists only segments that are not flowing (city_traffic_pulls.listing).';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.city_traffic_pulls_live IS 'The newest pull of each city traffic-state feed, without bookkeeping.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.city_traffic_segments_live IS 'The segments every city traffic-state feed has listed: the area a feed measures, which decides where it may answer.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.connect_transit_delays_live IS 'VBN Connect GTFS-RT trip updates (CC BY 4.0) of the last six hours, the NEWEST row per (trip_id, stop_sequence) — the 0113 shape of transit_delays_live: the publisher''s absolute time where it sent one (event_at) and a trip-level row''s start_date. No history, no run id, no raw record. Read with Connect trip ids as a parameter.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.db_facilities_live IS 'db_facilities without the provenance bookkeeping and without `extras` — what a reader may see. The public digest role is granted THIS and not the table, so it cannot read source_run_id, created_at/updated_at or the unmodelled payload fields, and it holds nothing on db_facility_ticks.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.delfi_service_alerts_live IS 'DELFI service alerts (CC BY-SA 4.0, GTFS-RT Service Alerts and SIRI SX, one share-alike database) that are open and publishable: the headline, validity, the lines, stops and Kreise they name and the keys that tell a duplicate across the two feeds. No description, URL or raw record. Read by the digest consumer.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.delfi_siri_et_calls_live IS 'DELFI SIRI ET calls (CC BY-SA, its own share-alike database) of the last eight hours, the columns a departure line prints: line, category, destination, aimed and expected departure, delay, platform, recorded/cancelled flags, the delivery stamp. Change-only, so the newest row per (service_day, journey_ref, stop_ref, visit) is the current state. Read by the digest consumer; the table and its history stay ungranted.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.feed_checks IS 'One row per open registered feed: its cadence on this host, when it was last CHECKED (the finish of its newest run that brought or confirmed a document: ok, unchanged, or degraded with items read — never a run the request budget refused), and since when — and whether — its refresh is paused (paused_at, paused), from control.data_status. The facts a freshness judgement needs and nothing else of the run ledger.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.fuel_feed_health IS 'One row of raw numbers about the Tankerkönig feed: newest observation across active watch rows (fuel_price_latest_live, retracted rows excluded), 24 h run counts and gap, 1 h error burst, watch-list coverage. Thresholds and the verdict live in the reader.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.fuel_price_latest_live IS 'fuel_price_latest with retracted rows removed. Read by fuel_feed_health, so a retracted price cannot prop up the freshness number.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.fuel_station_board_live IS 'Fuel board for the public tile: one row per station with a current positive price — brand, name, street, house number, postcode, town, E5/E10/Diesel and the poll instant, the Land of the nearest BKG Gemeinde (fuel_stations.land, 0141) and the candidate BKG Gemeinden its town may name (fuel_stations.town_places, 0217, keys and names only). No station id, no coordinates, no distance: MTS-K consumer information, no redistribution (0069). The caller applies the time window.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.fuel_stations_live IS 'fuel_stations with retracted rows removed.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.fuel_station_watch_due IS 'Stations due for a price poll, most neglected first. Due-ness includes per-target backoff: after 3 consecutive errors the interval doubles per further error (the exponent clamped at 40 so a very long dead streak cannot overflow the arithmetic), capped at the longer of 3600 s and 4x the tier''s own interval, and never shorter than that interval. The one definition both the scheduler and /feeds read.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.gbfs_stations_live IS 'Open stations of systems whose licence the gate admitted at the last read — what a tool may read.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.gbfs_station_status_live IS 'GBFS station counts of the last 25 hours for the place cards: counts, flags, our pull and the station''s report time; never the run id or older history.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.gbfs_vehicles_live IS 'The current free-floating vehicles of admitted systems, without seq — a reader has no use for a position in a list.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.hh_cameras_live IS 'Hamburg traffic-camera LOCATIONS for the road card: the open cameras'' id, label, point and last sighting; never the note, the run id or a closed camera. Read only through check_road_status''s camera statement.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.hh_roadworks_live IS 'The open, released rows of hh_roadworks — what a tool may read. A site that is not released is never visible through it.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.hydrogen_stations_live IS 'Hydrogen stations the publisher still lists: what a tool or a card may read. Named columns (0126): no run id, no publisher bookkeeping.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.lez_zones_live IS 'The open rows of lez_zones — what get_driving_rules, describe_location and the place card read.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.lorry_city_live IS 'The open city lorry rows: networks, restrictions (a group_id where one restriction covers many sections) and dated zones — what check_road_status and the place card read.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.lorry_hb_live IS 'The open VMZ Bremen rows — what check_road_status and the place card read; published_text is shown verbatim.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.lorry_ivm_live IS 'The open ivm rows — what check_road_status and the place card read.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.lorry_long_network_live IS 'The open long-lorry network rows — what check_road_status and the place card read.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.lorry_reads_live IS 'When each lorry-route file was last read — the date every answer from lorry_hb_live states, and the asOf of every lorry answer.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.lorry_sevas_live IS 'The open SEVAS rows — what check_road_status and the place card read.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.los_lane_now_live IS 'The Autobahn GmbH LoS per lane in NRW (GeoNutzV), publishable rows of open lanes on a placed main-carriageway cross-section, with that cross-section''s road and point. The failover of los_now_live: read only for a site whose own reading is not fresh.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.los_now_live IS 'The Autobahn GmbH LoS in NRW (GeoNutzV) on the main carriageway of a motorway we placed the site on, with the newest state. The reader judges freshness by last_seen_at; a stale row is no reading.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.los_sites_live IS 'The open LoS sites without provenance bookkeeping, publishable rows only.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.motorway_points_live IS 'BASt TMC Location Code List (CC BY 4.0) points on German motorways, for the Autobahn card and the motorway pages: road, code, type, junction number, name, neighbours and point; never the version or the load time.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.motorway_register_live IS 'Every German motorway number the BASt LCL or the Autobahn GmbH road list names: the set of motorway pages on viafrei.de, one national rule, independent of whether anything is reported on the road now.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.motorway_summary_live IS 'The per-motorway summary for the Autobahn card, the motorway index and the motorway page header.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.mvv_bus_load_live IS 'MVV regional-bus occupancy profiles, open keys only, for get_departures and the stations card: never the run id.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.opendata_roadworks_live IS 'The open, answerable, traffic-relevant rows of opendata_roadworks — what a tool or a place card may read.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.osm_addresses_live IS 'Open OpenStreetMap addresses (ODbL 1.0, share-alike database; 0026) with only the columns the public site needs to suggest an address and name a street: street, house number, postcode, town, the folded search columns, point, extract date. Read by the digest role for the place sheet and the place label; nothing may join it to a table of another licence.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.osm_pois_live IS 'Open OpenStreetMap points of interest (ODbL 1.0, share-alike database; 0037) with only the columns a place card shows: name, category and value, street and house number, point, extract date. Read by the digest consumer for the nearby-places card; nothing may join it to a table of another licence.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.osm_speed_cameras_live IS 'OpenStreetMap fixed speed cameras still in the newest extract of their Land — what a tool or a card may read.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.parking_facilities_bysa_live IS 'P+R NRW (CC BY-SA, its own database) for the parking card: the open facilities and the columns find_parking''s statement reads; never the external id, the opening hours or the run id. Read only through find_parking''s statement.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.parking_facilities_live IS 'parking_facilities with retracted rows hidden. Every read goes through this view; closed_at is deliberately NOT filtered here, because "this rest area closed last month" is an answer and "we withdrew this row" is not.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.parking_facilities_odbl_live IS 'Trucklounge Ecopark lorry parks (ODbL 1.0, its own database) for the parking card: the open facilities and the columns find_parking''s statement reads; never the external id, the opening hours or the run id. Read only through find_parking''s statement.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.parking_facilities_sid_live IS 'SID-only lorry parks for the two gated readers only: the MCP consent block (after an accepted elicitation) and the digest''s post-click fragment.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.parking_forecast_profiles_live IS 'parking_forecast_profiles with retracted rows hidden. Every read goes through this view; superseded rows are NOT hidden here, the reader asks for superseded_at is null.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.parking_occupancy_live IS 'Live car-park readings for the place cards: the printed figures, both clocks, the feed and the DATEX II marking; never the raw record.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.parking_occupancy_odbl_live IS 'Trucklounge Ecopark occupancy ticks (ODbL 1.0, the same database) for the parking card: figures, both clocks, feed, register key and marking; never the raw record or the run id. Read only through find_parking''s statement.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.parking_occupancy_sid_live IS 'SID truck-parking readings for the two gated readers only: the MCP consent block (after an accepted elicitation) and the digest''s post-click fragment.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.parking_sites_live IS 'parking_sites with retracted rows removed. closed_at rows are STILL HERE: a site the provider stopped publishing is a fact we keep and answer with, a retracted row is one we withdrew.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.registered_sources IS 'Sources registered in the database rather than in code: the generic contract the status consumer reads for its feed and source lists, defined over ingestor.sources. The licence label and the cadence are the ones the ingestor published to feed_catalog.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.road_context_live IS 'The open, answerable rows of road_context_features — what a tool or a place card may read, by kind and distance.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.road_event_locations_live IS 'road_event_locations with retracted stretches removed — its own deleted_at AND its event''s, because the foreign key cascades: a stretch of a retracted event is retracted.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.road_events_current IS 'Road events the provider still publishes AND that are in force at now() AND that the publisher has not marked restricted: road_events_live minus withdrawn rows (closed_at) minus rows whose validity window or validityStatus excludes this instant minus rows whose DATEX II confidentiality is anything but noRestriction or absent. The hot path for "what is going on right now" and the relation the national digest reads. It is NOT deduplicated across publishers — four feeds describe the same network and each keeps its own row, its own id and its own licence; the overlap between them is reported separately and never merged away.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.road_events_live IS 'road_events with retracted rows removed (deleted_at is null). THE read path: every MCP query, status query and alarm reads a *_live view, never the base table.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.road_weather_forecast_live IS 'The latest DWD SWSMOS run, the values a card prints: the road-condition CLASS (METRo model forecast, never a measured state and never an official warning), the road-surface temperature, and (0123) the air temperature and the hour''s liquid and solid precipitation — every one a forecast, per station and valid hour, with the run time the forecast''s age is measured from. Read by the digest consumer.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.road_weather_stations_live IS 'The open, active DWD road-weather stations (catalogue swsKatalog.csv): id, name, segment type, the catalogue point and its position source — the position source of every road-weather line. Read by the digest consumer for the weather card.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.routes_live IS 'routes with retracted rows removed.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.service_alert_lands_live IS 'Per Land code: the newest last_seen_at and the count of publishable DELFI service alerts placed there by a stop or a route, open or closed. Counts and an instant only. The rule of check_transit_disruption''s alert coverage by Land without its look-back. Read by the status console.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.speed_enforcement_live IS 'Speed-enforcement locations the publisher still lists — what a tool or a card may read.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.station_accessibility_live IS 'The open OpenStation platform accessibility rows, without extras, run ids or raw heights — what check_station_facilities and the stations card read.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.station_equipment_live IS 'The open inventory without provenance bookkeeping or extras — what a reader may see. Since 0253 a KVB unit''s published point (unit_lat, unit_lon), read to choose the platform it serves and never shown.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.station_metro_osm_decided IS 'OpenStreetMap-derived (ODbL 1.0, inside the station_metro_osm boundary): the zHV tram levels whose station page exists only because of the OSM match. The digest asks it by bound DHIDs to show the OpenStreetMap notice; never joined to another table.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.stops_live IS 'stops with retracted rows removed.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.stop_times_live IS 'stop_times with retracted rows removed.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.street_bays_live IS 'The open street bays and the newest state of each where a sensor reports. The reader counts a state only while its message_at is inside the freshness window of the request''s clock; a sensor-less row (spaces not null) never has one.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.traffic_count_now_live IS 'The open counting sites and the newest count of each measure. A count is a volume in an interval, never a statement that traffic flows or stands.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.traffic_count_profiles_live IS 'The newest month of every counted-traffic profile feed, per open site. A month''s typical hourly count, never a statement about the present.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.transit_delays_live IS 'DELFI GTFS-RT Trip Updates (CC BY-SA, unversioned) of the last six hours, the NEWEST row per (trip_id, stop_sequence) — the current state of a change-only table — with the publisher''s absolute time where it sent one (event_at) and a trip-level row''s start_date. No history, no run id, no raw record. Read with the trip ids as a parameter; never joined to another licence''s relation in one statement.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.transit_punctuality_area_live IS 'Regional punctuality nationwide (one row, trips de-duplicated across Länder), per Land (all sixteen, zeros included), per kreisfreie Stadt (BKG Gemeinde AGS = Kreis||000, level city) and per other Kreis with a report (BKG district name, level kreis, 0122), last 30 min of DELFI Trip Updates: trips observed/late (> 300 s at least once), trips with a SKIPPED stop, trips left out because the timetable gives every stop one time (their reported delay is their travel time), median of each counted trip''s largest delay, distinct stops, newest observation. Land and Kreis from the DHID AGS, names from the BKG gazetteer; derived per read, stored nowhere (0069, 0122, 0124).';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.transit_station_live IS 'Eight main stations (DHID and every static stop id below it), last 30 min of DELFI Trip Updates: trips observed/late (> 300 s at least once), trips with a SKIPPED stop, trips left out because the timetable gives every stop one time, median of each counted trip''s largest delay, newest observation. Aggregates only, no trip leaves the view; derived per read, stored nowhere.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.trips_live IS 'trips with retracted rows removed.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.vbb_transit_delays_live IS 'VBB GTFS-RT change-only rows of the last three hours for the place cards: pull, header time, trip, stop (DHID), delay, relationship, register key; never raw, never the run id. Read only through check_transit_disruption''s aggregate statement.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.vbn_transit_delays IS 'VBN SIRI ET calls (CC BY 4.0) in the trip-update shape check_transit_disruption reads: trip = dated journey, stop = StopPointRef, a cancelled call SKIPPED. Read only through the tool''s aggregate statement, and only when DELFI is silent or stale for the region.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.vbn_transit_delays_live IS 'VBN SIRI ET calls of the last three hours in the trip-update shape, for the place cards: pull, header time, journey, stop (DHID), delay, relationship, register key; never the run id. Read only through check_transit_disruption''s aggregate statement, only when DELFI is silent or stale.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.vrn_transit_delays_live IS 'VRN/RNN GTFS-RT change-only rows of the last three hours for the place cards: pull, header time, trip, stop (DHID), delay, relationship, register key; never raw, never the run id. Read only through check_transit_disruption''s aggregate statement.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.weather_warnings_area_live IS 'DWD warnings for a point: event, level, the official headline, validity, the cell polygon, the register key, (0123) the DWD''s description, and (0202) the official instruction and the DWD''s own English headline, description and instruction when it published them, with the hash taken over them at ingest — matched since 0204 by weather_warning_english (sent, onset, expiry, cell, eventCode II), never the raw record or the run ids. Read by the digest consumer for the place cards.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.weather_warnings_en_live IS 'The English text of every DWD alert in the current snapshot: read by check_weather_warnings and the digest''s place cards when the reader''s language is English, through weather_warning_english (0204). Since 0204 it carries the alert''s warn cells and its eventCode II, the two halves of the match key that are not timestamps. The raw record and the run ids stay out.';
COMMENT ON VIEW public.weather_warnings_live IS 'weather_warnings with retracted rows hidden. Every read goes through this view; closed_at is deliberately NOT filtered here, because "this warning was in force until 22:00" is an answer and "we withdrew this row" is not.';
